bout the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"3c10705a-ff0a-417e-8794-1e0cdb4d2b47"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a massive game implementation task.\n\nI will read the remainder of PLAN.md to understand testing requirements, done criteria, and what can be built on this computer. Then I will convert the objective into a structured plan with acceptance criteria.\n\nTASK.md specifies a Far Cry 2-inspired game built as a single HTML file and dev subfolder. The engine is pixel-engine from riftlight. Multiplayer will use the AI Hub. Required deliverables include exit-interview.json, an About/Credits screen, and PROGRESS.md.\n\nThe plan already extensively specifies Far Cry 2 mechanics. Researching the original game is required before building.\n\nThe objective itself does not name Far Cry 2 directly. It names STAGE.md and TASK.md, which contain the source specification. I will inspect those files first.\n\nThe deliverable is an original game inspired by Far Cry 2, not a recreation. PLAN.md explicitly states this and defines the core mechanics. I will briefly research the original game to verify these mechanics align with the plan.\n\nEngine kit availability and local build constraints need verification. The acceptance criteria must be small, outcome-based, and avoid prescribing architecture. PLAN.md is extremely specific about module layout and function signatures, so the plan should focus on deliverable outcomes rather than implementation details.\n\nThe acceptance criteria will be grouped into five outcome-based buckets: the single HTML deliverable, the core Far Cry 2-inspired gameplay loop, dev and multiplayer modes, the exit interview and about screen, and the RNG title generator. Each bucket must stay small while preserving every defining mechanic.","signature":"tco_0b5deb38-4fc5-905c-9a4f-2d8bd05ea541_call-e5ccb55e-45e0-4afb-86c9-2e0622bd62dd-5_"},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_0b5deb38-4fc5-905c-9a4f-2d8bd05ea541_call-e5ccb55e-45e0-4afb-86c9-2e0622bd62dd-5","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Far Cry 2 defining gameplay mechanics malaria weapon jamming fire buddy system map diamonds"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_0b5deb38-4fc5-905c-9a4f-2d8bd05ea541_call-e5ccb55e-45e0-4afb-86c9-2e0622bd62dd-5","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://screenrant.com/far-cry-2-game-malaria-most-irritating-mechanics/","title":"https://screenrant.com/far-cry-2-game-malaria-most-irritating-mechanics/"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://farcry2.zsg.dk/GameMechanics.php","title":"https://farcry2.zsg.dk/GameMechanics.php"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HpeweiqzPwc","title":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HpeweiqzPwc"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://farcry.fandom.com/wiki/Far_Cry_2","title":"https://farcry.fandom.com/wiki/Far_Cry_2"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://archive.org/stream/Far_Cry_2_Official_Game_Guide/Far_Cry_2_Official_Game_Guide_djvu.txt","title":"https://archive.org/stream/Far_Cry_2_Official_Game_Guide/Far_Cry_2_Official_Game_Guide_djvu.txt"}]},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e5ccb55e-45e0-4afb-86c9-2e0622bd62dd-4","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":990,"limit":150}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":29933,"output_tokens":1109,"cache_read_input_tokens":15104,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"8b723d8d-71bd-43e4-9401-cdb6cf57ba90"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-e5ccb55e-45e0-4afb-86c9-2e0622bd62dd-4","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"990→\\nTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\\n\\n**Suites:**\\n\\n1. **`static` (Node):** in `src/`: no `Math.random`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`. Every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\\n\\n2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\\n   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\\n   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\\n1000→   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\\n   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\\n   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\\n   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\\n   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\\n   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\\n   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\\n   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\\n   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\\n   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\\n1010→\\n3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\\n\\n4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\\n\\n5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\\n   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\\n   Check:\\n   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\\n   - `AIHub.active` false\\n1020→   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\\n   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\\n   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\\n   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\\n\\n6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** copy the built file to `tests/.site/game.html` beside a copy of `vendor/hub/*` (the harness only loads same-origin pages), serve that folder on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=game.html&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\\n   - `players=2&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\\n   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\\n1030→   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\\n\\n7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\\n\\n**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief; runs have no display, so everything here is headless): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1040→## 25. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\\n\\n| Step | Do | Done when |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\\n| 1 Walk | Terrain, roads, river, sky, wind, grass, trees. Player, `installLook`, actions, pad codes, device tracking, HUD basics, pause | You can walk the Kettle by pad or mouse |\\n| 2 Shoot | Hands rig and `tractor` first, soldier rig, one AI squad, damage, health, syrette, wear, jam, reload. SFX for each | A fight at P2 is fun on its own. Add the other guns |\\n| 3 Burn | Fire grid, flare, bottle, flamethrower, scars, regrowth, soldier panic, day and night. Fire sounds | Lighting a field at night makes you stop and watch |\\n1050→| 4 Drive | Vehicles, driving view, damage, repair, patrols, posts, train | Run a checkpoint in a pickup and break down after |\\n| 5 World | All sites, safehouses, Tibor, stones, tracker, fever, tablets, surgery, monocular, held map, rail halts, trig points, save and load | A whole session without missions works |\\n| 6 Story | Mission runner, dialogue, voices, buddies, rescues, M0 to End, Ledger, endings. Playthrough suite | A bot and you both finish it |\\n| 7 Shell | Title, all menus, settings, glyph sets, About, touch gate, attract, dev mode | Every screen works by pad, keyboard and mouse |\\n| 8 Multiplayer | Sim/view puppets, host, client, sessions, Cash Run, card, solo bots, link | Hub and link suites pass with a real round |\\n| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\\n| 10 Ship | Final build, About pages, exit interview, chat reply, `node tests/run.mjs --all` | Section 27 is all true |\\n\\n---\\n\\n1060→## 26. Cut order\\n\\nIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\\n\\n1. Fauna (vultures first).\\n2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\\n3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\\n4. Chase camera; dust weather.\\n5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\\n6. M5 (Mags offers her alternate plan in M6 instead: get the field workers out first, for less pay).\\n1070→7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\\n8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\\n9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\\n\\nNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 27. Done, reports, coverage\\n\\n1080→**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\\n- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\\n- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\\n- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\\n- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\\n- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\\n- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the copy in the kit's zip (keep the zip in `vendor/` and compare in the `static` suite).\\n1090→\\n**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\\n- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\\n- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\".\\n\\n**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\\n\\n**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\\n\\n1100→**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\\n\\n| Brief item | Section |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\\n| Genre, perspective | 1 |\\n| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\\n| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\\n| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\\n| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\\n1110→| Art direction: native base, new assets, library for generic props | 20 |\\n| Animation: native base, new animations | 21 |\\n| Audio in code, Animalese voices, music 10% | 22 |\\n| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 19 |\\n| Dev subfolder, what not to read, shared ports and processes | 4, 24 |\\n| Performance out of scope | 3 |\\n| Testing: headless, scripted playthrough, one command, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | 24 |\\n| Effort | Yours to spend; 25 and 26 order it |\\n| This computer and its stand-ins | 3, 4, 24 |\\n| Feature-complete vertical slice, infrastructure for later build-out | 4, 14, 25 |\\n1120→| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | 17 |\\n| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits | 18 |\\n| Narrative tone | 15 |\\n| 10-foot controller first, native keyboard and mouse, hover focus, glyphs, Xbox option | 16 |\\n| No dense web-style screens | 16 |\\n| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | 16, 18 |\\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | top, 26, 27 |\\n| Source Information, feedback sections, chat reply, About screen | 27 |\\n| Exit interview | 27 |\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"990→\\nTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\\n\\n**Suites:**\\n\\n1. **`static` (Node):** in `src/`: no `Math.random`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`. Every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\\n\\n2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\\n   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\\n   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\\n1000→   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\\n   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\\n   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\\n   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\\n   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\\n   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\\n   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\\n   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\\n   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\\n   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\\n1010→\\n3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\\n\\n4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\\n\\n5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\\n   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\\n   Check:\\n   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\\n   - `AIHub.active` false\\n1020→   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\\n   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\\n   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\\n   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\\n\\n6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** copy the built file to `tests/.site/game.html` beside a copy of `vendor/hub/*` (the harness only loads same-origin pages), serve that folder on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=game.html&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\\n   - `players=2&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\\n   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\\n1030→   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\\n\\n7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\\n\\n**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief; runs have no display, so everything here is headless): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1040→## 25. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\\n\\n| Step | Do | Done when |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\\n| 1 Walk | Terrain, roads, river, sky, wind, grass, trees. Player, `installLook`, actions, pad codes, device tracking, HUD basics, pause | You can walk the Kettle by pad or mouse |\\n| 2 Shoot | Hands rig and `tractor` first, soldier rig, one AI squad, damage, health, syrette, wear, jam, reload. SFX for each | A fight at P2 is fun on its own. Add the other guns |\\n| 3 Burn | Fire grid, flare, bottle, flamethrower, scars, regrowth, soldier panic, day and night. Fire sounds | Lighting a field at night makes you stop and watch |\\n1050→| 4 Drive | Vehicles, driving view, damage, repair, patrols, posts, train | Run a checkpoint in a pickup and break down after |\\n| 5 World | All sites, safehouses, Tibor, stones, tracker, fever, tablets, surgery, monocular, held map, rail halts, trig points, save and load | A whole session without missions works |\\n| 6 Story | Mission runner, dialogue, voices, buddies, rescues, M0 to End, Ledger, endings. Playthrough suite | A bot and you both finish it |\\n| 7 Shell | Title, all menus, settings, glyph sets, About, touch gate, attract, dev mode | Every screen works by pad, keyboard and mouse |\\n| 8 Multiplayer | Sim/view puppets, host, client, sessions, Cash Run, card, solo bots, link | Hub and link suites pass with a real round |\\n| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\\n| 10 Ship | Final build, About pages, exit interview, chat reply, `node tests/run.mjs --all` | Section 27 is all true |\\n\\n---\\n\\n1060→## 26. Cut order\\n\\nIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\\n\\n1. Fauna (vultures first).\\n2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\\n3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\\n4. Chase camera; dust weather.\\n5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\\n6. M5 (Mags offers her alternate plan in M6 instead: get the field workers out first, for less pay).\\n1070→7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\\n8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\\n9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\\n\\nNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 27. Done, reports, coverage\\n\\n1080→**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\\n- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\\n- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\\n- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\\n- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\\n- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\\n- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the copy in the kit's zip (keep the zip in `vendor/` and compare in the `static` suite).\\n1090→\\n**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\\n- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\\n- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\".\\n\\n**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\\n\\n**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\\n\\n1100→**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\\n\\n| Brief item | Section |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\\n| Genre, perspective | 1 |\\n| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\\n| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\\n| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\\n| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\\n1110→| Art direction: native base, new assets, library for generic props | 20 |\\n| Animation: native base, new animations | 21 |\\n| Audio in code, Animalese voices, music 10% | 22 |\\n| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 19 |\\n| Dev subfolder, what not to read, shared ports and processes | 4, 24 |\\n| Performance out of scope | 3 |\\n| Testing: headless, scripted playthrough, one command, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | 24 |\\n| Effort | Yours to spend; 25 and 26 order it |\\n| This computer and its stand-ins | 3, 4, 24 |\\n| Feature-complete vertical slice, infrastructure for later build-out | 4, 14, 25 |\\n1120→| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | 17 |\\n| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits | 18 |\\n| Narrative tone | 15 |\\n| 10-foot controller first, native keyboard and mouse, hover focus, glyphs, Xbox option | 16 |\\n| No dense web-style screens | 16 |\\n| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | 16, 18 |\\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | top, 26, 27 |\\n| Source Information, feedback sections, chat reply, About screen | 27 |\\n| Exit interview | 27 |\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":990,\"limit\":150,\"raw_output\":\"\\nTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\\n\\n**Suites:**\\n\\n1. **`static` (Node):** in `src/`: no `Math.random`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`. Every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\\n\\n2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\\n   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\\n   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\\n   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\\n   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\\n   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\\n   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\\n   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\\n   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\\n   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\\n   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\\n   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\\n   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\\n\\n3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\\n\\n4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\\n\\n5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\\n   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\\n   Check:\\n   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\\n   - `AIHub.active` false\\n   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\\n   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\\n   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\\n   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\\n\\n6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** copy the built file to `tests/.site/game.html` beside a copy of `vendor/hub/*` (the harness only loads same-origin pages), serve that folder on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=game.html&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\\n   - `players=2&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\\n   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\\n   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\\n\\n7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\\n\\n**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief; runs have no display, so everything here is headless): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 25. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\\n\\n| Step | Do | Done when |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\\n| 1 Walk | Terrain, roads, river, sky, wind, grass, trees. Player, `installLook`, actions, pad codes, device tracking, HUD basics, pause | You can walk the Kettle by pad or mouse |\\n| 2 Shoot | Hands rig and `tractor` first, soldier rig, one AI squad, damage, health, syrette, wear, jam, reload. SFX for each | A fight at P2 is fun on its own. Add the other guns |\\n| 3 Burn | Fire grid, flare, bottle, flamethrower, scars, regrowth, soldier panic, day and night. Fire sounds | Lighting a field at night makes you stop and watch |\\n| 4 Drive | Vehicles, driving view, damage, repair, patrols, posts, train | Run a checkpoint in a pickup and break down after |\\n| 5 World | All sites, safehouses, Tibor, stones, tracker, fever, tablets, surgery, monocular, held map, rail halts, trig points, save and load | A whole session without missions works |\\n| 6 Story | Mission runner, dialogue, voices, buddies, rescues, M0 to End, Ledger, endings. Playthrough suite | A bot and you both finish it |\\n| 7 Shell | Title, all menus, settings, glyph sets, About, touch gate, attract, dev mode | Every screen works by pad, keyboard and mouse |\\n| 8 Multiplayer | Sim/view puppets, host, client, sessions, Cash Run, card, solo bots, link | Hub and link suites pass with a real round |\\n| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\\n| 10 Ship | Final build, About pages, exit interview, chat reply, `node tests/run.mjs --all` | Section 27 is all true |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 26. Cut order\\n\\nIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\\n\\n1. Fauna (vultures first).\\n2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\\n3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\\n4. Chase camera; dust weather.\\n5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\\n6. M5 (Mags offers her alternate plan in M6 instead: get the field workers out first, for less pay).\\n7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\\n8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\\n9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\\n\\nNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 27. Done, reports, coverage\\n\\n**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\\n- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\\n- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\\n- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\\n- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\\n- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\\n- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the copy in the kit's zip (keep the zip in `vendor/` and compare in the `static` suite).\\n\\n**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\\n- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\\n- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\".\\n\\n**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\\n\\n**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\\n\\n**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\\n\\n| Brief item | Section |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\\n| Genre, perspective | 1 |\\n| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\\n| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\\n| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\\n| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\\n| Art direction: native base, new assets, library for generic props | 20 |\\n| Animation: native base, new animations | 21 |\\n| Audio in code, Animalese voices, music 10% | 22 |\\n| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 19 |\\n| Dev subfolder, what not to read, shared ports and processes | 4, 24 |\\n| Performance out of scope | 3 |\\n| Testing: headless, scripted playthrough, one command, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | 24 |\\n| Effort | Yours to spend; 25 and 26 order it |\\n| This computer and its stand-ins | 3, 4, 24 |\\n| Feature-complete vertical slice, infrastructure for later build-out | 4, 14, 25 |\\n| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | 17 |\\n| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits | 18 |\\n| Narrative tone | 15 |\\n| 10-foot controller first, native keyboard and mouse, hover focus, glyphs, Xbox option | 16 |\\n| No dense web-style screens | 16 |\\n| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | 16, 18 |\\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | top, 26, 27 |\\n| Source Information, feedback sections, chat reply, About screen | 27 |\\n| Exit interview | 27 |\\n\",\"total_lines\":1128}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"1f4cd8dc-e534-469f-ba46-780219d7c331"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_3","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The objective is to build a complete Far Cry 2-inspired FPS. PLAN.md, TASK.md, and STAGE.md define the deliverables.\n\nThe plan must describe outcomes rather than architecture. It should contain 3–5 atomic acceptance criteria grouped by related mechanics, with no file layout prescribed. Visual and interactive checks include unit tests for logic and a browser launch check. The entry point must also be verified. Criteria must reflect PLAN.md section 27 and TASK.md hand-in items exactly, without inventing scope or omitting core mechanics.\n\nThe never-cut mechanics from PLAN.md section 26 include fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue with the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, and voices. TASK.md adds a title generated by the 50-title RNG procedure.\n\nThe deliverable must be a single HTML file named `[GameTitle]-Far-Cry-2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917.html`. The brackets are placeholders for the actual game title. The filename format uses SOURCE as \"Far Cry 2\". Spaces in the title may cause issues.\n\nThe deliverable must also include a dev subfolder, an exit-interview.json file, a PROGRESS.md file, and an About/Credits section with source info and feedback. The pixel-engine must remain unmodified. A tests command must be available.\n\nThe first criterion groups all packaging and title requirements into a single atomic hand-in outcome. It specifies the RNG-chosen title constant on the title screen and document title, the self-contained HTML file at the TASK-specified name booting from file:// on the pixel engine, the dev subfolder, the exit-interview.json shape, PROGRESS.md, and the About/Credits source fields with feedback question sets.\n\nThe plan should contain five atomic criteria, not one giant packaging check. Gameplay mechanics will be grouped separately from deliverable hand-in items.\n\nThe first criterion is now fully drafted. It requires the 50-name RNG title to be shown, a single HTML file named per TASK.md in the work-folder root that runs from file:// with the shipped pixel engine unmodified, exit-interview.json and in-game About/Credits carrying required source and feedback fields, PROGRESS.md tracking work, and no TODOs, placeholders, or stubs. The second criterion is beginning to take shape.\n\nThe second criterion covers world, combat, body, and fire mechanics. It requires an open 1280 m Kettle environment with walkable and drivable terrain. Weapons must show wear and jam; reloading clears the jam. Fire spreads via a grid with wind affecting head speed between 2 and 5 m/s at 5 m/s wind, and burnt land cannot re-burn. Fever is cleared by tablet and ignores collapses and stone costs. Syrette and field surgery are available. Health and down states are implemented.\n\nThe third criterion covers hands-not-menus, story, and buddies. It requires the held paper map and GPS to remain active while the world continues, including during driving. The full story must run through stones, safehouses, and missions M0–End with both endings and Harrow’s twist, where scars form a firebreak and the player chooses who gets the sheet. Buddy rescue and the finish-it choice are included. The Ledger is present. Writing voice and verbatim lines are accurate. Music defaults to 10%. Animalese voices and all required SFX render non-silently.\n\nThe fourth criterion covers the controller-first UX shell. It requires a 10-foot HUD and menus with PS glyphs, an Xbox setting, native mouse hover and click, a touch-only gate requiring controller or keyboard plus Settings, attract mode starting after 10 seconds of idle input, and dev mode from pause with live balance sliders and reset to defaults.\n\nThe fifth criterion covers multiplayer and verification. It requires shared-world Cash Run 1–4 via the AI Hub protocol with four harness scenarios passing, solo hired-guns, the game running outside a session, and `node tests/run.mjs --all` reporting zero failures including both endings.\n\nThe five criteria now fully cover the never-cut mechanics and TASK.md hand-ins.\n\nA verification plan is being drafted for implementers and adversarial verifiers. It will use Playwright to probe the browser, serve and load the HTML, assert zero page errors, confirm the render surface is substantially filled, verify driven input produces visible change, and capture a screenshot. If the launcher cannot run, that failure must be documented.\n\nThis plan must align with the existing detailed test suite in PLAN.md without becoming unfinishable.\n\nThe verification plan will treat `node tests/run.mjs --all` reporting zero failures as the user’s gating bar. All four hub scenarios must pass locally via the vendor harness. Entry-point launch is confirmed through Playwright loading the HTML on file://.\n\nA conflict exists between TASK.md and PLAN.md regarding WebGPU canvas screenshots. TASK says a blank headless screenshot is acceptable and the game state should be checked instead. PLAN.md section 3 claims screenshots produced correct frames with specific flags. The plan writer must resolve this discrepancy.\n\nThe verification plan will prioritize game state checks over screenshot readbacks. TASK.md outranks PLAN.md on this point, so a blank canvas is acceptable if the internal game state is correct. The brief's explicit wording always wins over the plan.\n\nThe browser launch step will load the built HTML from file:// with WebGPU flags, assert zero page errors and a valid WebGPU backend, verify the game state shows the title screen and non-error boot, run the check multiple times for consistency, and capture a screenshot as evidence. If Playwright cannot start, the failure is recorded to {SCRATCH} and the harness falls back to headless Engine tests.\n\nThe verification plan must assert that the primary observable is correct, not merely present and non-empty. For the title screen, this means confirming the chosen title and mode title appear in the game state.\n\nThe repository does not yet exist, so no existing test convention applies beyond PLAN.md. The plan should therefore require adding `node tests/run.mjs` suites as PLAN section 24 describes, with zero failures reported by that command serving as the gating bar.\n\nAcceptance criteria will avoid prescribing exact file names; the implementation approach may still suggest structure.\n\nThe instruction forbids prescribing module or file layout. The implementation approach may reference PLAN.md sections as guidance, but copying the full file tree into it would make verifiers treat that structure as a contract.\n\nThe implementation approach will keep architecture guidance without making it an acceptance criterion. The task checklist is being compressed from PLAN section 25’s eleven steps down to eight ordered, completable checkboxes. The harness advances by checking the first unchecked box, so the first step must be the actual first act: pick the title with the RNG, create the dev folder, and unzip the kit.\n\nThe eight-step task checklist is now fully enumerated. It covers title selection and engine scaffolding, terrain and movement, weapons and health, fire and weather, vehicles and safehouses, missions and dialogue, and finally the UX shell.\n\nThe eight-step checklist is finalized. It includes Cash Run multiplayer, link or cut per section 26, `tests --all`, screenshots, `exit-interview.json`, and `PROGRESS.md`.\n\nNon-goals are explicitly defined. FPS optimization is out of scope. Real iOS Safari, physical controllers, real second humans, and audio listening are excluded. Byte-level recreation of Far Cry 2 proper nouns, map, and plot is also forbidden.\n\nAdditional non-goals are defined. Child soldiers, sexual violence, and targeting civilians are forbidden. Editing pixel-engine.js is prohibited. DOM UI and on-screen touch controls are excluded. Audio files are not required. CDN-hosted runtime dependencies are allowed because TASK.md outranks PLAN.md, though PLAN.md prefers a self-contained file.\n\nThe criterion will require a single HTML deliverable that runs from file://, with the engine remaining as shipped. TASK.md allows omitting downloadable dependencies from the HTML when easier, so inlining the bundle is the intended path. The work folder root will contain only the HTML, exit-interview.json, PROGRESS.md, and the PLAN, STAGE, and TASK documents.\n\nThe dev subfolder will be named with the chosen title and a timestamp, holding the kit, source, and downloaded assets including pixel-engine.zip, the ai-hub vendor/session.js, and harness. The build requires esbuild, playwright, and node.\n\nScope is enormous for one implementer. Section 26’s cut order is the sanctioned way to finish without stubs. If time runs out, cuts from that section are allowed and must remove all traces. The acceptance criteria should not require every mission, link, and card, or the goal becomes unfinishable.\n\nThe acceptance criteria will require the never-cut features and a complete vertical slice. Missions that remain must be fully implemented, with both endings reachable. This avoids making section 26’s cut order an acceptance criterion.\n\nCut-order items are in scope by default. If omitted, they must be fully absent with no leftover menu entries, map marks, or stubs. The playthrough must still reach both endings. The hub is never-cut. Link is cut #2. Card is cut #3.\n\nIf shared-world cannot be stable, the build may fall back to ghost mode. PLAN.md permits this fallback. The four hub scenarios must still pass in whatever mode ships, so meta tags must match the chosen mode.\n\nThe harness will run headless via xvfb with swiftshader flags. Software rendering caps performance near one frame per second, so tests must use e.step timing rather than wall-clock checks. Earlyoom may kill large browsers, and pkill is forbidden.\n\nThe plan must not dictate a specific title; the implementer selects it. The deliverable filename and dev subfolder name will follow the pattern `{chosen title}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`, using literal spaces rather than TASK.md’s bracketed placeholders.\n\nThe deliverable HTML file will sit directly in the work folder root, not inside the dev subfolder. The dev folder and its stem follow the exact template `<GameTitle>-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`, with the chosen title replacing `GameTitle`. The implementer still needs to resolve where `PROGRESS.md` should live.\n\nPROGRESS.md will reside in the work folder root alongside STAGE.md so the implementer can resume from it. The deliverable is PLAN.md. The plan writer already completed Far Cry 2 mechanics research and engine analysis in PLAN section 3.\n\nThe implementer records research in the exit interview. Verification gating requires two checks: a captured RNG script output in {SCRATCH} showing fifty titles and the selected index, with GAME_TITLE matching the document title and title-screen state; and `node tests/run.mjs --all` from the dev folder printing zero failures, captured to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log`.\n\nThe test suite covers fire speed, jam formula, fever, map-while-driving, buddy-down choice, both mission endings, slider resets, attract mode at ten seconds, music volume, round rules, and static checks. These include no Math.random calls, no TODO stubs, valid font charset, non-silent models and sounds, and an engine zip that is byte-identical to the shipped version.\n\nThe HTML deliverable must be launched twice from file:// with Playwright using the flags from PLAN §24. Each run must show no page errors, the chosen title on the title screen, a touch notice on phone profiles, and AIHub.active set to false outside a session. Both runs are captured to {SCRATCH}.\n\nIf Playwright cannot start, the headless suite is accepted as the bar and its failure is captured.\n\nThe hub harness is gating, not a fallback. It serves tests/.site and runs four scenarios; each must show `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` and dataset.complete. If the environment cannot run it, that blocks completion.\n\nEvidence includes screenshots from PLAN §24 in {SCRATCH}, plus exit-interview.json parsing and About text present in the built page or content module.\n\nThe phone profile must display the exact notice \"Controller or keyboard required\" and include Settings. Attract mode must start.\n\nThe verifier is not required to play the full game; the automated test suite serves as the stand-in.\n\nTests may assert against expected ranges drawn from PLAN, such as fire speed between 2 and 5 m/s, because those values are the specification rather than a reimplementation. The headless engine itself is the real path under test, so mocking it is prohibited.\n\nPROGRESS.md is a process requirement, not a pass/fail gate. It will be listed as a checklist step and a small criterion so the implementer does not forget to update it. The final acceptance criteria will total five items.\n\nThe plan will note in risks that the implementer should read the CHANGELOG if the downloaded engine kit is newer than version 0.46.0.\n\nThe plan file path is now fixed. The draft begins with the goal kind set to code-change and the first acceptance criterion: a title chosen by silently listing fifty playful riffs on \"Far Cry 2\" and selecting one with a real RNG.\n\nThe chosen title must appear verbatim on the title screen, in the HTML `<title>` tag, and in `exit-interview.json` under `aiHub.name`. The work folder root contains exactly three items: the HTML file, `exit-interview.json`, and `PROGRESS.md`. The dev files live in a subfolder named with the same stem. The built HTML runs from `file://` with no other runtime files beside it.\n\n`pixel-engine.js` in the dev folder must be byte-identical to the kit zip and must never be edited.\n\nThe in-game About/Credits screen must display every TASK.md field under Source Information, including Date, Source, Plan, genre, perspective, tech, art, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, and model. It must also list Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback as ordered brief answers, then credit Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, and the \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\" notice.\n\nNo TODO, FIXME, placeholder, stub, or \"coming soon\" remains. Every feature is fully built.\n\nThe never-cut survival-shooter systems run on a continuous 1280×1280 m basin. The player walks, sprints, crouches, jumps, and drives in first person. Guns wear down, jam after firing, and fall apart at zero condition; ground guns start worn and locker guns start clean. Fire spreads via a 5 m grid with wind measured head speed of 2–5 m/s at 5 m/s wind, flanks slower, and burnt cells cannot burn again. Scars appear on the held map.\n\nBlackwater fever is a tablet that clears an attack; ignoring it collapses the player and costs stones. Below 20 HP a heal runs a field-surgery scene; at 0 HP a living buddy can rescue, and a downed buddy offers syrette or finish-it (finish-it only if no syrette). A paper map and GPS are held in the hands while walking and driving and the world does not pause. Music volume is 0.1 on a clean boot; voices are code gibberish; required effects are code `SoundDef`s that render non-silent; no audio files. Story slice and original fiction are also required.\n\nThe campaign is an original country, cast, and plot with no Far Cry 2 proper nouns, characters, or map. Missions run from the opening train through Harrow's reveal that the player has been burning a firebreak, then a real choice of who receives the minefield sheet, then both ending cards. Completing the scar missions lays the arc even if the player burned something else. The Ledger reports hectares burned, people moved, men paid to kill, friends lost, and stones earned. Civilians cannot be killed by the player's bullets. A scripted bot can finish both endings.\n\nAny system omitted for time is a whole item from PLAN.md §26, taken from the top, with every menu entry, map mark, and line removed; the never-cut list in §26 is all present.\n\n4. 10-foot shell, attract, dev mode. All UI is HUD art pixels, not DOM. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox glyph setting, and keyboard/mouse glyphs; mouse hover moves menu focus and click activates; pad, keyboard, and mouse can operate menus. Pause sets `timeScale` to 0 and does not depend on a keydown that pointer lock may swallow.\n\nTouch-only detection shows the exact notice \"Controller or keyboard required\" and one Settings button, with no on-screen touch controls. Attract mode starts after 10 seconds on the title or main menu, loops without error, and displays a demo tag; any input returns to the screen it came from without writing a save. Pause opens Dev Mode, which exposes the balance sliders from PLAN §17; changing one applies immediately and Reset restores every default. Multiplayer and solo round are also required.\n\nCash Run is a shared-world round for up to four players. It lasts six minutes. Players carry cases to a truck. Friendly fire and bleed-and-revive mechanics are included. The round also runs solo with hired-gun AI. The game boots to the title screen outside any session.\n\nSession boot follows PLAN §19. It connects via `setInterval` headless server ticks instead of the render loop. `AIHub.ready()` fires without a key press. Card answers use Enter and M from a window keydown listener.\n\nThe four hub harness scenarios in PLAN §24 must pass.\n\nIf shared-world cannot be made stable, the fallback is ghost then parallel. Session meta tags must match the mode actually shipped. The four hub harness scenarios still pass. Same-computer link is in scope unless cut under PLAN §26 item 2.\n\nThe verification plan gates on `node tests/run.mjs --all` exiting zero with a summary line reporting zero failed tests. Output is captured to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`.\n\nThe test suites must exercise the shipped game directly via Engine.headless with real actions and step, not a reimplementation. They must cover every observation in criteria 2 through 5: fire head speed, burnt cells cannot reburn, jam blocks firing until reload clears it, guns fall apart at zero condition, the fever tablet versus collapse, both surgery and downed-buddy paths, raising the map while driving setting a waypoint, music volume of 0.1 from clean storage, sliders restoring defaults after set-all then reset, attract starting exactly at the ten-second boundary and exiting on input without saving, solo round bank drop revive and time-up finish, and a bot playthrough of every shipped scenario.\n\nThe verification plan now includes a static ban on `Math.random`, TODOs, stubs, and placeholders. Font-safe content strings are required. `renderSound` must be non-silent.\n\nA second gating step launches the real HTML from `file://` twice in Playwright Chromium at 1920×1080 with PLAN §24 WebGPU flags. Each run must show zero page or console errors. `AIHub.active` must be false. The title screen must display the RNG-picked title string. Both runs are captured to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `launch-2.log`. The two results must agree.\n\nIf Playwright fails to start, the error output is saved to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log` and the headless suite in step 1 becomes the accepted bar. A blank WebGPU canvas readback is not a pass by itself; state assertions remain the primary observable.\n\nA third gating step uses Playwright on an iPhone 14 Chromium profile. It verifies the exact notice text `Controller or keyboard required`, that the Settings button is present, that tapping it opens Settings, and that attract mode is allowed to start. The output is captured to `{SCRATCH}/touch.log`.\n\nA fourth gating step serves the built file from a local Python HTTP server on `127.0.0.1`. The hub page title must match the game. Each of the four hub scenarios runs in isolation. Completion is confirmed by `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Only that server process is killed. Output is captured to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. A two-page link round is gated only if it was not cut; otherwise the log must show its absence.\n\nScreenshots named in PLAN §24 are saved to `{SCRATCH}/shots/` after at least ten seconds with manual rendering off. `exit-interview.json` is parsed and its keys confirmed. A blank screenshot does not fail when the state check passed.\n\nThe hub harness is an internal local oracle, so gating remains valid.\n\nImplementation guidance keeps the code short and points to PLAN as design reference rather than contract. Simulation data is separated from views so headless tests need no GPU. Content is treated as data. Actions are modeled as objects instead of keycodes. `fixedUpdate` handles physics while `update` handles input. Tuning values read live from the engine. DOM access is guarded.\n\nThe implementation will use a single `createGame` entry point instead of `loadGame`. Build order follows §25 and cut order follows §26.\n\nThe task checklist has three phases. Phase one lists fifty titles, records the RNG pick, creates the dev subfolder, downloads the kit and AI Hub harness, proves engine bytes, scaffolds a booting `createGame`, and verifies the single-file build opens on `file://` with the four hub scenarios passing. It also writes `PROGRESS.md`.\n\nPhase two covers terrain, sky, wind, grass, player locomotion, look and pointer-lock fixes, action map, device glyphs, minimal HUD, and pause.\n\nPhase three covers weapons, wear, jam, break, hits, soldiers, health, syrette, surgery, fever, and tests for those systems.\n\nPhase four adds fire grid and weapons that ignite it, day/night cycles, vehicles and repair, held map, safehouses, stones, and save/load.\n\nPhase five covers missions through both endings, buddy rescue and finish-it, ledger, dialogue, music at ten percent, voices and sfx, and a playthrough suite.\n\nPhase six adds title, menus, settings, touch gate, attract mode, dev-mode sliders with reset, and About/Credits pages.\n\nPhase seven implements Cash Run host and client, solo bots, card listeners, and the hub suite. Link is included unless cut under §26.\n\nThe final checklist requires `node tests/run.mjs --all` to exit zero failed, plus launch captures, screenshots, `exit-interview.json`, and an updated `PROGRESS.md` noting any cuts.\n\nLeaving a stub for a cut feature is a failure even if the feature is removed under §26.\n\nBlank screenshots are not a failure when the game state is correct. The judge must rely on `e.step` and `__GAME__` state instead of frame rate.\n\nPointer-lock must handle Escape and reject `requestPointerLock` per PLAN §3 or the hub check fails.\n\nThe kit must be newer than 0.46.0; read the CHANGELOG after that version before trusting §3.\n\nOnly one browser should run at a time to avoid earlyoom.\n\nThe source filename contains spaces because the title is \"Far Cry 2\"; keep the exact stem so tools can find it.\n\nPLAN §23 forbids CDN and requires a single file served from `file://`. TASK.md permits CDN but its optional shortcut does not override PLAN §27, which the objective demands. The build must therefore be a standalone file with no external resources.\n\nI confirmed the core Far Cry 2 mechanics: malaria pills and pass-out, weapon jam and break, wind-driven savanna fire, diamond cases with tracker, buddies who rescue and can die, physical map/GPS, two factions, safehouses, and first-person on foot and in vehicles. PLAN.md already maps each to an original form, so I will not clone names.\n\nNon-goals include cloning Far Cry 2 names, map, Jackal plot, or real malaria branding. FPS optimization and art polish beyond what §20 requires to read as the game are out of scope. Physical DualSense, real iOS Safari, a human second player, and listening tests are also excluded.\n\nChild soldiers, sexual violence, and civilian kill targets are excluded. Engine editing and on-screen touch controls are out of scope. Multiplayer beyond four players, chat, emotes, and custom names are also excluded. Prone, ladders, and ledge grabs are forbidden by PLAN §20. Cuts must be taken top-down; partial or out-of-order cuts are failures.\n\nThe plan file is being written now. Each criterion will be atomic and independently checkable from its own start state, with no holistic end-to-end gates. Criterion 3 is decomposed into the playthrough suite and the bot finishing both endings.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"PLAN.md and TASK.md specify a full original Far Cry 2-style shooter plus exact hand-in files. I’ll turn that into a short outcome contract the implementer and verifiers can share."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-29d93fa8-b466-4679-8fd3-9af36c70018b-6","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c%2Fwork/01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df/goal/plan-01a1aadc-c88f-7f63-94c6-14c1229777fe.md","content":"# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person war-zone survival shooter and hand in every TASK.md artifact that this computer can produce.\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. Title and hand-in. Fifty playful riffs on \"Far Cry 2\" are listed and one is chosen with a real random-number generator (not by hand). That exact string is the on-screen title, the HTML `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The folder that contains `STAGE.md` holds (a) one HTML file whose filename stem is `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`, runnable from `file://` with nothing else required beside it, (b) `exit-interview.json` beside that HTML, one object in TASK.md's exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, \"unknown\" only where a fact cannot be confirmed, and (c) `PROGRESS.md`. All dev files live in a subfolder with that same stem. The shipped `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit zip and is never edited. About/Credits, reachable from the main menu and from pause, shows every TASK.md Source Information field (Date `2026-11-05 01:39:17`, Source `Far Cry 2`, Plan `PLAN.md`, genre, perspective, tech, art, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, Usage `added on import`), then Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback as the brief's questions with answers in that order, then credits for Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, and the words \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\". No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, or `coming soon`, and no half-built feature, remains.\n2. Never-cut survival systems. On one continuous 1280×1280 m basin the player moves and drives in first person (walk, run, sprint, crouch, jump, wade). Guns wear with use, jam so they cannot fire until repeated reloads clear them, and fall apart at condition 0; locker guns start clean and ground guns start worn. A 5 m fire grid spreads with wind (measured head speed 2–5 m/s when wind is 5 m/s, flanks slower); burnt ground cannot burn again; scars are visible on the map. Blackwater fever is cleared by a held tablet; ignoring it collapses the player and removes stones. Low health can run a field-surgery scene; at 0 HP a living buddy can rescue; a downed buddy offers a syrette or finish-it, and only finish-it when no syrette remains. The paper map and GPS are held in the hands while walking and while driving, and the world does not pause. With clean storage, music volume is 0.1. Voices are code gibberish. Required effects are code sounds that render non-silent. No audio files are used.\n3. Original story slice. Country, people, factions, weapons' names, and places are invented. None of Far Cry 2's proper nouns, characters, plot, or map appear. Shipped missions run from the opening ride through the reveal that the burns form a firebreak, then a real choice of who receives the minefield sheet, then both ending cards in PLAN.md §14. Story scars are laid even if the player burned something else. The Ledger reports hectares burned, people moved, men paid to kill, friends lost, and stones earned. The player's bullets cannot kill civilians. Each shipped mission can be started from its own setup and finished with real actions; both endings are reached that way, including once with a buddy already dead. Anything left out is a whole item from PLAN.md §26, taken from the top of that list, with its menu entries, map marks, and lines removed. The §26 never-cut list is all present.\n4. 10-foot shell, attract, and dev mode. UI is drawn with the engine HUD in art pixels, not DOM. Prompts follow the last used device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox glyph setting, and keyboard/mouse glyphs. Mouse hover moves menu focus and a click activates; pad, keyboard, and mouse all operate the same menus. Pause sets time scale to 0. A touch-only device shows the exact notice `Controller or keyboard required` and one Settings control, with no on-screen touch controls, and attract is still allowed to start. With no input, attract starts only after 10 seconds on the title or main menu (not on Settings or About), loops without error, shows a demo tag, and any input returns to the screen it came from without writing a save. Pause opens Dev Mode (hidden in a hub session) with the live balance sliders in PLAN.md §17; moving a slider changes that system immediately; Reset restores every default.\n5. Session round. Cash Run is a round of 1 to 4 players, about 6 minutes: carry stone cases to an extraction truck, friendly fire on, downed players can be revived or looted. The same round runs with no hub as solo play against hired-gun AI, and a normal boot with no session still reaches the title. Boot follows PLAN.md §19: hub connect, a headless server advanced on a timer rather than the render loop, ready signaled with no key press, and the card answering Enter and M from a window keydown listener. All four harness scenarios in PLAN.md §24 pass on the built file. If shared-world cannot be made stable, ship ghost, then parallel, only after that, with session meta tags matching the mode actually shipped, and the four scenarios still pass. Same-computer two-window link is required unless it is the §26 cut.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder run `node tests/run.mjs --all` and capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the summary line reports 0 failed. Suites must call the shipped game (headless engine, real actions, `step` from a fresh start or a mission setup), not a copy of the rules. Required observations: fire head speed in the 2–5 m/s band at 5 m/s wind and slower flanks; a burnt cell does not ignite again; a jam blocks fire and repeated reload clears it; condition 0 empties the slot; a tablet clears fever and ignoring it costs stones; surgery, rescue, and finish-it behave as in criterion 2; the map can be raised while driving and its cursor sets a waypoint; clean-storage music volume is 0.1; every balance slider changes its system and Reset restores every default; attract does not start before the 10-second boundary, does start at that boundary with no input, and exits on input without a save write; a solo round banks a case, drops it on down, revives, and finishes on time-up with every present player once in the standings; a bot finishes every shipped mission through both endings and the ledger/world assertions hold, including a run with one buddy dead; source has no `Math.random` and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub/coming soon; content strings stay inside the HUD font; every shipped sound renders non-silent; `pixel-engine.js` matches the kit zip byte for byte.\n2. gating: Launch the real built HTML from `file://` twice, in Chromium via Playwright at 1920×1080, with the WebGPU flags in PLAN.md §24. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Each run must show no page or console errors, `AIHub.active` false, and game state whose title string equals the RNG pick. The two runs must agree. If Playwright itself cannot start, write that failure to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; the headless suite in step 1 is then the accepted bar. A screenshot buffer that comes back blank is not proof of failure and not proof of success; the state assertion is the primary observable (this machine's software WebGPU canvas can be blank). Do not invent a stand-in page.\n3. gating: On Playwright's iPhone 14 profile under Chromium (drop the WebKit device default), open the same file and capture `{SCRATCH}/touch.log`. Pass only if the notice text is exactly `Controller or keyboard required`, a Settings control is shown, a tap opens Settings, and attract is not blocked. A WebKit iPhone load that fails for lack of WebGPU is evidence only; record it in `{SCRATCH}/webkit-iphone.log` and do not treat it as the gate.\n4. gating: Copy the built HTML beside the hub harness files, serve that directory with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the page title is the chosen game title, then run the four PLAN.md §24 scenarios one at a time. Pass only when each reaches `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`, including any failed `__hubCheck.checks`. Stop only that server, by its PID. If same-computer link was not cut, also drive two `file://` pages through one shared round to matching standings and a shared fire or case, logged in `{SCRATCH}/link.log`. If link was cut, that log must show the feature is absent (no menu entry).\n5. evidence: After the game has been rendering for at least 10 seconds, save the PLAN.md §24 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm the key shape. Screenshot absence or a blank WebGPU frame does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 hold.\n\n## Non-goals\n- A recreation of Far Cry 2: its proper nouns, characters, plot, map, logos, music, and look stay out. Defining mechanics are already rewritten in PLAN.md (fever, jam and break, wind-driven grass fire, handheld map, hired buddies who can die, stones and a tracker, two rotten factions, safehouses, first person on foot and in vehicles).\n- FPS targets, render optimization, and judging the game by wall-clock frame rate. Software WebGPU here is about 1 fps; tests advance with `step`.\n- Physical DualSense, a TV, a human listener, a second human, real iOS Safari, prone, ladders, ledge grabs, on-screen touch controls, audio files, chat, emotes, or custom player names.\n- Editing `pixel-engine.js`, or leaving a cut feature as a stub, menu entry, or map mark.\n- Civilian targets, child soldiers, and sexual violence.\n\n## Assumed scope\n- Work folder root (beside `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, `TASK.md`): the single HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`: unzipped riftlight pixel-engine kit (latest release zip), unmodified AI Hub `session.js` plus the hub harness used only for tests, game sources, art and animation data, generated audio, `node tests/run.mjs`, and the esbuild single-file packager.\n- Engine facts in PLAN.md §3 were verified on 0.46.0. Far Cry 2's defining mechanics were checked against public descriptions of malaria pills and pass-out, weapon jam and breakage, savanna fire driven by wind, diamond cases, buddies, and the in-hand map. PLAN.md already sets the original equivalents; do not research the game in order to copy it.\n- Runtime: Node, esbuild, Playwright 1.63 Chromium with the host platform override already set. Python Playwright is available. No `playwright install`. One browser at a time.\n\n## Implementation approach\nFollow PLAN.md §25 in order and §26 when cutting. Keep this as guidance, not a file-layout contract. Separate simulation data from meshes so headless tests never need a GPU. Put missions, weapons, sites, dialogue, tuning defaults, and model defs in plain data. Gameplay reads named actions, not raw keys. Simulate in the fixed step and draw in the frame update. Read tuning values at the moment of use. One game object owns the world; title, attract, campaign, and the round are modes, not a fresh terrain load. Guard every use of `window`, `document`, `navigator`, and `localStorage`. Randomness comes from the engine's seeded generator, forked by name. Build the single HTML the way PLAN.md §23 specifies (session script first, then hub meta tags, then the bundled module) so `file://` boots. Keep the four hub scenarios passing from the first bootable build. Log defects in the dev folder's issues note as tests catch them. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each step so a compacted context can resume.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] List 50 titles, pick one with a real RNG, record the list and the pick, create the dev subfolder, unzip the kit, vendor the hub client and harness, and prove `pixel-engine.js` matches the zip.\n- [ ] Scaffold the single-file build and a booting game so `file://` opens and all four hub scenarios pass. Start `PROGRESS.md`.\n- [ ] Add the basin, player locomotion, look and pointer-lock fixes, actions, device glyphs, minimal HUD, and pause. Cover them with headless tests.\n- [ ] Add weapons (wear, jam, break), hits, soldiers, health, syrette, surgery, and fever, with the system tests in criterion 2.\n- [ ] Add the fire grid, ignition weapons, day and night, vehicles and repair, the held map, safehouses, stones, and save/load.\n- [ ] Add missions through both endings, buddies (rescue and finish-it), the Ledger, dialogue, music at 0.1, and code voices and effects. Add the bot playthrough.\n- [ ] Add title, menus, settings, the touch gate, attract, dev-mode sliders with reset, and About/Credits.\n- [ ] Finish Cash Run (host, client, solo bots, card listeners). Re-run `node tests/run.mjs --all`, the two `file://` launches, the phone-profile check, and the four hub scenarios. Write `exit-interview.json` and finish `PROGRESS.md`, including any §26 cuts.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- PLAN.md §27 demands the full game and zero failing tests; §26 allows cutting whole features from the top of its list if time runs out. A cut that leaves a stub, menu entry, or map mark fails. Never-cut systems stay even if that forces a cut higher on the list.\n- TASK.md allows downloaded CDN dependencies \"if that is easier\". PLAN.md §23 and §27 require one HTML file that runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it. Ship the single file. Do not rely on a CDN.\n- The HTML stem contains spaces because SOURCE is `Far Cry 2`. Keep that stem exact.\n- There is no display. Headless Chromium with the §24 flags is the browser. A blank WebGPU screenshot with correct game state is a known limit of this machine, not a failed launch. A state read that is empty or wrong is a game defect.\n- The kit may be newer than 0.46.0. Read `CHANGELOG.md` after 0.46.0 before trusting §3. Do not edit the engine to paper over a newer API.\n- Esc under pointer lock may never arrive as a key, and `requestPointerLock()` can reject. Follow the §3 handling or the hub check can fail on an unhandled rejection.\n- earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory is exhausted. One Chromium at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\n- WebKit on an iPhone profile cannot run WebGPU. The touch gate is judged on Chromium's phone profile. That is the closest stand-in, not iOS Safari.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7117,"output_tokens":11073,"cache_read_input_tokens":44928,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"4cb18638-dc25-433b-9b35-b5b8e4ceab96"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-29d93fa8-b466-4679-8fd3-9af36c70018b-6","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person war-zone survival shooter and hand in every TASK.md artifact that this computer can produce.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Title and hand-in. Fifty playful riffs on \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" are listed and one is chosen with a real random-number generator (not by hand). That exact string is the on-screen title, the HTML `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The folder that contains `STAGE.md` holds (a) one HTML file whose filename stem is `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`, runnable from `file://` with nothing else required beside it, (b) `exit-interview.json` beside that HTML, one object in TASK.md's exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, \\\"unknown\\\" only where a fact cannot be confirmed, and (c) `PROGRESS.md`. All dev files live in a subfolder with that same stem. The shipped `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit zip and is never edited. About/Credits, reachable from the main menu and from pause, shows every TASK.md Source Information field (Date `2026-11-05 01:39:17`, Source `Far Cry 2`, Plan `PLAN.md`, genre, perspective, tech, art, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, Usage `added on import`), then Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback as the brief's questions with answers in that order, then credits for Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, and the words \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\". No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, or `coming soon`, and no half-built feature, remains.\\n2. Never-cut survival systems. On one continuous 1280×1280 m basin the player moves and drives in first person (walk, run, sprint, crouch, jump, wade). Guns wear with use, jam so they cannot fire until repeated reloads clear them, and fall apart at condition 0; locker guns start clean and ground guns start worn. A 5 m fire grid spreads with wind (measured head speed 2–5 m/s when wind is 5 m/s, flanks slower); burnt ground cannot burn again; scars are visible on the map. Blackwater fever is cleared by a held tablet; ignoring it collapses the player and removes stones. Low health can run a field-surgery scene; at 0 HP a living buddy can rescue; a downed buddy offers a syrette or finish-it, and only finish-it when no syrette remains. The paper map and GPS are held in the hands while walking and while driving, and the world does not pause. With clean storage, music volume is 0.1. Voices are code gibberish. Required effects are code sounds that render non-silent. No audio files are used.\\n3. Original story slice. Country, people, factions, weapons' names, and places are invented. None of Far Cry 2's proper nouns, characters, plot, or map appear. Shipped missions run from the opening ride through the reveal that the burns form a firebreak, then a real choice of who receives the minefield sheet, then both ending cards in PLAN.md §14. Story scars are laid even if the player burned something else. The Ledger reports hectares burned, people moved, men paid to kill, friends lost, and stones earned. The player's bullets cannot kill civilians. Each shipped mission can be started from its own setup and finished with real actions; both endings are reached that way, including once with a buddy already dead. Anything left out is a whole item from PLAN.md §26, taken from the top of that list, with its menu entries, map marks, and lines removed. The §26 never-cut list is all present.\\n4. 10-foot shell, attract, and dev mode. UI is drawn with the engine HUD in art pixels, not DOM. Prompts follow the last used device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox glyph setting, and keyboard/mouse glyphs. Mouse hover moves menu focus and a click activates; pad, keyboard, and mouse all operate the same menus. Pause sets time scale to 0. A touch-only device shows the exact notice `Controller or keyboard required` and one Settings control, with no on-screen touch controls, and attract is still allowed to start. With no input, attract starts only after 10 seconds on the title or main menu (not on Settings or About), loops without error, shows a demo tag, and any input returns to the screen it came from without writing a save. Pause opens Dev Mode (hidden in a hub session) with the live balance sliders in PLAN.md §17; moving a slider changes that system immediately; Reset restores every default.\\n5. Session round. Cash Run is a round of 1 to 4 players, about 6 minutes: carry stone cases to an extraction truck, friendly fire on, downed players can be revived or looted. The same round runs with no hub as solo play against hired-gun AI, and a normal boot with no session still reaches the title. Boot follows PLAN.md §19: hub connect, a headless server advanced on a timer rather than the render loop, ready signaled with no key press, and the card answering Enter and M from a window keydown listener. All four harness scenarios in PLAN.md §24 pass on the built file. If shared-world cannot be made stable, ship ghost, then parallel, only after that, with session meta tags matching the mode actually shipped, and the four scenarios still pass. Same-computer two-window link is required unless it is the §26 cut.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder run `node tests/run.mjs --all` and capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the summary line reports 0 failed. Suites must call the shipped game (headless engine, real actions, `step` from a fresh start or a mission setup), not a copy of the rules. Required observations: fire head speed in the 2–5 m/s band at 5 m/s wind and slower flanks; a burnt cell does not ignite again; a jam blocks fire and repeated reload clears it; condition 0 empties the slot; a tablet clears fever and ignoring it costs stones; surgery, rescue, and finish-it behave as in criterion 2; the map can be raised while driving and its cursor sets a waypoint; clean-storage music volume is 0.1; every balance slider changes its system and Reset restores every default; attract does not start before the 10-second boundary, does start at that boundary with no input, and exits on input without a save write; a solo round banks a case, drops it on down, revives, and finishes on time-up with every present player once in the standings; a bot finishes every shipped mission through both endings and the ledger/world assertions hold, including a run with one buddy dead; source has no `Math.random` and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub/coming soon; content strings stay inside the HUD font; every shipped sound renders non-silent; `pixel-engine.js` matches the kit zip byte for byte.\\n2. gating: Launch the real built HTML from `file://` twice, in Chromium via Playwright at 1920×1080, with the WebGPU flags in PLAN.md §24. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Each run must show no page or console errors, `AIHub.active` false, and game state whose title string equals the RNG pick. The two runs must agree. If Playwright itself cannot start, write that failure to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; the headless suite in step 1 is then the accepted bar. A screenshot buffer that comes back blank is not proof of failure and not proof of success; the state assertion is the primary observable (this machine's software WebGPU canvas can be blank). Do not invent a stand-in page.\\n3. gating: On Playwright's iPhone 14 profile under Chromium (drop the WebKit device default), open the same file and capture `{SCRATCH}/touch.log`. Pass only if the notice text is exactly `Controller or keyboard required`, a Settings control is shown, a tap opens Settings, and attract is not blocked. A WebKit iPhone load that fails for lack of WebGPU is evidence only; record it in `{SCRATCH}/webkit-iphone.log` and do not treat it as the gate.\\n4. gating: Copy the built HTML beside the hub harness files, serve that directory with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the page title is the chosen game title, then run the four PLAN.md §24 scenarios one at a time. Pass only when each reaches `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`, including any failed `__hubCheck.checks`. Stop only that server, by its PID. If same-computer link was not cut, also drive two `file://` pages through one shared round to matching standings and a shared fire or case, logged in `{SCRATCH}/link.log`. If link was cut, that log must show the feature is absent (no menu entry).\\n5. evidence: After the game has been rendering for at least 10 seconds, save the PLAN.md §24 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm the key shape. Screenshot absence or a blank WebGPU frame does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 hold.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- A recreation of Far Cry 2: its proper nouns, characters, plot, map, logos, music, and look stay out. Defining mechanics are already rewritten in PLAN.md (fever, jam and break, wind-driven grass fire, handheld map, hired buddies who can die, stones and a tracker, two rotten factions, safehouses, first person on foot and in vehicles).\\n- FPS targets, render optimization, and judging the game by wall-clock frame rate. Software WebGPU here is about 1 fps; tests advance with `step`.\\n- Physical DualSense, a TV, a human listener, a second human, real iOS Safari, prone, ladders, ledge grabs, on-screen touch controls, audio files, chat, emotes, or custom player names.\\n- Editing `pixel-engine.js`, or leaving a cut feature as a stub, menu entry, or map mark.\\n- Civilian targets, child soldiers, and sexual violence.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder root (beside `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, `TASK.md`): the single HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`: unzipped riftlight pixel-engine kit (latest release zip), unmodified AI Hub `session.js` plus the hub harness used only for tests, game sources, art and animation data, generated audio, `node tests/run.mjs`, and the esbuild single-file packager.\\n- Engine facts in PLAN.md §3 were verified on 0.46.0. Far Cry 2's defining mechanics were checked against public descriptions of malaria pills and pass-out, weapon jam and breakage, savanna fire driven by wind, diamond cases, buddies, and the in-hand map. PLAN.md already sets the original equivalents; do not research the game in order to copy it.\\n- Runtime: Node, esbuild, Playwright 1.63 Chromium with the host platform override already set. Python Playwright is available. No `playwright install`. One browser at a time.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md §25 in order and §26 when cutting. Keep this as guidance, not a file-layout contract. Separate simulation data from meshes so headless tests never need a GPU. Put missions, weapons, sites, dialogue, tuning defaults, and model defs in plain data. Gameplay reads named actions, not raw keys. Simulate in the fixed step and draw in the frame update. Read tuning values at the moment of use. One game object owns the world; title, attract, campaign, and the round are modes, not a fresh terrain load. Guard every use of `window`, `document`, `navigator`, and `localStorage`. Randomness comes from the engine's seeded generator, forked by name. Build the single HTML the way PLAN.md §23 specifies (session script first, then hub meta tags, then the bundled module) so `file://` boots. Keep the four hub scenarios passing from the first bootable build. Log defects in the dev folder's issues note as tests catch them. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each step so a compacted context can resume.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] List 50 titles, pick one with a real RNG, record the list and the pick, create the dev subfolder, unzip the kit, vendor the hub client and harness, and prove `pixel-engine.js` matches the zip.\\n- [ ] Scaffold the single-file build and a booting game so `file://` opens and all four hub scenarios pass. Start `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- [ ] Add the basin, player locomotion, look and pointer-lock fixes, actions, device glyphs, minimal HUD, and pause. Cover them with headless tests.\\n- [ ] Add weapons (wear, jam, break), hits, soldiers, health, syrette, surgery, and fever, with the system tests in criterion 2.\\n- [ ] Add the fire grid, ignition weapons, day and night, vehicles and repair, the held map, safehouses, stones, and save/load.\\n- [ ] Add missions through both endings, buddies (rescue and finish-it), the Ledger, dialogue, music at 0.1, and code voices and effects. Add the bot playthrough.\\n- [ ] Add title, menus, settings, the touch gate, attract, dev-mode sliders with reset, and About/Credits.\\n- [ ] Finish Cash Run (host, client, solo bots, card listeners). Re-run `node tests/run.mjs --all`, the two `file://` launches, the phone-profile check, and the four hub scenarios. Write `exit-interview.json` and finish `PROGRESS.md`, including any §26 cuts.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §27 demands the full game and zero failing tests; §26 allows cutting whole features from the top of its list if time runs out. A cut that leaves a stub, menu entry, or map mark fails. Never-cut systems stay even if that forces a cut higher on the list.\\n- TASK.md allows downloaded CDN dependencies \\\"if that is easier\\\". PLAN.md §23 and §27 require one HTML file that runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it. Ship the single file. Do not rely on a CDN.\\n- The HTML stem contains spaces because SOURCE is `Far Cry 2`. Keep that stem exact.\\n- There is no display. Headless Chromium with the §24 flags is the browser. A blank WebGPU screenshot with correct game state is a known limit of this machine, not a failed launch. A state read that is empty or wrong is a game defect.\\n- The kit may be newer than 0.46.0. Read `CHANGELOG.md` after 0.46.0 before trusting §3. Do not edit the engine to paper over a newer API.\\n- Esc under pointer lock may never arrive as a key, and `requestPointerLock()` can reject. Follow the §3 handling or the hub check can fail on an unhandled rejection.\\n- earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory is exhausted. One Chromium at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n- WebKit on an iPhone profile cannot run WebGPU. The touch gate is judged on Chromium's phone profile. That is the closest stand-in, not iOS Safari.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c%2Fwork/01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df/goal/plan-01a1aadc-c88f-7f63-94c6-14c1229777fe.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c%2Fwork/01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df/goal/plan-01a1aadc-c88f-7f63-94c6-14c1229777fe.md has been created.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c%2Fwork/01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df/goal/plan-01a1aadc-c88f-7f63-94c6-14c1229777fe.md\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"\",\"old_line\":1,\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person war-zone survival shooter and hand in every TASK.md artifact that this computer can produce.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Title and hand-in. Fifty playful riffs on \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" are listed and one is chosen with a real random-number generator (not by hand). That exact string is the on-screen title, the HTML `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The 