setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send`\"},{\"line_number\":263,\"content\":\"    addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel?: number): void;\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"b7ec680d-c9c3-43d2-a008-83c295cbc8e7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have a solid plan and am starting to write the game in a structured way.\n\nI'm designing the core SorrelGame class in src/game.js. It will expose a createGame factory and manage session and touch-only options. I'll also write a fire calibration script first, then tackle files in batches.\n\nThe game mode system now distinguishes title, card, and round states. A world reset routine will clear fire, actors, and mission state for new games and attract exits. Player simulation logic will be driven by step() instead of fixedUpdate, keeping all rules inside fixedUpdate.\n\nA dev API is being added to the game object with methods for mission control, teleportation, slider manipulation, and cheat toggles. A benchmark script will test terrain cell resolution before finalizing the grid size.\n\nThe benchmark will be the first implementation task, timing the terrain module immediately. Grass and water meshes will be skipped in headless mode by detecting the absence of a document object, while fire, player, and vehicle simulation continue running. Fauna is being removed as planned. The forty static tree cylinders will serve as real obstacles instead of stubs.\n\nA BroadcastChannel link will let tests drive two Playwright pages, though it may be dropped if unstable. The chase camera toggle is kept as a simple override. Dust weather is also kept as a basic flag, with optional particles. Only the first mercy run is guaranteed; extra runs are deferred if time runs short.\n\nThe mercy run bundle is being reduced to one run, three trigger points, and six cases. This partial cut of item five will save time.\n\nM4, M5, M8, and the link are all kept. Card mode is preserved because the harness checks for its own card. I will now generate files with a Python or Node script.\n\nI'm now sketching the FireGrid class in fire.js. It uses a 256×256 Uint8Array for fuel, a state array tracking fresh, burning, burnt, and regrowing cells, and Float64Arrays to track burn end times and burn timestamps.\n\nFireGrid now includes wind, rain, and slope fields. It provides index conversion between world coordinates and grid cells, plus bounds checking. The ignite method will light nearby fresh fuel cells. The tick loop advances the simulation at 4 Hz with a guard against runaway updates.\n\nThe fire spread logic now uses eight cardinal and diagonal neighbor offsets. Wind direction is compared to each neighbor’s direction using dot product, with clamped speed-based multipliers for forward and sideways motion.\n\nFire spread probability now combines fuel type, wind direction, slope, and rain into a single multiplier. Burn durations are set at 8–14 seconds for grass, 12–18 for scrub, and 30–40 for trees or buildings. Ember particles will spawn downwind when wind exceeds 6 m/s. A calibration test will measure head speed by igniting a 40-cell line with 5 m/s eastward wind and tuning the spread constant to hit roughly 3.5 m/s.\n\nI will run the fire calibration script now. Spread perpendicular to the wind will be slower than downwind, which is correct. I am adding a test that blocks fire from crossing a road stripe unless wind exceeds 6 m/s. Burnt cells will stay burnt and cannot be re-ignited. Regrowth timing will be tested by setting a very short regrowHours value and advancing the game clock.\n\nA round-trip save test will compress state with RLE, restore it, and assert equality. The game hour clock converts real minutes to days using TUNING.dayMinutes. Burnt cells will leave visible scars on the map texture for tests to detect.\n\nI will embed fire scars directly into the map pixel array so tests can verify them by checking for dark pixels. The dev folder will be named with a timestamp suffix, and PROGRESS.md will be placed at the work root. I will record the fifty title candidates and the final pick in src/content/title.js and TITLES.md, then populate the About content page with all required source fields.\n\nI will leave the exit-interview.json for the final step. The about.js draft answers must be real and contain no placeholder text. I will follow the README for finish validation. Only six stats will be shown: Stones, Downs, Revives, Kills.\n\nThe stats list is finalized at six entries: Stones, Downs, Revives, Kills, Lit, and Carried. Awards are capped at three: Last Friend, Paid in Full, and Arsonist. Player colors will come from the hub, and names will be sanitized to the font charset. Friendly fire is enabled during rounds.\n\nCase carry mechanics are set: speed drops to 85 percent with no sprint, the player banks at the truck, and drops on a down. Down bleed lasts twenty-five seconds, revive hold is three seconds, or the player can loot the case. Respawn occurs after fifteen seconds at the road end.\n\nThe six-minute round timer uses AIHub.time() when available; otherwise a local timer runs. The round ends when time expires or all cases are banked or burned. Tests will verify that the round finishes on time-up.\n\nThe round timer will be configurable through session options, defaulting to six minutes. Tests can override this with a shorter `roundSeconds` value for faster validation.\n\nStepping the simulation forward in large chunks is preferable to a slow frame-by-frame run. The world will stay light with no grass and few actors so sixty-second steps remain fast enough to reach the six-minute mark without a real clock.\n\nI realized that passing a large delta time to step() could break physics if fixedUpdate runs multiple times per frame. I will avoid cheating by not directly manipulating the round clock. Instead, I will let the solo round timer advance naturally through fixedUpdate, and use normal step sizes for the interesting gameplay moments.\n\nI will benchmark the engine step rate with a minimal boot before writing the full game. The fixedUpdate loop will stay lightweight: fire ticks only every fifteen steps, soldiers are spawned on demand, and the player controller uses a single capsule. This prevents a massive rewrite if the engine API behaves unexpectedly.\n\nI will write a minimal boot test that creates terrain and a player to benchmark step rates. The height function uses 512 cells, so I need to verify it does not stall generation. I will check whether createNoise and fbm are available in the kit. I will keep the kit's game.js and index.html in place until main.js exists, then overwrite them. The actual game code will live in src/game.js.\n\nI will replace index.html with the dev page and delete the kit's root game.js once our main.js is ready, avoiding two games. The static test only scans src/, so the old file can be removed safely. I will also delete the packs and examples folders later to reduce clutter. Before writing code, I need to verify the exact import names for applySky and skyAt.\n\nThe fixedUpdate loop will delegate to a sim method and skip execution when the engine timeScale is zero. Attract mode will feed bot inputs into fixedUpdate while overriding the camera and enabling god mode, but any real player input will exit the attract sequence.\n\nBot inputs will be injected through internal `this.bot` flags rather than the input device. Real input in `update` will exit attract mode. A `userInput` helper will detect key presses, mouse buttons, pointer movement, pad buttons, and taps.\n\nDevice flip logic separates right stick from mouse input. Attract mode exits on any nonzero stick past 0.35, any key press, or any mouse movement. I will check `queuedPresses` in update before the presses are consumed.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a4d71265-f72b-4485-9274-bb275d1819c9-44","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/input.d.ts","offset":1,"limit":50}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a4d71265-f72b-4485-9274-bb275d1819c9-45","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"interface ButtonEvent|type ButtonEvent|code:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/input.d.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7920,"output_tokens":4272,"cache_read_input_tokens":110592,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"20b3a646-61d2-4b9c-abbe-3f0a8d14b6fe"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-a4d71265-f72b-4485-9274-bb275d1819c9-44","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * Standard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\\n * KEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \\\"standard\\\" layout:\\n * 0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\\n */\\nexport declare const GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>;\\n/** The parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects). */\\nexport interface GamepadLike {\\n    readonly connected?: boolean;\\n10→    readonly mapping?: string;\\n    readonly axes: readonly number[];\\n    readonly buttons: readonly {\\n        readonly pressed: boolean;\\n        readonly value?: number;\\n    }[];\\n}\\n/**\\n * Radial deadzone with rescaling: inside `deadzone` reads 0, the rim reads 1, and the\\n * direction is kept (no square-gate snapping to the axes).\\n20→ */\\nexport declare function applyDeadzone(x: number, y: number, deadzone?: number): {\\n    x: number;\\n    y: number;\\n};\\n/** How long a queued press stays consumable, in seconds of game time. */\\nexport declare const PRESS_WINDOW = 0.15;\\n/**\\n * What the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\\n * Optional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\\n30→ * from the frame before.\\n */\\nexport interface InputFrame {\\n    /** Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it). */\\n    dt: number;\\n    /** Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted. */\\n    held?: string[];\\n    /** Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held). */\\n    pressed?: string[];\\n    /** The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone). */\\n40→    analog?: [number, number];\\n    pad?: [number, number];\\n    /** Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame. */\\n    delta?: [number, number];\\n    wheel?: number;\\n    /** Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says). */\\n    pointer?: [number, number, number, number];\\n    /** Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes. */\\n    gamepad?: boolean;\\n    /** Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes. */\\n50→    touch?: boolean;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * Standard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\\n * KEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \\\"standard\\\" layout:\\n * 0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\\n */\\nexport declare const GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>;\\n/** The parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects). */\\nexport interface GamepadLike {\\n    readonly connected?: boolean;\\n10→    readonly mapping?: string;\\n    readonly axes: readonly number[];\\n    readonly buttons: readonly {\\n        readonly pressed: boolean;\\n        readonly value?: number;\\n    }[];\\n}\\n/**\\n * Radial deadzone with rescaling: inside `deadzone` reads 0, the rim reads 1, and the\\n * direction is kept (no square-gate snapping to the axes).\\n20→ */\\nexport declare function applyDeadzone(x: number, y: number, deadzone?: number): {\\n    x: number;\\n    y: number;\\n};\\n/** How long a queued press stays consumable, in seconds of game time. */\\nexport declare const PRESS_WINDOW = 0.15;\\n/**\\n * What the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\\n * Optional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\\n30→ * from the frame before.\\n */\\nexport interface InputFrame {\\n    /** Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it). */\\n    dt: number;\\n    /** Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted. */\\n    held?: string[];\\n    /** Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held). */\\n    pressed?: string[];\\n    /** The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone). */\\n40→    analog?: [number, number];\\n    pad?: [number, number];\\n    /** Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame. */\\n    delta?: [number, number];\\n    wheel?: number;\\n    /** Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says). */\\n    pointer?: [number, number, number, number];\\n    /** Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes. */\\n    gamepad?: boolean;\\n    /** Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes. */\\n50→    touch?: boolean;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/input.d.ts\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * Standard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\\n * KEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \\\"standard\\\" layout:\\n * 0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\\n */\\nexport declare const GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>;\\n/** The parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects). */\\nexport interface GamepadLike {\\n    readonly connected?: boolean;\\n    readonly mapping?: string;\\n    readonly axes: readonly number[];\\n    readonly buttons: readonly {\\n        readonly pressed: boolean;\\n        readonly value?: number;\\n    }[];\\n}\\n/**\\n * Radial deadzone with rescaling: inside `deadzone` reads 0, the rim reads 1, and the\\n * direction is kept (no square-gate snapping to the axes).\\n */\\nexport declare function applyDeadzone(x: number, y: number, deadzone?: number): {\\n    x: number;\\n    y: number;\\n};\\n/** How long a queued press stays consumable, in seconds of game time. */\\nexport declare const PRESS_WINDOW = 0.15;\\n/**\\n * What the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\\n * Optional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\\n * from the frame before.\\n */\\nexport interface InputFrame {\\n    /** Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it). */\\n    dt: number;\\n    /** Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted. */\\n    held?: string[];\\n    /** Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held). */\\n    pressed?: string[];\\n    /** The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone). */\\n    analog?: [number, number];\\n    pad?: [number, number];\\n    /** Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame. */\\n    delta?: [number, number];\\n    wheel?: number;\\n    /** Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says). */\\n    pointer?: [number, number, number, number];\\n    /** Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes. */\\n    gamepad?: boolean;\\n    /** Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes. */\\n    touch?: boolean;\\n\",\"total_lines\":278}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-a4d71265-f72b-4485-9274-bb275d1819c9-45","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,