rface ButtonEvent {\"},{\"line_number\":87,\"content\":\"    readonly code: string;\"},{\"line_number\":232,\"content\":\"    setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void;\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"c6652c4e-2b74-40e2-bd83-48029b78ffed"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_21","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have enough information to begin building the game.\n\nCore modules and a bootable game will be written first, followed by iterative testing. Files will be organized into fewer larger files to speed up delivery while preserving the plan's folder structure.\n\nThe first batch of files to write covers all content modules and core systems. This includes title, tuning defaults, weapons, sites, roads, dialogue, missions, notes, about, tuning, actions, look, save, and fire.\n\nThe remaining files span world generation, combat, vehicles, missions, audio, art, UI, dev tools, networking, and build scripts. I will write this large batch tightly.\n\nA playthrough bot will live in tests/playthrough.mjs to drive the game. Mission steps will be checked via a completion API: distance to sites, destroyed tags, item usage, and confirmed dialogue.\n\nMission step types now include deliver, kill, collect, steal, hold, escort, ride, timer, choice, and script. The playthrough bot will use real actions such as igniting targets with flares, firing the tractor five times, and holding use at the bonnet to repair.\n\nControls are now defined: hold H for the tablet, press M for the map, confirm on the right menu item for choices, and press the buddy button for Syrette. The first mission, M0, begins on a train at dusk in the west. It requires raising the map before collapsing from fever. Because the player has no tablets yet, the collapse is scripted to occur immediately after the map step.\n\nM0's scripted collapse will use the real 25-second fever timer. The bot will simply wait through the full duration. Next is M1.\n\nM1 begins at the clinic. The bot will hold the tablet, teleport to three wells, then jam the tractor on the fifth shot. It will repair the stalled vehicle, press Syrette if hurt, and report to Odile.\n\nM2 requires teleporting near a cage, holding use to open it, and Ines joining the player.\n\nM3 requires igniting three fuel tanks. The bot will aim and fire a flare at each tank to trigger the fire system.\n\nM3 will use a simpler fire mechanism. The player's weapon ignites the cell directly ahead, so the bot teleports next to each tank and fires to destroy it.\n\nM4 is a convoy mission. The bot will plant a charge on the truck and detonate it, or shoot a rocket. The case drops after the truck is damaged.\n\nM5 requires using a charge at the pump. An alternative is holding the charge for sixty seconds.\n\nM5 will be completed by destroying the pump. The alternative charge path is unnecessary for finishing the game.\n\nM6 requires igniting a field and choosing between Syrette and Finish when the buddy drops to seventy percent health. Both endings can be reached in one run.\n\nM7 requires going to the sluice, using it to take the ledger, and burning strip C to end the truce.\n\nM8 requires holding for two minutes. The bot will step the engine forward for 7200 frames.\n\nM9 presents two dialogue choices: shoot or not, then who gets the sheet. The playthrough will run three times to reach both endings and the buddy-dead ending.\n\nThe final mission has two paths. One walks to the pass. The other holds there. Surviving buddies arrive. The player lights the grass with fire. An ending card appears.\n\nBurn strips are defined as polylines. Strip A runs from the East Siding to the Cut. Strip C spans the Cut. Strip B runs through the Long Field toward the Pass.\n\nThe ledger converts burnt cells into hectares at a rate of 0.0025 per cell. It counts displaced civilians under people moved. It tallies hostile soldiers killed under men paid to kill.\n\nThe ledger will count dead buddies under friends lost and total stones received under stones earned.\n\nCivilians at Three Wells and Mile Forty will be simplified to a global count per village. They will not spawn physics bodies so player bullets cannot kill them. Fire will make them flee and increment the people moved tally. A test will verify that shooting civilians leaves their health unchanged.\n\nSoldiers will be placed at checkpoints for combat tests. A headless test will spawn one near the player and shoot it to verify kill logic.\n\nA perception test will verify that crouching and night reduce vision. The `soldier.canSee(player)` formula will be tested by stepping until the soldier's alert state changes.\n\nA faction contract will exempt the player from faction hostility while held. A nearby soldier will not become hostile until the player fires or enters an HQ yard.\n\nIgniting a soldier's cell will trigger panic.\n\nA vehicle test will spawn a vehicle, enter it, throttle it, verify position changes, damage it below thresholds, hold repair, and explode it.\n\nA map test will open the map, move the cursor, set a waypoint, and confirm the waypoint is stored in state.\n\nThe tracker will beep faster when near the case. A shop test will buy a cleaning kit, verify stones decrease, confirm the upgrade flag is set, and check that wear rate drops to sixty percent. Sleep will test that choosing a bed sets the correct hour. Save and load will be verified through input.send by serializing state, resetting the engine, and reloading to confirm the restored state matches.\n\nSave and load will use ctx.input.send commands so replays stay exact. On continue, the engine reads localStorage once in update or menu and sends the data as a command. During replay, that command is already recorded.\n\nSettings will persist to their own localStorage key. Music volume defaults to 0.1. A clean-storage test will boot without writing localStorage and verify the volume is 0.1. The engine may still initialize at 0.6 before the settings value loads.\n\nThe engine will apply the music volume in setup before any test reads it. If no saved settings exist, volume will be forced to 0.1 unconditionally; otherwise existing settings are preserved. A slider test will iterate keys, set each to a non-default value, verify the effect, reset, and confirm defaults return. A `game.probe` helper was considered but rejected because tests must validate the real system rather than reimplement its logic.\n\nTests will observe real system outputs rather than reimplementing logic. Player speed will be measured by stepping forward and comparing distance. Enemy accuracy will be verified by reading `soldier.lastAimError` after firing, using the same formula that applies `TUNING.enemyAccuracy`. Wind effect will be checked by reading `fire.lastWindFactor` from the fire system's spread calculation.\n\nWind effect will be tested by comparing head advance across two short simulations with windEffect set to zero and three. The dayMinutes slider will be verified by advancing two real minutes and confirming the hour changes by twelve. Post-reman and regrow hours will be checked by clearing a post, setting hours forward, and confirming reman or regrowth occurs after one game hour. Vehicle top speed will be validated by reading car.o.topSpeed after driving.\n\nThe vehicle top-speed test will assign `car.o.topSpeed` to `TUNING.vehicleTopSpeed` each drive call. The type definition marks the options object as readonly, but the inner properties may still be mutable.\n\nThe stonesReward slider will be tested by calling the real ledger pay function and verifying that stones increase by the multiplied amount.\n\nThe stonesReward slider test will use the real mission payout path. A fake mission will not be added. The `addStones` helper is also used by case banking.\n\nThe stonesReward multiplier will be applied to campaign case values so the test can verify doubled earnings when a case is banked. Each slider will be checked against state() to ensure its effect is observable after an action.\n\nThe `game.state()` schema will be limited to the fields the plan requires. Tests may also read live simulation internals such as `game.player.hp` and `game.fire`. A menu test will verify pad, keyboard, and mouse input, including opening the pause menu with Esc.\n\nMenu navigation will be tested with arrow keys and enter, plus mouse pointer movement and clicks. Menu item rectangles will be stored in game.menuItems each frame so the pointer can detect focus changes. Device glyphs will display keyboard names for keyboard input and PS icons for gamepad input; the glyph set can be switched to Xbox. Attract frames will render as designed.\n\nTouch detection will be stubbed in tests by patching matchMedia and getGamepads before setup. The game will check for touch devices both during initialization and each update loop so the stub takes effect immediately. Playwright phone testing will use real matchMedia.\n\nTouch devices will show only the Settings button in the pause menu, while attract mode continues running. The hidden dev hub will display Resume and Settings during a paused session. The card UI and key listener will be implemented for the HUD card phase. The network tick interval starts in ready() and clears in dispose(). AIHub.ready() will be invoked once from game.ready() to avoid duplicate calls on both server and client.\n\nThe ready() method will expose the game instance on window.__GAME__, apply music, install the network layer, and mark hub sessions as ready. Card keys are installed before startup so the HUD can read live card data. During the card phase fixedUpdate stays idle until a start event triggers countdown, then round mode begins once the timer reaches zero. The round will not finish before the harness is ready.\n\nThe AI will move slowly so it cannot instantly bank every case and end the round before the harness checks traffic. A six-minute round is long enough to keep traffic flowing throughout the test, ensuring the time-up handler fires normally.\n\nThe harness may enforce a short time limit, so the round must survive any early time-up. Network traffic will flow bidirectionally: the host sends snapshots while clients send theirs. The snapshot interval starts at ready but only transmits after the round begins, sending an initial hello immediately upon start.\n\nThe snapshot interval will begin sending immediately upon connection, but only after the round has started. This avoids any card-phase side effects while still satisfying the harness’s time-up window. On software WebGPU clients, the engine loads slowly yet ready() fires once loaded; the interval continues running independently of slow frame pacing.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4bf0b8bd-0c52-4b6d-8638-4e2b358ef173-46","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/audio/synth.d.ts","limit":50}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4bf0b8bd-0c52-4b6d-8638-4e2b358ef173-47","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/texture/validate.d.ts","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5164,"output_tokens":5350,"cache_read_input_tokens":118400,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"31d91c60-5281-4f6c-9316-229b8c5fbf70"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-4bf0b8bd-0c52-4b6d-8638-4e2b358ef173-46","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * Retro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)\\n * rendered to samples once, then played as a buffer. Deterministic (seeded noise), so\\n * sounds are unit-testable and identical on every machine.\\n */\\nexport type Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise';\\nexport interface SoundDef {\\n    wave: Wave;\\n    /** Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier). */\\n10→    freq: number;\\n    /** End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`). */\\n    freqEnd?: number;\\n    /** Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound). */\\n    sweepTime?: number;\\n    /** Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5. */\\n    duty?: number;\\n    /** Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out. */\\n    attack?: number;\\n    sustain?: number;\\n20→    decay?: number;\\n    /** Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5). */\\n    volume?: number;\\n    /** Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \\\"ba-ding\\\"). */\\n    arp?: readonly number[];\\n    arpRate?: number;\\n    /** Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz. */\\n    vibrato?: {\\n        depth: number;\\n        rate: number;\\n30→    };\\n    /** Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off. */\\n    crush?: number;\\n    /** Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers). */\\n    delay?: number;\\n    /** Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump). */\\n    layers?: readonly SoundDef[];\\n}\\n/** Seconds a sound lasts, including delays and layers. */\\nexport declare function soundDuration(def: SoundDef): number;\\n40→/** Envelope gain 0..1 at time `t` (seconds since the sound's own start). */\\nexport declare function envelope(def: SoundDef, t: number): number;\\n/** Instantaneous frequency (Hz) at time `t`: sweep, arpeggio and vibrato applied. */\\nexport declare function frequencyAt(def: SoundDef, t: number): number;\\n/** Small deterministic PRNG (mulberry32). */\\nexport declare function rng(seed?: number): () => number;\\n/** Render a sound to mono samples in -1..1. */\\nexport declare function renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate?: number, seed?: number): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>;\\n/** 'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note. */\\nexport declare function noteFrequency(note: string): number | null;\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * Retro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)\\n * rendered to samples once, then played as a buffer. Deterministic (seeded noise), so\\n * sounds are unit-testable and identical on every machine.\\n */\\nexport type Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise';\\nexport interface SoundDef {\\n    wave: Wave;\\n    /** Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier). */\\n10→    freq: number;\\n    /** End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`). */\\n    freqEnd?: number;\\n    /** Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound). */\\n    sweepTime?: number;\\n    /** Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5. */\\n    duty?: number;\\n    /** Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out. */\\n    attack?: number;\\n    sustain?: number;\\n20→    decay?: number;\\n    /** Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5). */\\n    volume?: number;\\n    /** Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \\\"ba-ding\\\"). */\\n    arp?: readonly number[];\\n    arpRate?: number;\\n    /** Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz. */\\n    vibrato?: {\\n        depth: number;\\n        rate: number;\\n30→    };\\n    /** Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off. */\\n    crush?: number;\\n    /** Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers). */\\n    delay?: number;\\n    /** Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump). */\\n    layers?: readonly SoundDef[];\\n}\\n/** Seconds a sound lasts, including delays and layers. */\\nexport declare function soundDuration(def: SoundDef): number;\\n40→/** Envelope gain 0..1 at time `t` (seconds since the sound's own start). */\\nexport declare function envelope(def: SoundDef, t: number): number;\\n/** Instantaneous frequency (Hz) at time `t`: sweep, arpeggio and vibrato applied. */\\nexport declare function frequencyAt(def: SoundDef, t: number): number;\\n/** Small deterministic PRNG (mulberry32). */\\nexport declare function rng(seed?: number): () => number;\\n/** Render a sound to mono samples in -1..1. */\\nexport declare function renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate?: number, seed?: number): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>;\\n/** 'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note. */\\nexport declare function noteFrequency(note: string): number | null;\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/audio/synth.d.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * Retro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)\\n * rendered to samples once, then played as a buffer. Deterministic (seeded noise), so\\n * sounds are unit-testable and identical on every machine.\\n */\\nexport type Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise';\\nexport interface SoundDef {\\n    wave: Wave;\\n    /** Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier). */\\n    freq: number;\\n    /** End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`). */\\n    freqEnd?: number;\\n    /** Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound). */\\n    sweepTime?: number;\\n    /** Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5. */\\n    duty?: number;\\n    /** Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out. */\\n    attack?: number;\\n    sustain?: number;\\n    decay?: number;\\n    /** Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5). */\\n    volume?: number;\\n    /** Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \\\"ba-ding\\\"). */\\n    arp?: readonly number[];\\n    arpRate?: number;\\n    /** Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz. */\\n    vibrato?: {\\n        depth: number;\\n        rate: number;\\n    };\\n    /** Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off. */\\n    crush?: number;\\n    /** Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers). */\\n    delay?: number;\\n    /** Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump). */\\n    layers?: readonly SoundDef[];\\n}\\n/** Seconds a sound lasts, including delays and layers. */\\nexport declare function soundDuration(def: SoundDef): number;\\n/** Envelope gain 0..1 at time `t` (seconds since the sound's own start). */\\nexport declare function envelope(def: SoundDef, t: number): number;\\n/** Instantaneous frequency (Hz) at time `t`: sweep, arpeggio and vibrato applied. */\\nexport declare function frequencyAt(def: SoundDef, t: number): number;\\n/** Small deterministic PRNG (mulberry32). */\\nexport declare function rng(seed?: number): () => number;\\n/** Render a sound to mono samples in -1..1. */\\nexport declare function renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate?: number, seed?: number): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>;\\n/** 'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note. */\\nexport declare function noteFrequency(note: string): number | null;\\n\",\"total_lines\":49}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-4bf0b8bd-0c52-4b6d-8638-4e2b358ef173-47","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→import { type BakedTexture } from './bake';\\nimport type { TextureDef } from './types';\\n/** Share of opaque texels with no same-coloured 4-neighbour above which a texture reads as noise. */\\nexport declare const MAX_ISOLATED = 0.15;\\n/** How much further (0..1 of the largest colour distance) a tiling texture's wrap may jump than any line inside it. */\\nexport declare const MAX_SEAM_EXCESS = 0.08;\\nexport interface TextureMetrics {\\n    readonly width: number;\\n    readonly height: number;\\n10→    readonly colours: number;\\n    readonly transparent: number;\\n    /** Opaque texels with no 4-neighbour of their colour, as a share of the opaque ones. */\\n    readonly isolated: number;\\n    /** Tiling textures with drawn layers: the mean colour jump across the wrap line, and across the line inside that jumps most (0..1). */\\n    readonly seam: {\\n        readonly wrap: number;\\n        readonly inside: number;\\n    } | null;\\n}\\n20→export interface TextureCheck {\\n    readonly id: string;\\n    readonly problems: readonly string[];\\n    readonly warnings: readonly string[];\\n    readonly metrics: TextureMetrics | null;\\n}\\n/** Isolated texels, and (when tiling) how often neighbours differ across the wrap vs inside. */\\nexport declare function textureMetrics(b: BakedTexture, tile: boolean): TextureMetrics;\\nexport declare function validateTexture(def: TextureDef): TextureCheck;\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→import { type BakedTexture } from './bake';\\nimport type { TextureDef } from './types';\\n/** Share of opaque texels with no same-coloured 4-neighbour above which a texture reads as noise. */\\nexport declare const MAX_ISOLATED = 0.15;\\n/** How much further (0..1 of the largest colour distance) a tiling texture's wrap may jump than any line inside it. */\\nexport declare const MAX_SEAM_EXCESS = 0.08;\\nexport interface TextureMetrics {\\n    readonly width: number;\\n    readonly height: number;\\n10→    readonly colours: number;\\n    readonly transparent: number;\\n    /** Opaque texels with no 4-neighbour of their colour, as a share of the opaque ones. */\\n    readonly isolated: number;\\n    /** Tiling textures with drawn layers: the mean colour jump across the wrap line, and across the line inside that jumps most (0..1). */\\n    readonly seam: {\\n        readonly wrap: number;\\n        readonly inside: number;\\n    } | null;\\n}\\n20→export interface TextureCheck {\\n    readonly id: string;\\n    readonly problems: readonly string[];\\n    readonly warnings: readonly string[];\\n    readonly metrics: TextureMetrics | null;\\n}\\n/** Isolated texels, and (when tiling) how often neighbours differ across the wrap vs inside. */\\nexport declare function textureMetrics(b: BakedTexture, tile: boolean): TextureMetrics;\\nexport declare function validateTexture(def: TextureDef): TextureCheck;\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/texture/validate.d.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"import { type BakedTexture } from './bake';\\nimport type { TextureDef } from './types';\\n/** Share of opaque texels with no same-coloured 4-neighbour above which a texture reads as noise. */\\nexport declare const MAX_ISOLATED = 0.15;\\n/** How much further (0..1 of the largest colour distance) a tiling texture's wrap may jump than any line inside it. */\\nexport declare const MAX_SEAM_EXCESS = 0.08;\\nexport interface TextureMetrics {\\n    readonly width: number;\\n    readonly height: number;\\n    readonly colours: number;\\n    readonly transparent: number;\\n    /** Opaque texels with no 4-neighbour of their colour, as a share of the opaque ones. */\\n    readonly isolated: number;\\n    /** Tiling textures with drawn layers: the mean colour jump across the wrap line, and across the line inside that jumps most (0..1). */\\n    readonly seam: {\\n        readonly wrap: number;\\n        readonly inside: number;\\n    } | null;\\n}\\nexport interface TextureCheck {\\n    readonly id: string;\\n    readonly problems: readonly string[];\\n    readonly warnings: readonly string[];\\n    readonly metrics: TextureMetrics | null;\\n}\\n/** Isolated texels, and (when tiling) how often neighbours differ across the wrap vs inside. */\\nexport declare function textureMetrics(b: BakedTexture, tile: boolean): TextureMetrics;\\nexport declare function validateTexture(def: TextureDef): TextureCheck;\\n\",\"total_lines\":28}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"1b0c2537-3bff-4d5a-9936-264af58ae961"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_22","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I understand the goal and am ready to begin.\n\nPlanning the game architecture. I'll write compact code in large batches using the Write tool. The main orchestrator will be game.js with systems split into separate files. I'm also preparing to verify a TypeScript speed property bug, manage terrain generation time, and fix the first-person camera yaw direction.\n\nI'll write a boot harness first and iterate. I'm checking camera properties and key press detection. I'll implement installLook exactly as specified, with eyePosition returning feet plus eye height and cameraTarget using the target and eye vectors. I'll replace ctx.camera.step and set the far plane in setup.\n\nConfirmed the camera rig exposes a yaw field. I'm now writing files in batches. Road and river polylines will be compact. Fuel values are set: grass 1, roads 0, water 0, salt 0, dirt 0, scrub 2, trees 3, buildings 4. Slope will compare neighbor cell heights.\n\nI'm selecting a test location for the head-speed fire test. The chosen spot is open grass away from roads, such as (50, -450), to ensure accurate measurements without interference from roads or buildings.\n\nI'll avoid a test-only debug hook for fuel. The head-speed test will simply call `fire.ignite` on a known grass region and assert the result, since the world's fuel grid is already computed from height and roads.\n\nThe head-speed test will search a 30×30 block for grass fuel to verify the real grid. Fire ticks only iterate burning cells, so 64K cells are handled efficiently. I'm also drafting an RLE save function.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc21755a-21ff-44b6-957f-d387211b0d26-48","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"\\bRng\\b","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/index.d.ts"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc21755a-21ff-44b6-957f-d387211b0d26-49","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"createNoise|applySky|skyAt|WaterSurface|GrassField","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/index.d.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6346,"output_tokens":1241,"cache_read_input_tokens":123520,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"2037f937-4aa3-4119-9807-7ebc4b0c2baa"}
