{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_32","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Head at wind 5 is 2.5–3.44, inside the 2–5 range.\n\nFlanks are slower than the head. Wind 0 shows a head of 2.19 and a flank of 0.94, which is unexpected because no wind should spread equally. The flank measurement starts from the ends of a long line, so those ends may spread outward slower than the head.\n\nThe no-wind flank is slower because line fire tips spread slower than the advancing head. The test should use a wide line for 12+ seconds, asserting the head stays in [2,5] and the flank stays below it. Variance remains.\n\nThe fire RNG is forked from the engine seed, so a fixed seed makes the test deterministic. The verification requires tests to call the shipped game rather than a standalone copy of the rules. A unit test of FireGrid is acceptable because it exercises the shipped code.\n\nThe fire test will drive the game's step() entry point rather than FireGrid directly. The game's internal fire grid is identical, but the RNG fork may shift speeds slightly across seeds. The constant should still land in range.\n\nEight seeds all landed in the 2.5–3.44 range.\n\nA 1-cell road at wind 5 may not block fire. Cardinal spread stops at fuel-0 cells, but diagonal neighbors could still cross a single-cell road.\n\nA 1-cell fuel-0 wall blocks fire at wind 5 because ember jumps only occur above wind 6. The next step is writing the remaining game files: sites, roads, weapons, dialogue, missions, notes, and about.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-448ca29d-d038-4b88-a00e-0359f69ae5a5-63","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/procgen/noise.d.ts","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":896,"output_tokens":1158,"cache_read_input_tokens":156288,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"4a6cb073-88b3-47b2-a7e5-8aa7ee8efcbb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-448ca29d-d038-4b88-a00e-0359f69ae5a5-63","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→export interface FbmOptions {\\n    /** How many layers of noise (default 5). Each adds detail at twice the frequency. */\\n    octaves?: number;\\n    /** Frequency step per octave (default 2). */\\n    lacunarity?: number;\\n    /** Amplitude step per octave, the roughness (default 0.5). */\\n    gain?: number;\\n}\\nexport interface Noise {\\n10→    readonly seed: number;\\n    /** Simplex noise in 2D, about -1..1. */\\n    n2(x: number, y: number): number;\\n    /** Simplex noise in 3D, about -1..1 (a 2D field that changes over time: n3(x, y, t)). */\\n    n3(x: number, y: number, z: number): number;\\n    /** Fractal Brownian motion: octaves of 2D noise added up, normalised to about -1..1. */\\n    fbm2(x: number, y: number, o?: FbmOptions): number;\\n    /** Ridged noise: 1 − |noise| per octave, squared, so creases become sharp crests; 0..1. */\\n    ridged2(x: number, y: number, o?: FbmOptions): number;\\n}\\n20→export declare function createNoise(seed?: number): Noise;\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→export interface FbmOptions {\\n    /** How many layers of noise (default 5). Each adds detail at twice the frequency. */\\n    octaves?: number;\\n    /** Frequency step per octave (default 2). */\\n    lacunarity?: number;\\n    /** Amplitude step per octave, the roughness (default 0.5). */\\n    gain?: number;\\n}\\nexport interface Noise {\\n10→    readonly seed: number;\\n    /** Simplex noise in 2D, about -1..1. */\\n    n2(x: number, y: number): number;\\n    /** Simplex noise in 3D, about -1..1 (a 2D field that changes over time: n3(x, y, t)). */\\n    n3(x: number, y: number, z: number): number;\\n    /** Fractal Brownian motion: octaves of 2D noise added up, normalised to about -1..1. */\\n    fbm2(x: number, y: number, o?: FbmOptions): number;\\n    /** Ridged noise: 1 − |noise| per octave, squared, so creases become sharp crests; 0..1. */\\n    ridged2(x: number, y: number, o?: FbmOptions): number;\\n}\\n20→export declare function createNoise(seed?: number): Noise;\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/procgen/noise.d.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"export interface FbmOptions {\\n    /** How many layers of noise (default 5). Each adds detail at twice the frequency. */\\n    octaves?: number;\\n    /** Frequency step per octave (default 2). */\\n    lacunarity?: number;\\n    /** Amplitude step per octave, the roughness (default 0.5). */\\n    gain?: number;\\n}\\nexport interface Noise {\\n    readonly seed: number;\\n    /** Simplex noise in 2D, about -1..1. */\\n    n2(x: number, y: number): number;\\n    /** Simplex noise in 3D, about -1..1 (a 2D field that changes over time: n3(x, y, t)). */\\n    n3(x: number, y: number, z: number): number;\\n    /** Fractal Brownian motion: octaves of 2D noise added up, normalised to about -1..1. */\\n    fbm2(x: number, y: number, o?: FbmOptions): number;\\n    /** Ridged noise: 1 − |noise| per octave, squared, so creases become sharp crests; 0..1. */\\n    ridged2(x: number, y: number, o?: FbmOptions): number;\\n}\\nexport declare function createNoise(seed?: number): Noise;\\n\",\"total_lines\":20}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"6508c461-be43-4043-a2d6-528791866709"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_33","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll use three octaves for speed while keeping the formula structure.\n\nI'll use the default 5 octaves first and benchmark. Then I'll write terrain.js, sites, roads, weapons, and the main game. I'm designing a World class in session-world.js to hold player state, weapons, fire, missions, vehicles, and actors.\n\nThe World class will hold a FireGrid, PlayerState, weapons, actors, vehicles, and missions. It tracks resources like stones, tablets, and syrettes. It also stores time, wind, flags, seen items, waypoints, and map controls.\n\nThe World class will also track the active vehicle, fever state, surgery status, god mode, and cheat flags. Tests will access it through `game.world`. The `startMission` method applies mission setup, launches the runner, and switches to play mode.\n\n`applySetup` will configure player position, time, weapons, buddies, scars, and stones. I'll define mission setups carefully so the bot can run them. A `missions.js` file will hold full step lists and a runner that interprets step types. I'm also adding a `playMission` test helper that steps the engine through each mission until completion or a 500-step timeout.\n\nThe bot's `act` function will handle each mission step type. Teleport and leave commands move the player. Tag maps and tablets trigger M and H. Destroy targets are ignited via a flare gun aimed at the camera forward vector. Script jamming requires shooting five times and reloading. Repair holds E. Menu choices select options. Hold steps pause for duration. Report and talk press confirm. The bot faces targets by setting yaw, equips the flare, and fires.\n\nFlare weapons will use a projectile system instead of hitscan. Each projectile stores position, velocity, and kind. The bot will stand ten meters away from a target, aim, fire, and step the simulation until the projectile lands to ignite the cell.\n\nProjectiles will move each fixed step without raycasting. They ignite when they hit the ground or reach their target time. The bot will stand eight meters from a tank, aim at it, fire a flare, and step until the tank is destroyed.\n\nTanks are entities with tag, position, alive status, and health. When their cell burns, they explode and die. The destroy step counts dead tags.\n\nScripted jamming uses a weapon shot counter. On the fifth shot, a mission flag forces a jam.\n\nChoice steps will render as two-button dialogue stored in `game.choice`. Only soldiers within 260 meters will be simulated, keeping performance tight. Mission M2 has eight soldiers and is manageable. Mission M4 uses a three-vehicle convoy; the bot can rocket them to destroy it. One rocket deals 180 damage against 100 vehicle health. God mode prevents player death from enemy fire while still allowing kills.\n\nA `dev.teleport(x,z)` helper will move the player to the terrain height plus 0.2. The bot will not use a direct `ignite` command during missions; it will rely on the flare gun instead, and the mission script will call `burnStrip` only after completion. I will write the missions and dialogue content in uppercase strings, keeping each line under 60 characters. For `about.js`, I will draft provisional honest answers and update the real counts after testing.\n\nI will write world.js and game.js as the core simulation and iterate on test failures rather than planning indefinitely. The file plan is now concrete: content files first, then terrain, audio, models, actions, networking, UI, the core engine, and finally tests.\n\nI'm now writing the core content files together. Sites will define towns and safehouses with coordinates and radii. Checkpoints will mark faction posts with positions and numbers. Strips will store polygon paths for terrain features.\n\nStrip B and C are defined with polygon points. Twelve CASES positions will be placed around the map. Six TRIGS will sit on hilltops near the ridge and around the basin, starting with an observatory at (-130, -520).\n\nThe six TRIGS are now fully defined with coordinates. Twelve load-screen epigraphs will be added. The core simulation will be split into six modules: player, combat, people, mission, round, and world. I am now drafting the player fixed step function to ensure a stable first test run.\n\nThe player fixed step now consumes input actions and blocks movement during surgery, rescue, or dialogue. Speed is calculated from stance: crouch at 2, walk at 3.6, sprint at 7.4 when stamina allows, wading at 2.7, and carrying at 85% with sprint disabled. The hero entity receives the final speed value.\n\nThe player step now feeds movement, jump, and jumpHeld into the hero entity for fixedUpdate. Gameplay keys are consumed in fixedUpdate while menus use wasPressed in update. Pause freezes the world by setting timeScale to zero. The map toggle consumes M, but cursor look uses deltas while WASD still drives movement.\n\nThe camera is disabled during menus so mouse deltas still reach the cursor. The camera step runs in update, so the cursor can read those deltas before the camera consumes them. I will verify setKey and consumePress with a test.\n\nWeapon fire rate logic is now defined. High-rate weapons fire continuously while held. Lower-rate weapons fire on each press. A jam counter decrements on reload and clears the jam once it reaches zero.\n\nA weapon slot empties and plays a break sound when its shot count crosses zero. Ground guns spawn at pickup coordinates with condition values. Mounted machine guns are swapped in while inside a technical vehicle, using Triangle or F, and they overheat for six seconds. The fever timer counts down during play and triggers collapse after twenty-five seconds; scripted missions can force it to zero immediately.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-40b5f32c-19d7-4cb3-a6a2-d5ef6253683d-64","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/vendor/session.js","offset":330,"limit":120}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1454,"output_tokens":3238,"cache_read_input_tokens":157184,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"c6271221-a6a4-4af5-85e3-edc1a688ac22"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-40b5f32c-19d7-4cb3-a6a2-d5ef6253683d-64","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"330→    if (what !== 'ready' && what !== 'more') return warn('answer is ready or more');\\n    post('answer', { answer: what });\\n  };\\n\\n  hub.on = function (type, fn) {\\n    (handlers[type] = handlers[type] || []).push(fn);\\n    if (pending[type] && !pending[type].flushing) {\\n      pending[type].flushing = true;\\n      setTimeout(function () {\\n        var queued = pending[type];\\n340→        delete pending[type];\\n        for (var i = 0; i < queued.length; i++) emit(type, queued[i]);\\n      });\\n    }\\n    return function () {\\n      var list = handlers[type] || [];\\n      var at = list.indexOf(fn);\\n      if (at !== -1) list.splice(at, 1);\\n    };\\n  };\\n350→\\n  /** Send JSON data to every other player, or to one with {to: id}. {reliable: false} may drop or reorder. */\\n  hub.send = function (data, opts) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return false;\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    var text;\\n    try {\\n      text = JSON.stringify(data);\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      warn('send: data is not JSON');\\n360→      return false;\\n    }\\n    if (text === undefined) return false;\\n    if (text.length > limits.maxBytes) {\\n      warn('send: message over ' + limits.maxBytes + ' bytes dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    var t = now();\\n    tokens = Math.min(limits.burst, tokens + ((t - refilled) / 1000) * limits.ratePerS);\\n    refilled = t;\\n370→    if (tokens < 1) {\\n      warn('send: over ' + limits.ratePerS + ' messages a second, dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    tokens -= 1;\\n    var fields = { data: JSON.parse(text), reliable: opts.reliable !== false };\\n    if (opts.to !== undefined && opts.to !== null) fields.to = opts.to;\\n    post('send', fields);\\n    return true;\\n  };\\n380→\\n  /** Milliseconds since the synchronized start: negative during the countdown, -Infinity before start. */\\n  hub.time = function () {\\n    return goAt === null ? -Infinity : now() - goAt;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A seeded random number generator (0 ≤ n < 1), the same sequence on every device for the same label. */\\n  hub.rng = function (label) {\\n    var h = 2166136261;\\n    var s = String(label === undefined ? '' : label);\\n390→    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\\n      h ^= s.charCodeAt(i);\\n      h = Math.imul(h, 16777619);\\n    }\\n    return generator((hub.seed ^ h) >>> 0);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A number the hub can rank: finite, given as a number or as text. Not null, '' or true, which JSON or Number would make 0. */\\n  function finite(v) {\\n    return (typeof v === 'number' || (typeof v === 'string' && v.trim() !== '')) && isFinite(v);\\n400→  }\\n\\n  /**\\n   * The whole outcome as plain JSON, field by field: the hub checks and trims\\n   * it, so new optional fields need no new client. A field that is not JSON\\n   * (a cycle, a BigInt) is left out, not the whole outcome.\\n   */\\n  function outcomeOf(outcome) {\\n    if (!outcome || typeof outcome !== 'object' || Array.isArray(outcome)) {\\n      warn('the outcome is not a plain object');\\n410→      return null;\\n    }\\n    var copy = {};\\n    for (var key in outcome) {\\n      if (!own(outcome, key)) continue;\\n      var value = json(outcome[key]);\\n      if (value !== undefined) copy[key] = value;\\n      else if (outcome[key] !== undefined) warn('the outcome\\\\'s ' + key + ' is not JSON: left out');\\n    }\\n    return copy;\\n420→  }\\n\\n  /** Race and side by side: report your own outcome once, {time: ms} (lower wins) or {score: n} (higher wins), with optional stats and awards. */\\n  hub.result = function (outcome) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    var given = outcome || {};\\n    delete fields.time;\\n    delete fields.score;\\n430→    if (finite(given.time)) fields.time = Number(given.time);\\n    if (finite(given.score)) fields.score = Number(given.score);\\n    if (!own(fields, 'time') && !own(fields, 'score')) return warn('result needs a finite time or score');\\n    reported = true;\\n    if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n    post('result', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Versus and co-op: the host reports the standings once, best first, ties grouped: [[2], [0, 3], [1]]. */\\n  hub.finish = function (outcome) {\\n440→    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    if (!hub.isHost) return warn('finish is for the host only');\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    if (!Array.isArray(fields.standings)) return warn('finish needs standings');\\n    reported = true;\\n    post('finish', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  function sendProgress() {\",\"content_concise\":\"330→    if (what !== 'ready' && what !== 'more') return warn('answer is ready or more');\\n    post('answer', { answer: what });\\n  };\\n\\n  hub.on = function (type, fn) {\\n    (handlers[type] = handlers[type] || []).push(fn);\\n    if (pending[type] && !pending[type].flushing) {\\n      pending[type].flushing = true;\\n      setTimeout(function () {\\n        var queued = pending[type];\\n340→        delete pending[type];\\n        for (var i = 0; i < queued.length; i++) emit(type, queued[i]);\\n      });\\n    }\\n    return function () {\\n      var list = handlers[type] || [];\\n      var at = list.indexOf(fn);\\n      if (at !== -1) list.splice(at, 1);\\n    };\\n  };\\n350→\\n  /** Send JSON data to every other player, or to one with {to: id}. {reliable: false} may drop or reorder. */\\n  hub.send = function (data, opts) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return false;\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    var text;\\n    try {\\n      text = JSON.stringify(data);\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      warn('send: data is not JSON');\\n360→      return false;\\n    }\\n    if (text === undefined) return false;\\n    if (text.length > limits.maxBytes) {\\n      warn('send: message over ' + limits.maxBytes + ' bytes dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    var t = now();\\n    tokens = Math.min(limits.burst, tokens + ((t - refilled) / 1000) * limits.ratePerS);\\n    refilled = t;\\n370→    if (tokens < 1) {\\n      warn('send: over ' + limits.ratePerS + ' messages a second, dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    tokens -= 1;\\n    var fields = { data: JSON.parse(text), reliable: opts.reliable !== false };\\n    if (opts.to !== undefined && opts.to !== null) fields.to = opts.to;\\n    post('send', fields);\\n    return true;\\n  };\\n380→\\n  /** Milliseconds since the synchronized start: negative during the countdown, -Infinity before start. */\\n  hub.time = function () {\\n    return goAt === null ? -Infinity : now() - goAt;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A seeded random number generator (0 ≤ n < 1), the same sequence on every device for the same label. */\\n  hub.rng = function (label) {\\n    var h = 2166136261;\\n    var s = String(label === undefined ? '' : label);\\n390→    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\\n      h ^= s.charCodeAt(i);\\n      h = Math.imul(h, 16777619);\\n    }\\n    return generator((hub.seed ^ h) >>> 0);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A number the hub can rank: finite, given as a number or as text. Not null, '' or true, which JSON or Number would make 0. */\\n  function finite(v) {\\n    return (typeof v === 'number' || (typeof v === 'string' && v.trim() !== '')) && isFinite(v);\\n400→  }\\n\\n  /**\\n   * The whole outcome as plain JSON, field by field: the hub checks and trims\\n   * it, so new optional fields need no new client. A field that is not JSON\\n   * (a cycle, a BigInt) is left out, not the whole outcome.\\n   */\\n  function outcomeOf(outcome) {\\n    if (!outcome || typeof outcome !== 'object' || Array.isArray(outcome)) {\\n      warn('the outcome is not a plain object');\\n410→      return null;\\n    }\\n    var copy = {};\\n    for (var key in outcome) {\\n      if (!own(outcome, key)) continue;\\n      var value = json(outcome[key]);\\n      if (value !== undefined) copy[key] = value;\\n      else if (outcome[key] !== undefined) warn('the outcome\\\\'s ' + key + ' is not JSON: left out');\\n    }\\n    return copy;\\n420→  }\\n\\n  /** Race and side by side: report your own outcome once, {time: ms} (lower wins) or {score: n} (higher wins), with optional stats and awards. */\\n  hub.result = function (outcome) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    var given = outcome || {};\\n    delete fields.time;\\n    delete fields.score;\\n430→    if (finite(given.time)) fields.time = Number(given.time);\\n    if (finite(given.score)) fields.score = Number(given.score);\\n    if (!own(fields, 'time') && !own(fields, 'score')) return warn('result needs a finite time or score');\\n    reported = true;\\n    if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n    post('result', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Versus and co-op: the host reports the standings once, best first, ties grouped: [[2], [0, 3], [1]]. */\\n  hub.finish = function (outcome) {\\n440→    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    if (!hub.isHost) return warn('finish is for the host only');\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    if (!Array.isArray(fields.standings)) return warn('finish needs standings');\\n    reported = true;\\n    post('finish', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  function sendProgress() {\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/vendor/session.js\",\"offset\":330,\"limit\":120,\"raw_output\":\"    if (what !== 'ready' && what !== 'more') return warn('answer is ready or more');\\n    post('answer', { answer: what });\\n  };\\n\\n  hub.on = function (type, fn) {\\n    (handlers[type] = handlers[type] || []).push(fn);\\n    if (pending[type] && !pending[type].flushing) {\\n      pending[type].flushing = true;\\n      setTimeout(function () {\\n        var queued = pending[type];\\n        delete pending[type];\\n        for (var i = 0; i < queued.length; i++) emit(type, queued[i]);\\n      });\\n    }\\n    return function () {\\n      var list = handlers[type] || [];\\n      var at = list.indexOf(fn);\\n      if (at !== -1) list.splice(at, 1);\\n    };\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Send JSON data to every other player, or to one with {to: id}. {reliable: false} may drop or reorder. */\\n  hub.send = function (data, opts) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return false;\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    var text;\\n    try {\\n      text = JSON.stringify(data);\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      warn('send: data is not JSON');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    if (text === undefined) return false;\\n    if (text.length > limits.maxBytes) {\\n      warn('send: message over ' + limits.maxBytes + ' bytes dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    var t = now();\\n    tokens = Math.min(limits.burst, tokens + ((t - refilled) / 1000) * limits.ratePerS);\\n    refilled = t;\\n    if (tokens < 1) {\\n      warn('send: over ' + limits.ratePerS + ' messages a second, dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    tokens -= 1;\\n    var fields = { data: JSON.parse(text), reliable: opts.reliable !== false };\\n    if (opts.to !== undefined && opts.to !== null) fields.to = opts.to;\\n    post('send', fields);\\n    return true;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Milliseconds since the synchronized start: negative during the countdown, -Infinity before start. */\\n  hub.time = function () {\\n    return goAt === null ? -Infinity : now() - goAt;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A seeded random number generator (0 ≤ n < 1), the same sequence on every device for the same label. */\\n  hub.rng = function (label) {\\n    var h = 2166136261;\\n    var s = String(label === undefined ? '' : label);\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\\n      h ^= s.charCodeAt(i);\\n      h = Math.imul(h, 16777619);\\n    }\\n    return generator((hub.seed ^ h) >>> 0);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A number the hub can rank: finite, given as a number or as text. Not null, '' or true, which JSON or Number would make 0. */\\n  function finite(v) {\\n    return (typeof v === 'number' || (typeof v === 'string' && v.trim() !== '')) && isFinite(v);\\n  }\\n\\n  /**\\n   * The whole outcome as plain JSON, field by field: the hub checks and trims\\n   * it, so new optional fields need no new client. A field that is not JSON\\n   * (a cycle, a BigInt) is left out, not the whole outcome.\\n   */\\n  function outcomeOf(outcome) {\\n    if (!outcome || typeof outcome !== 'object' || Array.isArray(outcome)) {\\n      warn('the outcome is not a plain object');\\n      return null;\\n    }\\n    var copy = {};\\n    for (var key in outcome) {\\n      if (!own(outcome, key)) continue;\\n      var value = json(outcome[key]);\\n      if (value !== undefined) copy[key] = value;\\n      else if (outcome[key] !== undefined) warn('the outcome\\\\'s ' + key + ' is not JSON: left out');\\n    }\\n    return copy;\\n  }\\n\\n  /** Race and side by side: report your own outcome once, {time: ms} (lower wins) or {score: n} (higher wins), with optional stats and awards. */\\n  hub.result = function (outcome) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    var given = outcome || {};\\n    delete fields.time;\\n    delete fields.score;\\n    if (finite(given.time)) fields.time = Number(given.time);\\n    if (finite(given.score)) fields.score = Number(given.score);\\n    if (!own(fields, 'time') && !own(fields, 'score')) return warn('result needs a finite time or score');\\n    reported = true;\\n    if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n    post('result', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Versus and co-op: the host reports the standings once, best first, ties grouped: [[2], [0, 3], [1]]. */\\n  hub.finish = function (outcome) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    if (!hub.isHost) return warn('finish is for the host only');\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    if (!Array.isArray(fields.standings)) return warn('finish needs standings');\\n    reported = true;\\n    post('finish', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  function sendProgress() {\\n\",\"total_lines\":488}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"34da6c52-aa75-4e7d-94db-becaa1a64e2a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_34","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"AIHub.rng returns a function that generates numbers, not an object.\n\nAn `asRng` helper will normalize both function-based and object-based RNG sources into a unified interface with `.next()`, `.int()`, and `.chance()` methods.\n\nThe fallback RNG returns zero values. Math.floor is used instead of Math.random.\n\nfinish() exits early if the player has not been welcomed. The time-up handler only runs after the round starts, so this is safe.\n\nServer copies omit info.me and do not index players by it.\n\nStandings list every still-present player once, with present players first and absent ones last.\n\nStats will be a map of up to six player IDs with counts for Stones, Downs, Revives, and Kills. Awards will be an array of up to three entries with id, title, and detail.\n\nLeave and end handlers must be registered before the ready event so that queued events during loading are captured. They will be attached immediately when the hub session is created, before the engine starts.\n\nThe hubSession function forwards AIHub events to a local emitter. The game subscribes to that emitter via api.on.\n\nThe session will cache the latest card and start state immediately in createGame. The game reads these values during update to avoid missing early events. A Set tracks present players, and leave events remove the departing ID.\n\nIf the host leaves and no players remain, the round is voided without crashing. The end handler stops audio loops and music, mutes the sound, releases input, and sets an ended flag so network ticks skip sends. The time-up handler finishes immediately inside its callback rather than on the next frame.\n\nThe time-up handler only finishes if the local player is the host and the round is not already finished. It calls buildOutcome synchronously, which must be available even before game setup completes so the round can still end with known players.\n\nRound stats will live on the session object and update each step. The time-up handler reads them directly.\n\nA network tick interval runs every 80 milliseconds once ready() fires. dispose() clears it.\n\nClient sends use the unreliable `me` channel at 12 per second. Host sends use the unreliable `s` channel at 10 per second.\n\nBidirectional traffic will use the `me` channel for clients and the `s` channel for the host. The network interval will begin sending immediately when the start event arrives, not from the render loop, so the round can begin without delay.\n\nThe countdown shows 3-2-1 when AIHub.time() is negative, then plays once it reaches zero.\n\nLocal movement is predicted by each client. The host corrects remote positions via snapshots.\n\nFriendly fire is handled by the host: it applies damage when a shot hits another player and broadcasts the result in `w`.\n\nThe shared world model uses local avatar simulation on clients. Each client sends its position to the host, which relays all positions to every client. Clients render other players at those host-sent coordinates. The host also simulates cases, fire, and AI.\n\nSolo mode runs the CashRound class locally with AI mercenaries as actors. It does not use the network.\n\nLink mode will use BroadcastChannel for peer-to-peer state sharing between host and join pages. A simpler implementation is preferred over the full Link system. The harness can run two pages in one context on file://.\n\nThe main menu offers Solo, Host, and Join options. The Join page waits for a host hello before starting.\n\nThe host will either wait for a joiner or start immediately with a button. The test flow has one page host and another join, then the host starts the round; both players should see each other's actions, and standings must match at time-up. This requires built HTML on file://. Two full WebGPU pages may exhaust memory.\n\nThe link test will run in headless Chrome with WebGPU flags to save memory. If it exhausts the 20 GB limit, that feature will be removed. Terrain generation is expensive, so I will benchmark it during boot and lower the cell count from 512 to 256 if setup exceeds five seconds.\n\nI will write terrain.js and a minimal boot game to benchmark terrain generation and CharacterController speed. Content files will be produced in parallel. The full game.js will be expanded from that base.\n\nI will write the full implementation in chunks of files first, then run a single boot test to validate everything at once. For terrain height generation, roads will be flattened by sampling distance to polylines. The expected workload is roughly 13 million distance checks across 263,000 height samples, which is acceptable. I am now implementing the `distToSeg` helper.\n\nFuel painting will run after terrain generation, sampling height slope and road proximity for each of the 65,000 fire cells. GrassField will be skipped in headless mode to save memory; in the browser it will build only a few chunks near the camera lazily during updates, capped at four chunks. Tree geometry is lightweight enough to include.\n\nA single comprehensive world.js will hold the main game logic. Models will be compact: a soldier model copied exactly from the plan, plus simple boxes with thickness at least 0.06. A static checkModel test will validate geometry before use. The song system will use a simple pattern format.\n\nThe lead and drum patterns each contain exactly 16 steps, so they align. Six songs will be created: title, day, night, stalk, fight, and end.\n\nA seventh song, Fever, will be added alongside the six planned tracks. A `crack` helper will generate sound definitions for required effects. A voice system using twelve syllable definitions and a pitch map per character will handle speech playback.\n\nThe menu system will live in `src/ui/menu.js` with `openMenu`, `menuInput`, and `drawMenu`. HUD drawing goes in `src/ui/draw.js`. Glyphs will be small sprites. The bot that drives the playthrough will be embedded directly in the test file rather than inside the game, using `dev.teleport` and `dev.startMission`. A shipped dev tool named `dev.teleport` is available for this.\n\nThe bot may use `dev.teleport` and `dev.startMission` between objectives. `startMission` will reset the world while preserving terrain and fire grids, then apply setup flags, scars, position, loadout, and buddies before launching the mission runner. A `god` flag on the world object will be toggled by tests. Terrain will not be rebuilt during reset to save time. The `applySetup` function is being drafted to ingest mission data such as ID, title, giver, and prerequisites.\n\nMission setup objects now include pay, hour, and a full inventory of stones, tablets, syrettes, and a tractor gun. Flags from completed missions are preserved so each new startMission call is self-contained. The playthrough will either chain through free play or reset to canonical setups.\n\nThe bot will teleport between objectives. A continuous run without resetting preserves the ledger naturally, so each mission auto-queues after the previous one and assertions check the accumulated state.\n\nA separate test will call startMission for each mission to prove isolated setups work. The main playthrough test will run one continuous chain per ending, starting each mission from the previous one's end state. Verification requires the bot to finish every shipped mission through both endings with correct ledger and world assertions, including a run where one buddy dies.\n\nIsolated startMission tests will finish each mission with real actions. The full playthrough is the primary proof of correctness. A shorter isolated test may be added to avoid doubling total test time, since the 120-second hold is itself the action.\n\nThe continuous playthrough will call startMission for each mission. This satisfies the acceptance requirement that every shipped mission can be started from its own setup and finished with real actions. The ledger assertions will use the setup's prior state plus the mission's effects. Endings diverge at mission nine.\n\nTwo full playthroughs are planned: one through the give-sheet ending with buddies alive, and one through the walk-out ending with a buddy killed at mission six. Total simulation time is estimated at roughly five hundred seconds, which converts to about fifteen to sixty seconds of actual runtime depending on step speed. The M8 hold will be kept at its intended one hundred twenty seconds. A benchmark is needed to confirm whether the CharacterController update cost makes this feasible.\n\nThe Fever interval is set to twelve minutes by default, so it will not trigger during short missions except the scripted M0 collapse. That collapse lasts twenty-five seconds and resets the player to forty health, reduces stones by twenty percent, and clears fever before the mission ends. After M0 completes, the bot asserts its state and then calls startMission for the next mission.\n\nThe M0 collapse will use the real fever system rather than a hardcoded delay, letting the bot step until it triggers. God mode will not block collapse. The HUD draw function will guard against a null context. A fingerprint method is being drafted to capture player position, weapon, and fire counts.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-985812fd-a030-4a70-bee0-6d4ccc0a1992-65","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/Engine.d.ts","offset":530,"limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4552,"output_tokens":4361,"cache_read_input_tokens":158592,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"2e546f9e-3446-49fe-a3fd-56b83ae8a4d8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-985812fd-a030-4a70-bee0-6d4ccc0a1992-65","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"530→    get seed(): number;\\n    private _seed;\\n    private _random;\\n    /** The running level's recording (from its start), and whether a replay is driving the frames. */\\n    private rec;\\n    private replaying;\\n    private readonly recordLimit;\\n    /** Whether promise callbacks (microtasks) ran since the last frame ended (`InputFrame.sync`). */\\n    private drained;\\n    private readonly markDrained;\\n540→    /** This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`). */\\n    get random(): Rng;\\n    /** A fresh stream for `game`, before its setup: the same seed and game give the same numbers. */\\n    private reseed;\\n    /** Keep this frame's input in the level's recording (live play and `step()`, not replays). */\\n    private record;\\n    /**\\n     * The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since\\n     * (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\\n     */\\n550→    recording(): Recording | null;\\n    /**\\n     * Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was\\n     * made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load\\n     * without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manual time\\n     * (`step()`), waiting for promise callbacks wherever the recorded run did. `frames` stops\\n     * early. Afterwards the level is where the recorded one was (`fingerprint()` matches if the\\n     * game is deterministic) and stays in manual time: `step()` plays on from there, live, and\\n     * the level's recording carries on from it. Live input is ignored during a replay, from the\\n     * moment `start` runs (`input.playing`: a game's setup can tell); afterwards nothing the\\n560→     * recording held stays held. A game hook's exception is reported and the replay\\n     * goes on, as in live play.\\n     */\\n    replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o?: {\\n        frames?: number;\\n    }): Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the\\n     * clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the\\n     * game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the last frame's promise\",\"content_concise\":\"530→    get seed(): number;\\n    private _seed;\\n    private _random;\\n    /** The running level's recording (from its start), and whether a replay is driving the frames. */\\n    private rec;\\n    private replaying;\\n    private readonly recordLimit;\\n    /** Whether promise callbacks (microtasks) ran since the last frame ended (`InputFrame.sync`). */\\n    private drained;\\n    private readonly markDrained;\\n540→    /** This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`). */\\n    get random(): Rng;\\n    /** A fresh stream for `game`, before its setup: the same seed and game give the same numbers. */\\n    private reseed;\\n    /** Keep this frame's input in the level's recording (live play and `step()`, not replays). */\\n    private record;\\n    /**\\n     * The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since\\n     * (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\\n     */\\n550→    recording(): Recording | null;\\n    /**\\n     * Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was\\n     * made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load\\n     * without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manual time\\n     * (`step()`), waiting for promise callbacks wherever the recorded run did. `frames` stops\\n     * early. Afterwards the level is where the recorded one was (`fingerprint()` matches if the\\n     * game is deterministic) and stays in manual time: `step()` plays on from there, live, and\\n     * the level's recording carries on from it. Live input is ignored during a replay, from the\\n     * moment `start` runs (`input.playing`: a game's setup can tell); afterwards nothing the\\n560→     * recording held stays held. A game hook's exception is reported and the replay\\n     * goes on, as in live play.\\n     */\\n    replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o?: {\\n        frames?: number;\\n    }): Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the\\n     * clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the\\n     * game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the last frame's promise\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/Engine.d.ts\",\"offset\":530,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"    get seed(): number;\\n    private _seed;\\n    private _random;\\n    /** The running level's recording (from its start), and whether a replay is driving the frames. */\\n    private rec;\\n    private replaying;\\n    private readonly recordLimit;\\n    /** Whether promise callbacks (microtasks) ran since the last frame ended (`InputFrame.sync`). */\\n    private drained;\\n    private readonly markDrained;\\n    /** This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`). */\\n    get random(): Rng;\\n    /** A fresh stream for `game`, before its setup: the same seed and game give the same numbers. */\\n    private reseed;\\n    /** Keep this frame's input in the level's recording (live play and `step()`, not replays). */\\n    private record;\\n    /**\\n     * The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since\\n     * (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\\n     */\\n    recording(): Recording | null;\\n    /**\\n     * Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was\\n     * made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load\\n     * without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manual time\\n     * (`step()`), waiting for promise callbacks wherever the recorded run did. `frames` stops\\n     * early. Afterwards the level is where the recorded one was (`fingerprint()` matches if the\\n     * game is deterministic) and stays in manual time: `step()` plays on from there, live, and\\n     * the level's recording carries on from it. Live input is ignored during a replay, from the\\n     * moment `start` runs (`input.playing`: a game's setup can tell); afterwards nothing the\\n     * recording held stays held. A game hook's exception is reported and the replay\\n     * goes on, as in live play.\\n     */\\n    replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o?: {\\n        frames?: number;\\n    }): Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the\\n     * clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the\\n     * game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the last frame's promise\\n\",\"total_lines\":613}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"5aa211d7-9249-4132-9b8d-3782f986c6f0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_35","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Replay uses loadGame, which rebuilds the world.\n\nTerrain generation is deterministic via createNoise(seed), so loadGame rebuilds the world reliably. Replaying a short walk of 30 frames incurs only the cost of running setup twice. The replay callback must produce identical fingerprints, so Date.now will be avoided in setup. Wind random walk uses ctx.random.\n\nReplay restores ctx.random by reseeding from the recording seed and consuming the same fixedUpdates sequence. Audio volume changes are excluded from fingerprints. Fire uses no random when not burning, so only the wind walk step consumes random numbers. performance.now() will be avoided in simulation code. setInterval timers in setup or ready handlers would break replay determinism and fire during tests.\n\nThe interval timer will only start for hub or link sessions, not solo campaigns. Attract mode is disabled during replay. A `tests/lib/boot.js` helper is being added to bootstrap the headless engine with a fixed seed and camera preset.\n\nThe boot helper returns both the engine and the game. The character controller spawns at ground level via heightAt, with feet at the bottom of the capsule and the eye positioned 1.55 units above. Minor failures will not throw exceptions.\n\nThe hero speed property may not be writable, so the move vector will be scaled instead. A minimal boot test with only terrain and a character is being considered first, but the full game source will be written directly now. Content data will be split into separate files for sites, roads, weapons, and dialogue.\n\nThe full source tree is now mapped out. Content files will hold missions, notes, about text, and epigraphs. Core modules cover actions, look, save, and font helpers. World files handle terrain and map drawing. Simulation modules include weapons, body physics, actors, vehicles, missions, round logic, and world state. Audio, art, UI, and networking modules are also planned. game.js serves as the main entry point.\n\nsrc/main.js and src/look-grade.js will be added to the source tree. The file split is finalized, and writing will proceed in parallel batches starting with data files and core modules, then simulation, then game logic. Dialogue text will be kept short and in all caps. DESIGNERS.md and PROGRESS.md will be created after the game boots. Mission step texts must remain font-safe. A full draft of the first mission, \"THE GOODS LINE,\" has been started.\n\nThe first mission draft now includes its setup state, three step actions, and an onDone flag. A second mission, \"MERCY RUN,\" is being added for Odile with a 5-unit pay and a prerequisite on the first mission.\n\nThe second mission draft is now fully detailed. It sets up the player at coordinates 8, 48 with one tablet, two syrettes, a cane and a tractor gun, and the flag from the first mission. The seven-step sequence covers taking a tablet, driving medicine to Three Wells, navigating the West Road, clearing a jam, repairing the truck, using a syrette, and reporting back to Odile.\n\nThe second mission's onDone rewards now include a safehouse flag, three tablets, and a ledger payment. The remaining step types are defined: lines requires confirming subtitles; map requires raising the map; fever-fall requires collapsing from fever; tablet requires consuming one; goto completes when the player is within radius, with teleport allowed between objectives.\n\nThe goto objective must be a real driving action, not teleport. The bot will enter the truck and steer toward the target using simple autopilot controls with a timeout. Teleport is reserved only for moving between objectives.\n\nA `driveToward` helper will handle the Mercy Run objective by entering the truck and steering toward the target with a PD controller. The distance from the clinic to Three Wells is roughly 330 meters, so the bot will need about thirteen hundred steps to reach it at normal speed. The first mission spawns a truck beside the player for this purpose. For the script-jam step, the bot will fire five times before the weapon jams.\n\nThe script-jam step will reload the weapon five times before it jams. The repair step will set the truck to zero health at the start, then the bot holds E for three seconds to repair it. The syrette step will damage the player to sixty unless god mode is active; with god mode, the damage is blocked but the step still completes when the syrette is consumed.\n\nA `force` flag will let mission scripts bypass god mode when applying damage, so the syrette step actually restores health. God mode still blocks enemy fire. The report objective is completed by teleporting near the giver and pressing use; travel between objectives is not part of the action.\n\nThe destroy-tanks objective will use real fire: the bot drives or teleports near the tanks, then fires a flare at each one. Teleport is reserved for travel between objectives, while map raising, tablet consumption, jamming, repair, syrette use, weapon firing, choices, and use actions all require genuine inputs.\n\nThe first mission will use `drive` for its two travel objectives instead of `goto`, reserving teleport only for simple movement between steps. The second mission will use `destroy` to burn three tanks with flares, and the fourth mission will use rocket shots to destroy convoy vehicles.\n\nM5 will use a real charge plant and detonate action. M6 will require igniting the field, then a buddy-down script, then choosing between a syrette or finishing. M7 is a goto to the sluice to take the ledger. M8 is a 120-second hold, followed by boarding a train for 60 seconds. M9 uses subtitles, then choices between shooting or not, then giving or walking.\n\nThe ending branches based on the sheet choice: either goto pass and light grass or hold and light grass. A dead buddy after M6 still allows later missions to proceed, but alternative actions will not fire. Continuous runs must replay all missions from the start. Ledger assertions will verify that burns leave hectares above zero, the ledger reflects payments, and endings display the correct card text.\n\nA separate systems test will verify civilians cannot be killed. The design is now solid enough to begin writing code compactly, with an issues log added for any failures. Menu items will be laid out vertically centered in the HUD at 1920x1080, with each item spaced 28 pixels apart. A `pointerHud` helper will convert pointer coordinates into HUD space for hover detection.\n\nA `pad` helper is being drafted to simulate controller input with sixteen buttons. Clicks will be tested by setting pointer coordinates, pressing the button, stepping, and releasing.\n\nA `pad` helper will simulate a sixteen-button controller by returning a connected gamepad object with axes and pressed buttons. The engine polls `getGamepads` during `beginFrame`, so this mock must be injected before each step. Pad buttons are remapped so `PadCross` works correctly. The default input device is set to pad using PlayStation glyphs, switching to keyboard only when explicitly requested.\n\nA touch gate will display a notice and restrict access to settings only. Default settings are being defined with keys for audio levels, input sensitivity, aim assist, rumble, glyph set, pixel quality, visual effects, subtitles, and field of view.\n\nSettings will default to music at 0.1 and reset to that level. Pixel size will be applied via `engine.settings.set` if the method exists. The simulation will live in a large `world.js` class. A bug in `burnStrip` was verified: setting state to 2 for previously unburnt cells correctly increments `burntCount`.\n\nA performance bug was found in the fire system. `regrow()` currently scans all 65,536 cells every step, which is 4 million operations per second. It should instead run only on the 4 Hz spread tick or once per second, and scan only the burnt cell list.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a6324e76-c705-440d-aa1a-213228d7c28a-66","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/world/fire.js","old_string":"    this.burning = [];\n    this.time = 0;","new_string":"    this.burning = [];\n    this.burntList = [];\n    this.time = 0;"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a6324e76-c705-440d-aa1a-213228d7c28a-67","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/world/fire.js","old_string":"        this.state[i] = 2;\n        this.burntAt[i] = this.time;\n        this.burntCount++;\n        const ix = i % FIRE_N;\n        const iz = (i / FIRE_N) | 0;\n        this.onBurnt(ix, iz, i);\n        continue;","new_string":"        this.markBurnt(i);\n        continue;"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a6324e76-c705-440d-aa1a-213228d7c28a-68","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/world/fire.js","old_string":"            if (this.state[i] === 1) {\n              this.state[i] = 2;\n              this.burntAt[i] = this.time;\n              this.burntCount++;\n              this.onBurnt(ix, iz, i);\n            } else if (this.state[i] === 0) {\n              this.state[i] = 2;\n              this.burntAt[i] = this.time;\n              this.burntCount++;\n              this.onBurnt(ix, iz, i);\n            }","new_string":"            if (this.state[i] === 1 || this.state[i] === 0) this.markBurnt(i);"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a6324e76-c705-440d-aa1a-213228d7c28a-69","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/world/fire.js","old_string":"    this.regrow();\n  }","new_string":"  }\n\n  markBurnt(i) {\n    if (this.state[i] === 2 || this.state[i] === 3) return;\n    this.state[i] = 2;\n    this.burntAt[i] = this.time;\n    this.burntCount++;\n    this.burntList.push(i);\n    const ix = i % FIRE_N;\n    const iz = (i / FIRE_N) | 0;\n    this.onBurnt(ix, iz, i);\n  }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a6324e76-c705-440d-aa1a-213228d7c28a-70","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/world/fire.js","old_string":"  regrow() {\n    const hours = this.time / 3600;\n    if (TUNING.regrowHours <= 0) return;\n    for (let i = 0; i < this.state.length; i++) {\n      if (this.state[i] !== 2) continue;\n      const ageH = (this.time - this.burntAt[i]) / 3600;\n      if (ageH >= TUNING.regrowHours) {\n        this.state[i] = 3;\n        this.burntCount = Math.max(0, this.burntCount - 1);\n      }\n    }\n    void hours;\n  }","new_string":"  regrow() {\n    if (TUNING.regrowHours <= 0 || this.burntList.length === 0) return;\n    const keep = [];\n    for (const i of this.burntList) {\n      if (this.state[i] !== 2) continue;\n      const ageH = (this.time - this.burntAt[i]) / 3600;\n      if (ageH >= TUNING.regrowHours) {\n        this.state[i] = 3;\n        this.burntCount = Math.max(0, this.burntCount - 1);\n      } else keep.push(i);\n    }\n    this.burntList = keep;\n  }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a6324e76-c705-440d-aa1a-213228d7c28a-71","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/world/fire.js","old_string":"      this.acc -= 0.25;\n      this.spread();\n    }","new_string":"      this.acc -= 0.25;\n      this.spread();\