{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_76","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"round.tryUse will be called only if round is not null.\n\ntickMission's double-complete guard is harmless because _reported is never set. I will write compact but complete files for audio, about, glyphs, round, session, game, main, art, build, and tests. The combat bot fake uses heldAction so it auto-fires every frame while cooldown allows; consumeAction is bypassed entirely. Semi weapons will not fire on the bot.\n\nThe bot combat fake correctly supports heldAction and anyDown, so semi weapons will not fire on the bot. I will create the round before calling tryUse. Game.js will be large, and I am structuring the audio, voice, and glyphs files.\n\nI am now drafting the Round class with spawn points and case definitions. I have also mapped out the remaining files: session, art models and textures, game.js, main.js, build.mjs, index.html, and the test runner.\n\nCashRound now defines the truck position and maps each case with a taken flag and carrier. It builds per-player seat data including health, stones, kills, downs, and AI status. The local player is set to null on the server.\n\nThe local player is teleported to their seat. The round initializes the world with a cane, flare, and tractor gun, two syrettes, and an hour of 16. Eight soldiers spawn in alternating factions. Merc actors are created for remote and AI players.\n\nEach merc actor is tagged with its seat ID and full health. The noteDown method marks the local player as down, resets their HP to zero, and drops any carried case. DamageActor treats mercs as friendly fire and reduces the corresponding seat's health.\n\nThe AI tick logic is now defined. Carrying players move toward the truck; otherwise they head for the nearest case. If a downed seat is nearby, the AI moves to it and revives after three seconds. Movement speed is set to 3.5 m/s. A player can bank a case by using tryUse while near the truck.\n\ntryUse now checks if the local player is down before acting. If they are carrying a case and within five meters of the truck, it deposits the stones into their wallet and the ledger, marks the case as taken, and clears the carrier. Otherwise it begins scanning nearby downed seats to revive them.\n\ntryUse now scans for nearby downed seats to revive and for untaken cases to pick up. Revive progress accumulates in the tick loop rather than inside tryUse, because handleUse repeatedly calls tryUse while the use action is held.\n\ntick() now syncs the local seat position, health, and yaw from the hero while standing. Downed seats lose bleed over time and respawn once it reaches zero. AI players are ticked via think(dt), and merc actors are moved next.\n\nMerc actor positions and alive states are synced to their seats each tick. The host ends the round after its time limit. A revive method restores a seat to 60 health and clears its down state.\n\nThe revive method resets bleed to 25, increments the reviver's revive count, and clears the local player's down state when they are revived. A test sequence is planned: pick up a case, bank it at the truck, drop it by being hurt to zero, have an AI revive the player after three seconds, and verify the round finishes with all IDs in the standings.\n\nThe test wires session time-up events directly to round finish. The finish method groups seats by stone count into a Map so ties are handled together.\n\nThe finish method sorts grouped seats by stone count into standings. It builds per-player score and stats maps, then awards titles like \"Last Friend\" to the player with the most revives.\n\nThe finish method now calls session.finish with the standings, scores, stats, and awards. Host-leave events will not trigger a round finish. The network game interval sends snapshots; for solo play this is a no-op. A hubSession wrapper is being built to expose AIHub.time and AIHub.rng through a standard event listener interface.\n\nThe hubSession wrapper forwards card, start, time-up, end, leave, and message events from AIHub to local listeners. It tracks an ended flag that stops further sends after the end event. The finish method is gated so only the host can call it.\n\nThe hubSession wrapper is complete. AIHub event listeners must be registered before the engine starts so queued card events are not lost. The constructor will attach these listeners immediately after connect, before createGame or Engine.start trigger ready().\n\nThe main entry point will connect to AIHub, wrap the session, and start the engine. A soloSession wrapper with emit is also planned. For multiplayer, a BroadcastChannel linkSession will handle lobby handshakes and relay messages between two players.\n\nThe simulation timer uses the round's internal clock, while display time uses Date. Fire ignitions will be sent from the host to clients for synchronized effects, though this may be flaky and could be removed if tests fail. Link tests will run Playwright against two local HTML pages and require a built output. The link session will use a seeded RNG instead of Math.random.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d2868a1b-c86e-493c-b894-66c85f3828d9-84","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/hud/Hud.d.ts","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1067,"output_tokens":3634,"cache_read_input_tokens":147840,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"a6f8e36a-a4cf-4a1d-a37b-b93cf5b2f8ce"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-d2868a1b-c86e-493c-b894-66c85f3828d9-84","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→import type { Framing, Resolution } from '../framing';\\nimport { type PaletteColor } from '../palette';\\n/** Where an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left). */\\nexport type HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right';\\nexport type HudColor = PaletteColor | number;\\nexport interface HudOptions {\\n    /** Default 'white'. */\\n    color?: HudColor;\\n    /** 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'. */\\n10→    shadow?: HudColor | false;\\n    /** Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1. */\\n    scale?: number;\\n    /** Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels. */\\n    anchor?: HudAnchor;\\n}\\n/**\\n * Pixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\\n * the post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\\n * laid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\\n20→ * (`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\\n *\\n * Retained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\\n * (clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\\n * resolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\\n *\\n *   ctx.hud.clear();\\n *   ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\\n *   ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\\n */\\n30→export declare class Hud {\\n    private readonly container;\\n    /** The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn). */\\n    canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null;\\n    visible: boolean;\\n    private items;\\n    private drawn;\\n    private size;\\n    private layoutKey;\\n    constructor(container: HTMLElement | null);\\n40→    /** HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution). */\\n    get width(): number;\\n    get height(): number;\\n    /** Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation). */\\n    text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options?: HudOptions): void;\\n    /** A filled rectangle (panels, bars). */\\n    rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor?: HudAnchor): void;\\n    /**\\n     * A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped\\n     * through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\\n50→     *\\n     *   hud.sprite(4, 4, ['.yy.', 'ywyy', 'yyyy', '.yy.'], { y: 'sand', w: 'white' });\\n     */\\n    sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options?: HudOptions): void;\\n    /** Remove everything. */\\n    clear(): void;\\n    /** Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`. */\\n    measure(text: string, scale?: number): {\\n        width: number;\\n        height: number;\\n60→    };\\n    /** Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed. */\\n    sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void;\\n    dispose(): void;\\n    private createCanvas;\\n    private paint;\\n    private glyphs;\\n    private place;\\n}\\n/**\\n70→ * Top-left corner, in whole art pixels, of a w×h element placed at offset (x, y) from\\n * `anchor` on a screen of `size` art pixels. Pure (unit-tested).\\n */\\nexport declare function hudPlace(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, anchor: HudAnchor, size: Resolution): [number, number];\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→import type { Framing, Resolution } from '../framing';\\nimport { type PaletteColor } from '../palette';\\n/** Where an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left). */\\nexport type HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right';\\nexport type HudColor = PaletteColor | number;\\nexport interface HudOptions {\\n    /** Default 'white'. */\\n    color?: HudColor;\\n    /** 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'. */\\n10→    shadow?: HudColor | false;\\n    /** Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1. */\\n    scale?: number;\\n    /** Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels. */\\n    anchor?: HudAnchor;\\n}\\n/**\\n * Pixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\\n * the post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\\n * laid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\\n20→ * (`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\\n *\\n * Retained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\\n * (clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\\n * resolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\\n *\\n *   ctx.hud.clear();\\n *   ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\\n *   ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\\n */\\n30→export declare class Hud {\\n    private readonly container;\\n    /** The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn). */\\n    canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null;\\n    visible: boolean;\\n    private items;\\n    private drawn;\\n    private size;\\n    private layoutKey;\\n    constructor(container: HTMLElement | null);\\n40→    /** HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution). */\\n    get width(): number;\\n    get height(): number;\\n    /** Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation). */\\n    text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options?: HudOptions): void;\\n    /** A filled rectangle (panels, bars). */\\n    rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor?: HudAnchor): void;\\n    /**\\n     * A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped\\n     * through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\\n50→     *\\n     *   hud.sprite(4, 4, ['.yy.', 'ywyy', 'yyyy', '.yy.'], { y: 'sand', w: 'white' });\\n     */\\n    sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options?: HudOptions): void;\\n    /** Remove everything. */\\n    clear(): void;\\n    /** Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`. */\\n    measure(text: string, scale?: number): {\\n        width: number;\\n        height: number;\\n60→    };\\n    /** Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed. */\\n    sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void;\\n    dispose(): void;\\n    private createCanvas;\\n    private paint;\\n    private glyphs;\\n    private place;\\n}\\n/**\\n70→ * Top-left corner, in whole art pixels, of a w×h element placed at offset (x, y) from\\n * `anchor` on a screen of `size` art pixels. Pure (unit-tested).\\n */\\nexport declare function hudPlace(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, anchor: HudAnchor, size: Resolution): [number, number];\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/types/engine/hud/Hud.d.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"import type { Framing, Resolution } from '../framing';\\nimport { type PaletteColor } from '../palette';\\n/** Where an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left). */\\nexport type HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right';\\nexport type HudColor = PaletteColor | number;\\nexport interface Hud