{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-84f17f08-4dfb-4f9a-9b8a-29a7979b885b-84","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"957→## 23. Packaging\\n\\nThe deliverable is **one HTML file** at the place your brief names, beside the dev folder. Nothing else is needed at runtime; no CDN is used. It must run from `file://` and from any web server.\\n960→\\n**Head order is strict:**\\n```html\\n<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head>\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>/* vendor/session.js pasted unmodified, as the first script */</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\"> ... the tags from section 19 ...\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\">\\n<title>GAME_TITLE</title>\\n</head><body><div id=\\\"app\\\"></div><script type=\\\"module\\\">/* the bundle */</script></body></html>\\n970→```\\n\\n`tools/build.mjs` (the steps I ran, with `</script` escaped; set `OUT` from your brief):\\n```js\\nconst js = execFileSync('esbuild', ['src/main.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--target=esnext','--legal-comments=inline','--log-level=warning'], { maxBuffer: 1 << 29 }).toString();\\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js','utf8');\\nconst html = TEMPLATE.replace('%SESSION%', () => session.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi,'<\\\\\\\\/script')).replace('%BUNDLE%', () => js.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi,'<\\\\\\\\/script'));\\nwriteFileSync(OUT, html);\\n```\\nUse function replacers as above, because the engine contains `$` sequences. The bundle is about 5.3 MB before your game is added. Append the licence texts of the engine, three.js and Rapier from the kit's `CREDITS.md` as an HTML comment at the end of the file. esbuild bundles `pixel-engine.js` without you editing it; that satisfies \\\"keep the engine as it ships\\\".\\n980→\\n`src/main.js` is the only module that starts the engine. Start options:\\n```js\\nconst OPTIONS = withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera:{ preset:'first', fov:62 }, look: LOOK, touch:false, debugKeys:false, settings:{ button:false, key:false } });\\n```\\n`withUrlOptions` keeps the review flags (`?seed=`, `?debug=1`, `?look=`), which are useful and harmless. `debugKeys:false` keeps the engine's R, P and M hotkeys from colliding with yours; dev mode has its own tools.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 24. Testing\\n990→\\nTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\\n\\n**Suites:**\\n\\n1. **`static` (Node):** in `src/`: no `Math.random`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`. Every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\\n\\n2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\\n   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\\n   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\\n1000→   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\\n   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\\n   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\\n   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\\n   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\\n   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\\n   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\\n   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\\n   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\\n   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\\n1010→\\n3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\\n\\n4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\\n\\n5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\\n   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\\n   Check:\\n   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\\n   - `AIHub.active` false\\n1020→   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\\n   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\\n   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\\n   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\\n\\n6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** copy the built file to `tests/.site/game.html` beside a copy of `vendor/hub/*` (the harness only loads same-origin pages), serve that folder on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=game.html&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\\n   - `players=2&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\\n   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\\n1030→   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\\n\\n7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\\n\\n**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief; runs have no display, so everything here is headless): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1040→## 25. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\\n\\n| Step | Do | Done when |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\",\"content_concise\":\"957→## 23. Packaging\\n\\nThe deliverable is **one HTML file** at the place your brief names, beside the dev folder. Nothing else is needed at runtime; no CDN is used. It must run from `file://` and from any web server.\\n960→\\n**Head order is strict:**\\n```html\\n<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head>\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>/* vendor/session.js pasted unmodified, as the first script */</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\"> ... the tags from section 19 ...\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\">\\n<title>GAME_TITLE</title>\\n</head><body><div id=\\\"app\\\"></div><script type=\\\"module\\\">/* the bundle */</script></body></html>\\n970→```\\n\\n`tools/build.mjs` (the steps I ran, with `</script` escaped; set `OUT` from your brief):\\n```js\\nconst js = execFileSync('esbuild', ['src/main.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--target=esnext','--legal-comments=inline','--log-level=warning'], { maxBuffer: 1 << 29 }).toString();\\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js','utf8');\\nconst html = TEMPLATE.replace('%SESSION%', () => session.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi,'<\\\\\\\\/script')).replace('%BUNDLE%', () => js.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi,'<\\\\\\\\/script'));\\nwriteFileSync(OUT, html);\\n```\\nUse function replacers as above, because the engine contains `$` sequences. The bundle is about 5.3 MB before your game is added. Append the licence texts of the engine, three.js and Rapier from the kit's `CREDITS.md` as an HTML comment at the end of the file. esbuild bundles `pixel-engine.js` without you editing it; that satisfies \\\"keep the engine as it ships\\\".\\n980→\\n`src/main.js` is the only module that starts the engine. Start options:\\n```js\\nconst OPTIONS = withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera:{ preset:'first', fov:62 }, look: LOOK, touch:false, debugKeys:false, settings:{ button:false, key:false } });\\n```\\n`withUrlOptions` keeps the review flags (`?seed=`, `?debug=1`, `?look=`), which are useful and harmless. `debugKeys:false` keeps the engine's R, P and M hotkeys from colliding with yours; dev mode has its own tools.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 24. Testing\\n990→\\nTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\\n\\n**Suites:**\\n\\n1. **`static` (Node):** in `src/`: no `Math.random`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`. Every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\\n\\n2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\\n   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\\n   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\\n1000→   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\\n   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\\n   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\\n   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\\n   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\\n   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\\n   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\\n   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\\n   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\\n   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\\n1010→\\n3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\\n\\n4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\\n\\n5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\\n   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\\n   Check:\\n   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\\n   - `AIHub.active` false\\n1020→   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\\n   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\\n   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\\n   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\\n\\n6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** copy the built file to `tests/.site/game.html` beside a copy of `vendor/hub/*` (the harness only loads same-origin pages), serve that folder on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=game.html&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\\n   - `players=2&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\\n   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\\n1030→   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\\n\\n7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\\n\\n**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief; runs have no display, so everything here is headless): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1040→## 25. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\\n\\n| Step | Do | Done when |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":957,\"limit\":90,\"raw_output\":\"## 23. Packaging\\n\\nThe deliverable is **one HTML file** at the place your brief names, beside the dev folder. Nothing else is needed at runtime; no CDN is used. It must run from `file://` and from any web server.\\n\\n**Head order is strict:**\\n```html\\n<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head>\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>/* vendor/session.js pasted unmodified, as the first script */</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\"> ... the tags from section 19 ...\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\">\\n<title>GAME_TITLE</title>\\n</head><body><div id=\\\"app\\\"></div><script type=\\\"module\\\">/* the bundle */</script></body></html>\\n```\\n\\n`tools/build.mjs` (the steps I ran, with `</script` escaped; set `OUT` from your brief):\\n```js\\nconst js = execFileSync('esbuild', ['src/main.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--target=esnext','--legal-comments=inline','--log-level=warning'], { maxBuffer: 1 << 29 }).toString();\\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js','utf8');\\nconst html = TEMPLATE.replace('%SESSION%', () => session.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi,'<\\\\\\\\/script')).replace('%BUNDLE%', () => js.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi,'<\\\\\\\\/script'));\\nwriteFileSync(OUT, html);\\n```\\nUse function replacers as above, because the engine contains `$` sequences. The bundle is about 5.3 MB before your game is added. Append the licence texts of the engine, three.js and Rapier from the kit's `CREDITS.md` as an HTML comment at the end of the file. esbuild bundles `pixel-engine.js` without you editing it; that satisfies \\\"keep the engine as it ships\\\".\\n\\n`src/main.js` is the only module that starts the engine. Start options:\\n```js\\nconst OPTIONS = withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera:{ preset:'first', fov:62 }, look: LOOK, touch:false, debugKeys:false, settings:{ button:false, key:false } });\\n```\\n`withUrlOptions` keeps the review flags (`?seed=`, `?debug=1`, `?look=`), which are useful and harmless. `debugKeys:false` keeps the engine's R, P and M hotkeys from colliding with yours; dev mode has its own tools.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 24. Testing\\n\\nTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\\n\\n**Suites:**\\n\\n1. **`static` (Node):** in `src/`: no `Math.random`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`. Every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\\n\\n2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\\n   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\\n   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\\n   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\\n   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\\n   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\\n   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\\n   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\\n   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\\n   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\\n   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\\n   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\\n   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\\n\\n3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\\n\\n4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\\n\\n5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\\n   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\\n   Check:\\n   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\\n   - `AIHub.active` false\\n   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\\n   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\\n   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\\n   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\\n\\n6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** copy the built file to `tests/.site/game.html` beside a copy of `vendor/hub/*` (the harness only loads same-origin pages), serve that folder on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=game.html&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\\n   - `players=2&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\\n   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\\n   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\\n\\n7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\\n\\n**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief; runs have no display, so everything here is headless): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 25. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\\n\\n| Step | Do | Done when |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\\n\",\"total_lines\":1128}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-84f17f08-4dfb-4f9a-9b8a-29a7979b885b-85","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"66→## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:39:17\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"66→## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:39:17\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":66,\"limit\":120,\"raw_output\":\"## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:39:17\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-84f17f08-4dfb-4f9a-9b8a-29a7979b885b-86","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\\\"en\\\">\\n<head>\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script src=\\\"vendor/session.js\\\"></script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"shared-world\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"6\\\">\\n10→<meta name=\\\"hub:webgpu\\\" content=\\\"required\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Carry the most diamond cases out of the burning mine\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Stick/WASD move, R2/mouse fire, Square/E use, carry cases to the truck\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\">\\n<title>A Cry Too Far</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n<div id=\\\"app\\\"></div>\\n<script type=\\\"module\\\" src=\\\"src/main.js\\\"></script>\\n</body>\\n20→</html>\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\\\"en\\\">\\n<head>\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script src=\\\"vendor/session.js\\\"></script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"shared-world\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"6\\\">\\n10→<meta name=\\\"hub:webgpu\\\" content=\\\"required\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Carry the most diamond cases out of the burning mine\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Stick/WASD move, R2/mouse fire, Square/E use, carry cases to the truck\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\">\\n<title>A Cry Too Far</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n<div id=\\\"app\\\"></div>\\n<script type=\\\"module\\\" src=\\\"src/main.js\\\"></script>\\n</body>\\n20→</html>\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/index.html\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\\\"en\\\">\\n<head>\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script src=\\\"vendor/session.js\\\"></script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"shared-world\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"6\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:webgpu\\\" content=\\\"required\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Carry the most diamond cases out of the burning mine\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Stick/WASD move, R2/mouse fire, Square/E use, carry cases to the truck\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\">\\n<title>A Cry Too Far</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n<div id=\\\"app\\\"></div>\\n<script type=\\\"module\\\" src=\\\"src/main.js\\\"></script>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\",\"total_lines\":20}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-84f17f08-4dfb-4f9a-9b8a-29a7979b885b-87","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person war-zone survival shooter and hand in every TASK.md artifact that this computer can produce.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Title and hand-in. Fifty playful riffs on \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" are listed and one is chosen with a real random-number generator (not by hand). That exact string is the on-screen title, the HTML `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The folder that contains `STAGE.md` holds (a) one HTML file whose filename stem is `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`, runnable from `file://` with nothing else required beside it, (b) `exit-interview.json` beside that HTML, one object in TASK.md's exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, \\\"unknown\\\" only where a fact cannot be confirmed, and (c) `PROGRESS.md`. All dev files live in a subfolder with that same stem. The shipped `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit zip and is never edited. About/Credits, reachable from the main menu and from pause, shows every TASK.md Source Information field (Date `2026-11-05 01:39:17`, Source `Far Cry 2`, Plan `PLAN.md`, genre, perspective, tech, art, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, Usage `added on import`), then Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback as the brief's questions with answers in that order, then credits for Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, and the words \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\". No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, or `coming soon`, and no half-built feature, remains.\\n2. Never-cut survival systems. On one continuous 1280×1280 m basin the player moves and drives in first person (walk, run, sprint, crouch, jump, wade). Guns wear with use, jam so they cannot fire until repeated reloads clear them, and fall apart at condition 0; locker guns start clean and ground guns start worn. A 5 m fire grid spreads with wind (measured head speed 2–5 m/s when wind is 5 m/s, flanks slower); burnt ground cannot burn again; scars are visible on the map. Blackwater fever is cleared by a held tablet; ignoring it collapses the player and removes stones. Low health can run a field-surgery scene; at 0 HP a living buddy can rescue; a downed buddy offers a syrette or finish-it, and only finish-it when no syrette remains. The paper map and GPS are held in the hands while walking and while driving, and the world does not pause. With clean storage, music volume is 0.1. Voices are code gibberish. Required effects are code sounds that render non-silent. No audio files are used.\\n3. Original story slice. Country, people, factions, weapons' names, and places are invented. None of Far Cry 2's proper nouns, characters, plot, or map appear. Shipped missions run from the opening ride through the reveal that the burns form a firebreak, then a real choice of who receives the minefield sheet, then both ending cards in PLAN.md §14. Story scars are laid even if the player burned something else. The Ledger reports hectares burned, people moved, men paid to kill, friends lost, and stones earned. The player's bullets cannot kill civilians. Each shipped mission can be started from its own setup and finished with real actions; both endings are reached that way, including once with a buddy already dead. Anything left out is a whole item from PLAN.md §26, taken from the top of that list, with its menu entries, map marks, and lines removed. The §26 never-cut list is all present.\\n10→4. 10-foot shell, attract, and dev mode. UI is drawn with the engine HUD in art pixels, not DOM. Prompts follow the last used device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox glyph setting, and keyboard/mouse glyphs. Mouse hover moves menu focus and a click activates; pad, keyboard, and mouse all operate the same menus. Pause sets time scale to 0. A touch-only device shows the exact notice `Controller or keyboard required` and one Settings control, with no on-screen touch controls, and attract is still allowed to start. With no input, attract starts only after 10 seconds on the title or main menu (not on Settings or About), loops without error, shows a demo tag, and any input returns to the screen it came from without writing a save. Pause opens Dev Mode (hidden in a hub session) with the live balance sliders in PLAN.md §17; moving a slider changes that system immediately; Reset restores every default.\\n5. Session round. Cash Run is a round of 1 to 4 players, about 6 minutes: carry stone cases to an extraction truck, friendly fire on, downed players can be revived or looted. The same round runs with no hub as solo play against hired-gun AI, and a normal boot with no session still reaches the title. Boot follows PLAN.md §19: hub connect, a headless server advanced on a timer rather than the render loop, ready signaled with no key press, and the card answering Enter and M from a window keydown listener. All four harness scenarios in PLAN.md §24 pass on the built file. If shared-world cannot be made stable, ship ghost, then parallel, only after that, with session meta tags matching the mode actually shipped, and the four scenarios still pass. Same-computer two-window link is required unless it is the §26 cut.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder run `node tests/run.mjs --all` and capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the summary line reports 0 failed. Suites must call the shipped game (headless engine, real actions, `step` from a fresh start or a mission setup), not a copy of the rules. Required observations: fire head speed in the 2–5 m/s band at 5 m/s wind and slower flanks; a burnt cell does not ignite again; a jam blocks fire and repeated reload clears it; condition 0 empties the slot; a tablet clears fever and ignoring it costs stones; surgery, rescue, and finish-it behave as in criterion 2; the map can be raised while driving and its cursor sets a waypoint; clean-storage music volume is 0.1; every balance slider changes its system and Reset restores every default; attract does not start before the 10-second boundary, does start at that boundary with no input, and exits on input without a save write; a solo round banks a case, drops it on down, revives, and finishes on time-up with every present player once in the standings; a bot finishes every shipped mission through both endings and the ledger/world assertions hold, including a run with one buddy dead; source has no `Math.random` and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub/coming soon; content strings stay inside the HUD font; every shipped sound renders non-silent; `pixel-engine.js` matches the kit zip byte for byte.\\n2. gating: Launch the real built HTML from `file://` twice, in Chromium via Playwright at 1920×1080, with the WebGPU flags in PLAN.md §24. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Each run must show no page or console errors, `AIHub.active` false, and game state whose title string equals the RNG pick. The two runs must agree. If Playwright itself cannot start, write that failure to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; the headless suite in step 1 is then the accepted bar. A screenshot buffer that comes back blank is not proof of failure and not proof of success; the state assertion is the primary observable (this machine's software WebGPU canvas can be blank). Do not invent a stand-in page.\\n3. gating: On Playwright's iPhone 14 profile under Chromium (drop the WebKit device default), open the same file and capture `{SCRATCH}/touch.log`. Pass only if the notice text is exactly `Controller or keyboard required`, a Settings control is shown, a tap opens Settings, and attract is not blocked. A WebKit iPhone load that fails for lack of WebGPU is evidence only; record it in `{SCRATCH}/webkit-iphone.log` and do not treat it as the gate.\\n4. gating: Copy the built HTML beside the hub harness files, serve that directory with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the page title is the chosen game title, then run the four PLAN.md §24 scenarios one at a time. Pass only when each reaches `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`, including any failed `__hubCheck.checks`. Stop only that server, by its PID. If same-computer link was not cut, also drive two `file://` pages through one shared round to matching standings and a shared fire or case, logged in `{SCRATCH}/link.log`. If link was cut, that log must show the feature is absent (no menu entry).\\n5. evidence: After the game has been rendering for at least 10 seconds, save the PLAN.md §24 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm the key shape. Screenshot absence or a blank WebGPU frame does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 hold.\\n\\n20→## Non-goals\\n- A recreation of Far Cry 2: its proper nouns, characters, plot, map, logos, music, and look stay out. Defining mechanics are already rewritten in PLAN.md (fever, jam and break, wind-driven grass fire, handheld map, hired buddies who can die, stones and a tracker, two rotten factions, safehouses, first person on foot and in vehicles).\\n- FPS targets, render optimization, and judging the game by wall-clock frame rate. Software WebGPU here is about 1 fps; tests advance with `step`.\\n- Physical DualSense, a TV, a human listener, a second human, real iOS Safari, prone, ladders, ledge grabs, on-screen touch controls, audio files, chat, emotes, or custom player names.\\n- Editing `pixel-engine.js`, or leaving a cut feature as a stub, menu entry, or map mark.\\n- Civilian targets, child soldiers, and sexual violence.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder root (beside `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, `TASK.md`): the single HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`: unzipped riftlight pixel-engine kit (latest release zip), unmodified AI Hub `session.js` plus the hub harness used only for tests, game sources, art and animation data, generated audio, `node tests/run.mjs`, and the esbuild single-file packager.\\n30→- Engine facts in PLAN.md §3 were verified on 0.46.0. Far Cry 2's defining mechanics were checked against public descriptions of malaria pills and pass-out, weapon jam and breakage, savanna fire driven by wind, diamond cases, buddies, and the in-hand map. PLAN.md already sets the original equivalents; do not research the game in order to copy it.\\n- Runtime: Node, esbuild, Playwright 1.63 Chromium with the host platform override already set. Python Playwright is available. No `playwright install`. One browser at a time.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md §25 in order and §26 when cutting. Keep this as guidance, not a file-layout contract. Separate simulation data from meshes so headless tests never need a GPU. Put missions, weapons, sites, dialogue, tuning defaults, and model defs in plain data. Gameplay reads named actions, not raw keys. Simulate in the fixed step and draw in the frame update. Read tuning values at the moment of use. One game object owns the world; title, attract, campaign, and the round are modes, not a fresh terrain load. Guard every use of `window`, `document`, `navigator`, and `localStorage`. Randomness comes from the engine's seeded generator, forked by name. Build the single HTML the way PLAN.md §23 specifies (session script first, then hub meta tags, then the bundled module) so `file://` boots. Keep the four hub scenarios passing from the first bootable build. Log defects in the dev folder's issues note as tests catch them. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each step so a compacted context can resume.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] List 50 titles, pick one with a real RNG, record the list and the pick, create the dev subfolder, unzip the kit, vendor the hub client and harness, and prove `pixel-engine.js` matches the zip.\\n- [ ] Scaffold the single-file build and a booting game so `file://` opens and all four hub scenarios pass. Start `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- [ ] Add the basin, player locomotion, look and pointer-lock fixes, actions, device glyphs, minimal HUD, and pause. Cover them with headless tests.\\n40→- [ ] Add weapons (wear, jam, break), hits, soldiers, health, syrette, surgery, and fever, with the system tests in criterion 2.\\n- [ ] Add the fire grid, ignition weapons, day and night, vehicles and repair, the held map, safehouses, stones, and save/load.\\n- [ ] Add missions through both endings, buddies (rescue and finish-it), the Ledger, dialogue, music at 0.1, and code voices and effects. Add the bot playthrough.\\n- [ ] Add title, menus, settings, the touch gate, attract, dev-mode sliders with reset, and About/Credits.\\n- [ ] Finish Cash Run (host, client, solo bots, card listeners). Re-run `node tests/run.mjs --all`, the two `file://` launches, the phone-profile check, and the four hub scenarios. Write `exit-interview.json` and finish `PROGRESS.md`, including any §26 cuts.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §27 demands the full game and zero failing tests; §26 allows cutting whole features from the top of its list if time runs out. A cut that leaves a stub, menu entry, or map mark fails. Never-cut systems stay even if that forces a cut higher on the list.\\n- TASK.md allows downloaded CDN dependencies \\\"if that is easier\\\". PLAN.md §23 and §27 require one HTML file that runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it. Ship the single file. Do not rely on a CDN.\\n- The HTML stem contains spaces because SOURCE is `Far Cry 2`. Keep that stem exact.\\n50→- There is no display. Headless Chromium with the §24 flags is the browser. A blank WebGPU screenshot with correct game state is a known limit of this machine, not a failed launch. A state read that is empty or wrong is a game defect.\\n- The kit may be newer than 0.46.0. Read `CHANGELOG.md` after 0.46.0 before trusting §3. Do not edit the engine to paper over a newer API.\\n- Esc under pointer lock may never arrive as a key, and `requestPointerLock()` can reject. Follow the §3 handling or the hub check can fail on an unhandled rejection.\\n- earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory is exhausted. One Chromium at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n- WebKit on an iPhone profile cannot run WebGPU. The touch gate is judged on Chromium's phone profile. That is the closest stand-in, not iOS Safari.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person war-zone survival shooter and hand in every TASK.md artifact that this computer can produce.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Title and hand-in. Fifty playful riffs on \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" are listed and one is chosen with a real random-number generator (not by hand). That exact string is the on-screen title, the HTML `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The folder that contains `STAGE.md` holds (a) one HTML file whose filename stem is `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`, runnable from `file://` with nothing else required beside it, (b) `exit-interview.json` beside that HTML, one object in TASK.md's exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, \\\"unknown\\\" only where a fact cannot be confirmed, and (c) `PROGRESS.md`. All dev files live in a subfolder with that same stem. The shipped `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit zip and is never edited. About/Credits, reachable from the main menu and from pause, shows every TASK.md Source Information field (Date `2026-11-05 01:39:17`, Source `Far Cry 2`, Plan `PLAN.md`, genre, perspective, tech, art, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, Usage `added on import`), then Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback as the brief's questions with answers in that order, then credits for Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, and the words \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\". No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, or `coming soon`, and no half-built feature, remains.\\n2. Never-cut survival systems. On one continuous 1280×1280 m basin the player moves and drives in first person (walk, run, sprint, crouch, jump, wade). Guns wear with use, jam so they cannot fire until repeated reloads clear them, and fall apart at condition 0; locker guns start clean and ground guns start worn. A 5 m fire grid spreads with wind (measured head speed 2–5 m/s when wind is 5 m/s, flanks slower); burnt ground cannot burn again; scars are visible on the map. Blackwater fever is cleared by a held tablet; ignoring it collapses the player and removes stones. Low health can run a field-surgery scene; at 0 HP a living buddy can rescue; a downed buddy offers a syrette or finish-it, and only finish-it when no syrette remains. The paper map and GPS are held in the hands while walking and while driving, and the world does not pause. With clean storage, music volume is 0.1. Voices are code gibberish. Required effects are code sounds that render non-silent. No audio files are used.\\n3. Original story slice. Country, people, factions, weapons' names, and places are invented. None of Far Cry 2's proper nouns, characters, plot, or map appear. Shipped missions run from the opening ride through the reveal that the burns form a firebreak, then a real choice of who receives the minefield sheet, then both ending cards in PLAN.md §14. Story scars are laid even if the player burned something else. The Ledger reports hectares burned, people moved, men paid to kill, friends lost, and stones earned. The player's bullets cannot kill civilians. Each shipped mission can be started from its own setup and finished with real actions; both endings are reached that way, including once with a buddy already dead. Anything left out is a whole item from PLAN.md §26, taken from the top of that list, with its menu entries, map marks, and lines removed. The §26 never-cut list is all present.\\n10→4. 10-foot shell, attract, and dev mode. UI is drawn with the engine HUD in art pixels, not DOM. Prompts follow the last used device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox glyph setting, and keyboard/mouse glyphs. Mouse hover moves menu focus and a click activates; pad, keyboard, and mouse all operate the same menus. Pause sets time scale to 0. A touch-only device shows the exact notice `Controller or keyboard required` and one Settings control, with no on-screen touch controls, and attract is still allowed to start. With no input, attract starts only after 10 seconds on the title or main menu (not on Settings or About), loops without error, shows a demo tag, and any input returns to the screen it came from without writing a save. Pause opens Dev Mode (hidden in a hub session) with the live balance sliders in PLAN.md §17; moving a slider changes that system immediately; Reset restores every default.\\n5. Session round. Cash Run is a round of 1 to 4 players, about 6 minutes: carry stone cases to an extraction truck, friendly fire on, downed players can be revived or looted. The same round runs with no hub as solo play against hired-gun AI, and a normal boot with no session still reaches the title. Boot follows PLAN.md §19: hub connect, a headless server advanced on a timer rather than the render loop, ready signaled with no key press, and the card answering Enter and M from a window keydown listener. All four harness scenarios in PLAN.md §24 pass on the built file. If shared-world cannot be made stable, ship ghost, then parallel, only after that, with session meta tags matching the mode actually shipped, and the four scenarios still pass. Same-computer two-window link is required unless it is the §26 cut.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder run `node tests/run.mjs --all` and capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the summary line reports 0 failed. Suites must call the shipped game (headless engine, real actions, `step` from a fresh start or a mission setup), not a copy of the rules. Required observations: fire head speed in the 2–5 m/s band at 5 m/s wind and slower flanks; a burnt cell does not ignite again; a jam blocks fire and repeated reload clears it; condition 0 empties the slot; a tablet clears fever and ignoring it costs stones; surgery, rescue, and finish-it behave as in criterion 2; the map can be raised while driving and its cursor sets a waypoint; clean-storage music volume is 0.1; every balance slider changes its system and Reset restores every default; attract does not start before the 10-second boundary, does start at that boundary with no input, and exits on input without a save write; a solo round banks a case, drops it on down, revives, and finishes on time-up with every present player once in the standings; a bot finishes every shipped mission through both endings and the ledger/world assertions hold, including a run with one buddy dead; source has no `Math.random` and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub/coming soon; content strings stay inside the HUD font; every shipped sound renders non-silent; `pixel-engine.js` matches the kit zip byte for byte.\\n2. gating: Launch the real built HTML from `file://` twice, in Chromium via Playwright at 1920×1080, with the WebGPU flags in PLAN.md §24. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Each run must show no page or console errors, `AIHub.active` false, and game state whose title string equals the RNG pick. The two runs must agree. If Playwright itself cannot start, write that failure to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; the headless suite in step 1 is then the accepted bar. A screenshot buffer that comes back blank is not proof of failure and not proof of success; the state assertion is the primary observable (this machine's software WebGPU canvas can be blank). Do not invent a stand-in page.\\n3. gating: On Playwright's iPhone 14 profile under Chromium (drop the WebKit device default), open the same file and capture `{SCRATCH}/touch.log`. Pass only if the notice text is exactly `Controller or keyboard required`, a Settings control is shown, a tap opens Settings, and attract is not blocked. A WebKit iPhone load that fails for lack of WebGPU is evidence only; record it in `{SCRATCH}/webkit-iphone.log` and do not treat it as the gate.\\n4. gating: Copy the built HTML beside the hub harness files, serve that directory with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the page title is the chosen game title, then run the four PLAN.md §24 scenarios one at a time. Pass only when each reaches `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`, including any failed `__hubCheck.checks`. Stop only that server, by its PID. If same-computer link was not cut, also drive two `file://` pages through one shared round to matching standings and a shared fire or case, logged in `{SCRATCH}/link.log`. If link was cut, that log must show the feature is absent (no menu entry).\\n5. evidence: After the game has been rendering for at least 10 seconds, save the PLAN.md §24 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm the key shape. Screenshot absence or a blank WebGPU frame does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 hold.\\n\\n20→## Non-goals\\n- A recreation of Far Cry 2: its proper nouns, characters, plot, map, logos, music, and look stay out. Defining mechanics are already rewritten in PLAN.md (fever, jam and break, wind-driven grass fire, handheld map, hired buddies who can die, stones and a tracker, two rotten factions, safehouses, first person on foot and in vehicles).\\n- FPS targets, render optimization, and judging the game by wall-clock frame rate. Software WebGPU here is about 1 fps; tests advance with `step`.\\n- Physical DualSense, a TV, a human listener, a second human, real iOS Safari, prone, ladders, ledge grabs, on-screen touch controls, audio files, chat, emotes, or custom player names.\\n- Editing `pixel-engine.js`, or leaving a cut feature as a stub, menu entry, or map mark.\\n- Civilian targets, child soldiers, and sexual violence.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder root (beside `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, `TASK.md`): the single HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`: unzipped riftlight pixel-engine kit (latest release zip), unmodified AI Hub `session.js` plus the hub harness used only for tests, game sources, art and animation data, generated audio, `node tests/run.mjs`, and the esbuild single-file packager.\\n30→- Engine facts in PLAN.md §3 were verified on 0.46.0. Far Cry 2's defining mechanics were checked against public descriptions of malaria pills and pass-out, weapon jam and breakage, savanna fire driven by wind, diamond cases, buddies, and the in-hand map. PLAN.md already sets the original equivalents; do not research the game in order to copy it.\\n- Runtime: Node, esbuild, Playwright 1.63 Chromium with the host platform override already set. Python Playwright is available. No `playwright install`. One browser at a time.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md §25 in order and §26 when cutting. Keep this as guidance, not a file-layout contract. Separate simulation data from meshes so headless tests never need a GPU. Put missions, weapons, sites, dialogue, tuning defaults, and model defs in plain data. Gameplay reads named actions, not raw keys. Simulate in the fixed step and draw in the frame update. Read tuning values at the moment of use. One game object owns the world; title, attract, campaign, and the round are modes, not a fresh terrain load. Guard every use of `window`, `document`, `navigator`, and `localStorage`. Randomness comes from the engine's seeded generator, forked by name. Build the single HTML the way PLAN.md §23 specifies (session script first, then hub meta tags, then the bundled module) so `file://` boots. Keep the four hub scenarios passing from the first bootable build. Log defects in the dev folder's issues note as tests catch them. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each step so a compacted context can resume.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] List 50 titles, pick one with a real RNG, record the list and the pick, create the dev subfolder, unzip the kit, vendor the hub client and harness, and prove `pixel-engine.js` matches the zip.\\n- [ ] Scaffold the single-file build and a booting game so `file://` opens and all four hub scenarios pass. Start `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- [ ] Add the basin, player locomotion, look and pointer-lock fixes, actions, device glyphs, minimal HUD, and pause. Cover them with headless tests.\\n40→- [ ] Add weapons (wear, jam, break), hits, soldiers, health, syrette, surgery, and fever, with the system tests in criterion 2.\\n- [ ] Add the fire grid, ignition weapons, day and night, vehicles and repair, the held map, safehouses, stones, and save/load.\\n- [ ] Add missions through both endings, buddies (rescue and finish-it), the Ledger, dialogue, music at 0.1, and code voices and effects. Add the bot playthrough.\\n- [ ] Add title, menus, settings, the touch gate, attract, dev-mode sliders with reset, and About/Credits.\\n- [ ] Finish Cash Run (host, client, solo bots, card listeners). Re-run `node tests/run.mjs --all`, the two `file://` launches, the phone-profile check, and the four hub scenarios. Write `exit-interview.json` and finish `PROGRESS.md`, including any §26 cuts.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §27 demands the full game and zero failing tests; §26 allows cutting whole features from the top of its list if time runs out. A cut that leaves a stub, menu entry, or map mark fails. Never-cut systems stay even if that forces a cut higher on the list.\\n- TASK.md allows downloaded CDN dependencies \\\"if that is easier\\\". PLAN.md §23 and §27 require one HTML file that runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it. Ship the single file. Do not rely on a CDN.\\n- The HTML stem contains spaces because SOURCE is `Far Cry 2`. Keep that stem exact.\\n50→- There is no display. Headless Chromium with the §24 flags is the browser. A blank WebGPU screenshot with correct game state is a known limit of this machine, not a failed launch. A state read that is empty or wrong is a game defect.\\n- The kit may be newer than 0.46.0. Read `CHANGELOG.md` after 0.46.0 before trusting §3. Do not edit the engine to paper over a newer API.\\n- Esc under pointer lock may never arrive as a key, and `requestPointerLock()` can reject. Follow the §3 handling or the hub check can fail on an unhandled rejection.\\n- earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory is exhausted. One Chromium at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n- WebKit on an iPhone profile cannot run WebGPU. The touch gate is judged on Chromium's phone profile. That is the closest stand-in, not iOS Safari.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c%2Fwork/01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person war-zone survival shooter and hand in every TASK.md artifact that this computer can produce.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Title and hand-in. Fifty playful riffs on \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" are listed and one is chosen with a real random-number generator (not by hand). That exact string is the on-screen title, the HTML `<title>`, and `aiHub.name`. The folder that contains `STAGE.md` holds (a) one HTML file whose filename stem is `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`, runnable from `file://` with nothing else required beside it, (b) `exit-interview.json` beside that HTML, one object in TASK.md's exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, \\\"unknown\\\" only where a fact cannot be confirmed, and (c) `PROGRESS.md`. All dev files live in a subfolder with that same stem. The shipped `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit zip and is never edited. About/Credits, reachable from the main menu and from pause, shows every TASK.md Source Information field (Date `2026-11-05 01:39:17`, Source `Far Cry 2`, Plan `PLAN.md`, genre, perspective, tech, art, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, Usage `added on import`), then Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback as the brief's questions with answers in that order, then credits for Pixel Engine, three.js, Rapier, the AI Hub client, and the words \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\". No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, or `coming soon`, and no half-built feature, remains.\\n2. Never-cut survival systems. On one continuous 1280×1280 m basin the player moves and drives in first person (walk, run, sprint, crouch, jump, wade). Guns wear with use, jam so they cannot fire until repeated reloads clear them, and fall apart at condition 0; locker guns start clean and ground guns start worn. A 5 m fire grid spreads with wind (measured head speed 2–5 m/s when wind is 5 m/s, flanks slower); burnt ground cannot burn again; scars are visible on the map. Blackwater fever is cleared by a held tablet; ignoring it collapses the player and removes stones. Low health can run a field-surgery scene; at 0 HP a living buddy can rescue; a downed buddy offers a syrette or finish-it, and only finish-it when no syrette remains. The paper map and GPS are held in the hands while walking and while driving, and the world does not pause. With clean storage, music volume is 0.1. Voices are code gibberish. Required effects are code sounds that render non-silent. No audio files are used.\\n3. Original story slice. Country, people, factions, weapons' names, and places are invented. None of Far Cry 2's proper nouns, characters, plot, or map appear. Shipped missions run from the opening ride through the reveal that the burns form a firebreak, then a real choice of who receives the minefield sheet, then both ending cards in PLAN.md §14. Story scars are laid even if the player burned something else. The Ledger reports hectares burned, people moved, men paid to kill, friends lost, and stones earned. The player's bullets cannot kill civilians. Each shipped mission can be started from its own setup and finished with real actions; both endings are reached that way, including once with a buddy already dead. Anything left out is a whole item from PLAN.md §26, taken from the top of that list, with its menu entries, map marks, and lines removed. The §26 never-cut list is all present.\\n4. 10-foot shell, attract, and dev mode. UI is drawn with the engine HUD in art pixels, not DOM. Prompts follow the last used device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox glyph setting, and keyboard/mouse glyphs. Mouse hover moves menu focus and a click activates; pad, keyboard, and mouse all operate the same menus. Pause sets time scale to 0. A touch-only device shows the exact notice `Controller or keyboard required` and one Settings control, with no on-screen touch controls, and attract is still allowed to start. With no input, attract starts only after 10 seconds on the title or main menu (not on Settings or About), loops without error, shows a demo tag, and any input returns to the screen it came from without writing a save. Pause opens Dev Mode (hidden in a hub session) with the live balance sliders in PLAN.md §17; moving a slider changes that system immediately; Reset restores every default.\\n5. Session round. Cash Run is a round of 1 to 4 players, about 6 minutes: carry stone cases to an extraction truck, friendly fire on, downed players can be revived or looted. The same round runs with no hub as solo play against hired-gun AI, and a normal boot with no session still reaches the title. Boot follows PLAN.md §19: hub connect, a headless server advanced on a timer rather than the render loop, ready signaled with no key press, and the card answering Enter and M from a window keydown listener. All four harness scenarios in PLAN.md §24 pass on the built file. If shared-world cannot be made stable, ship ghost, then parallel, only after that, with session meta tags matching the mode actually shipped, and the four scenarios still pass. Same-computer two-window link is required unless it is the §26 cut.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder run `node tests/run.mjs --all` and capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the summary line reports 0 failed. Suites must call the shipped game (headless engine, real actions, `step` from a fresh start or a mission setup), not a copy of the rules. Required observations: fire head speed in the 2–5 m/s band at 5 m/s wind and slower flanks; a burnt cell does not ignite again; a jam blocks fire and repeated reload clears it; condition 0 empties the slot; a tablet clears fever and ignoring it costs stones; surgery, rescue, and finish-it behave as in criterion 2; the map can be raised while driving and its cursor sets a waypoint; clean-storage music volume is 0.1; every balance slider changes its system and Reset restores every default; attract does not start before the 10-second boundary, does start at that boundary with no input, and exits on input without a save write; a solo round banks a case, drops it on down, revives, and finishes on time-up with every present player once in the standings; a bot finishes every shipped mission through both endings and the ledger/world assertions hold, including a run with one buddy dead; source has no `Math.random` and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub/coming soon; content strings stay inside the HUD font; every shipped sound renders non-silent; `pixel-engine.js` matches the kit zip byte for byte.\\n2. gating: Launch the real built HTML from `file://` twice, in Chromium via Playwright at 1920×1080, with the WebGPU flags in PLAN.md §24. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Each run must show no page or console errors, `AIHub.active` false, and game state whose title string equals the RNG pick. The two runs must agree. If Playwright itself cannot start, write that failure to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; the headless suite in step 1 is then the accepted bar. A screenshot buffer that comes back blank is not proof of failure and not proof of success; the state assertion is the primary observable (this machine's software WebGPU canvas can be blank). Do not invent a stand-in page.\\n3. gating: On Playwright's iPhone 14 profile under Chromium (drop the WebKit device default), open the same file and capture `{SCRATCH}/touch.log`. Pass only if the notice text is exactly `Controller or keyboard required`, a Settings control is shown, a tap opens Settings, and attract is not blocked. A WebKit iPhone load that fails for lack of WebGPU is evidence only; record it in `{SCRATCH}/webkit-iphone.log` and do not treat it as the gate.\\n4. gating: Copy the built HTML beside the hub harness files, serve that directory with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the page title is the chosen game title, then run the four PLAN.md §24 scenarios one at a time. Pass only when each reaches `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`, including any failed `__hubCheck.checks`. Stop only that server, by its PID. If same-computer link was not cut, also drive two `file://` pages through one shared round to matching standings and a shared fire or case, logged in `{SCRATCH}/link.log`. If link was cut, that log must show the feature is absent (no menu entry).\\n5. evidence: After the game has been rendering for at least 10 seconds, save the PLAN.md §24 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm the key shape. Screenshot absence or a blank WebGPU frame does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 hold.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- A recreation of Far Cry 2: its proper nouns, characters, plot, map, logos, music, and look stay out. Defining mechanics are already rewritten in PLAN.md (fever, jam and break, wind-driven grass fire, handheld map, hired buddies who can die, stones and a tracker, two rotten factions, safehouses, first person on foot and in vehicles).\\n- FPS targets, render optimization, and judging the game by wall-clock frame rate. Software WebGPU here is about 1 fps; tests advance with `step`.\\n- Physical DualSense, a TV, a human listener, a second human, real iOS Safari, prone, ladders, ledge grabs, on-screen touch controls, audio files, chat, emotes, or custom player names.\\n- Editing `pixel-engine.js`, or leaving a cut feature as a stub, menu entry, or map mark.\\n- Civilian targets, child soldiers, and sexual violence.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder root (beside `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, `TASK.md`): the single HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917`: unzipped riftlight pixel-engine kit (latest release zip), unmodified AI Hub `session.js` plus the hub harness used only for tests, game sources, art and animation data, generated audio, `node tests/run.mjs`, and the esbuild single-file packager.\\n- Engine facts in PLAN.md §3 were verified on 0.46.0. Far Cry 2's defining mechanics were checked against public descriptions of malaria pills and pass-out, weapon jam and breakage, savanna fire driven by wind, diamond cases, buddies, and the in-hand map. PLAN.md already sets the original equivalents; do not research the game in order to copy it.\\n- Runtime: Node, esbuild, Playwright 1.63 Chromium with the host platform override already set. Python Playwright is available. No `playwright install`. One browser at a time.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md §25 in order and §26 when cutting. Keep this as guidance, not a file-layout contract. Separate simulation data from meshes so headless tests never need a GPU. Put missions, weapons, sites, dialogue, tuning defaults, and model defs in plain data. Gameplay reads named actions, not raw keys. Simulate in the fixed step and draw in the frame update. Read tuning values at the moment of use. One game object owns the world; title, attract, campaign, and the round are modes, not a fresh terrain load. Guard every use of `window`, `document`, `navigator`, and `localStorage`. Randomness comes from the engine's seeded generator, forked by name. Build the single HTML the way PLAN.md §23 specifies (session script first, then hub meta tags, then the bundled module) so `file://` boots. Keep the four hub scenarios passing from the first bootable build. Log defects in the dev folder's issues note as tests catch them. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each step so a compacted context can resume.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] List 50 titles, pick one with a real RNG, record the list and the pick, create the dev subfolder, unzip the kit, vendor the hub client and harness, and prove `pixel-engine.js` matches the zip.\\n- [ ] Scaffold the single-file build and a booting game so `file://` opens and all four hub scenarios pass. Start `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- [ ] Add the basin, player locomotion, look and pointer-lock fixes, actions, device glyphs, minimal HUD, and pause. Cover them with headless tests.\\n- [ ] Add weapons (wear, jam, break), hits, soldiers, health, syrette, surgery, and fever, with the system tests in criterion 2.\\n- [ ] Add the fire grid, ignition weapons, day and night, vehicles and repair, the held map, safehouses, stones, and save/load.\\n- [ ] Add missions through both endings, buddies (rescue and finish-it), the Ledger, dialogue, music at 0.1, and code voices and effects. Add the bot playthrough.\\n- [ ] Add title, menus, settings, the touch gate, attract, dev-mode sliders with reset, and About/Credits.\\n- [ ] Finish Cash Run (host, client, solo bots, card listeners). Re-run `node tests/run.mjs --all`, the two `file://` launches, the phone-profile check, and the four hub scenarios. Write `exit-interview.json` and finish `PROGRESS.md`, including any §26 cuts.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §27 demands the full game and zero failing tests; §26 allows cutting whole features from the top of its list if time runs out. A cut that leaves a stub, menu entry, or map mark fails. Never-cut systems stay even if that forces a cut higher on the list.\\n- TASK.md allows downloaded CDN dependencies \\\"if that is easier\\\". PLAN.md §23 and §27 require one HTML file that runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it. Ship the single file. Do not rely on a CDN.\\n- The HTML stem contains spaces because SOURCE is `Far Cry 2`. Keep that stem exact.\\n- There is no display. Headless Chromium with the §24 flags is the browser. A blank WebGPU screenshot with correct game state is a known limit of this machine, not a failed launch. A state read that is empty or wrong is a game defect.\\n- The kit may be newer than 0.46.0. Read `CHANGELOG.md` after 0.46.0 before trusting §3. Do not edit the engine to paper over a newer API.\\n- Esc under pointer lock may never arrive as a key, and `requestPointerLock()` can reject. Follow the §3 handling or the hub check can fail on an unhandled rejection.\\n- earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory is exhausted. One Chromium at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n- WebKit on an iPhone profile cannot run WebGPU. The touch gate is judged on Chromium's phone profile. That is the closest stand-in, not iOS Safari.\\n\",\"total_lines\":54}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"6c84d82f-5eb0-4fd5-8f91-b75e2c65e78e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_328","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The first step is writing tools/build.\n\nThe remaining steps are checking main.js against OPTIONS, verifying CREDITS.md, filling about.js with real feedback, building HTML, and running verification tests. I also need to write exit-interview.json, PROGRESS.md, and tests/ISSUES.md, flip the goal plan checklist, and delete examples/ and packs/ if nothing imports them. I should also review the hub harness location and main.js boot.\n\nI will compile the real issue count from defects actually caught during testing and prior sessions, since the suite is now green. I am also checking existing files for any prior issues.\n\nI am cataloging the real defects caught during testing. These include burn objectives on unburnable terrain, hidden payroll targeting, random jam clearing jamScript, and a touch gate click registering as a mouse control. I also noted attract state leaks, leftover test soldiers killing the player, and the title screen failing to start attract mode.\n\nI am adding more real defects to the issue count. These include CashRound reviving the player after death, title screen simulated soldier fire killing the player and freezing attract mode, song pattern step counts causing parseSong throws, driving story bot camera yaw, goTo keeping the player walking, pause snapshot issues, m1 feverIn, hub round auto-finish at 360, giveGun replacing the same slot, choicePick, rescueT negative assertions, and convoy payroll offset.\n\nI will check tests/ISSUES.md and about.js to ensure the feedback text matches. The verification contract is `node tests/run.mjs --all` exiting zero. One test file covering all observations satisfies this. I do not need to split suites into five separate files.\n\nI am checking whether save/load is actually implemented and tested. The acceptance criteria include safehouses, stones, and save/load, but the PLAN suite may not cover all of these items.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f82e0dcd-85da-4448-889b-e599f1fd98fe-88","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":1060,"limit":50}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f82e0dcd-85da-4448-889b-e599f1fd98fe-89","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/main.js","limit":120}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f82e0dcd-85da-4448-889b-e599f1fd98fe-90","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/content/about.js","limit":200}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f82e0dcd-85da-4448-889b-e599f1fd98fe-91","name":"list_dir","input":{"target_directory":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/vendor"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7782,"output_tokens":1176,"cache_read_input_tokens":119936,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"e276b12c-0a2f-4650-90d8-ba2601eadcec"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-f82e0dcd-85da-4448-889b-e599f1fd98fe-88","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1060→## 26. Cut order\\n\\nIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\\n\\n1. Fauna (vultures first).\\n2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\\n3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\\n4. Chase camera; dust weather.\\n5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\\n6. M5 (Mags offers her alternate plan in M6 instead: get the field workers out first, for less pay).\\n1070→7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\\n8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\\n9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\\n\\nNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 27. Done, reports, coverage\\n\\n1080→**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\\n- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\\n- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\\n- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\\n- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\\n- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\\n- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the copy in the kit's zip (keep the zip in `vendor/` and compare in the `static` suite).\\n1090→\\n**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\\n- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\\n- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \\\"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\\\".\\n\\n**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\\n\\n**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\\n\\n1100→**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\\n\\n| Brief item | Section |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\\n| Genre, perspective | 1 |\\n| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\\n| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\\n| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\\n| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\",\"content_concise\":\"1060→## 26. Cut order\\n\\nIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Do