{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_540","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The beige rectangle in the map shot is likely the held map plane viewed edge-on from inside the tilted truck camera.\n\nThe map sheet is a blank beige wall rather than a readable map. The texture appears to be failing to sample, so only the base material color shows through.\n\nThe beige color is likely the sand base texture itself. The dark scar pixels and player dot are probably too small to see at this distance, which matches the description of a low-resolution map without UI chrome. The plane is positioned as a left-side wall rather than being held in the hands.\n\nThe map plane is too close and rotated, so it fills the left side as a wall instead of sitting in the hands. I will reposition it in the lower center as a face-on paper sheet so the sand texture and scars are readable.\n\nThe fight shot still shows the beige map panel even though map.anim was set to zero and syncViews should have hidden it. The sheet may linger because readNeeds continues decrementing anim while map.up is false, or because the previous frame rendered before the visibility update took effect.\n\nThe beige panel is likely the clinic building, not the map. It sits beside the camera near the clinic block at 12, 55. The fight shot shows the same block with the soldier to its right.\n\nThe held map may be completely absent from the frame. It could be too small, clipped, backface culled, or behind the camera. The toon material might also fail to render the texture in this pipeline.\n\nThe map plane is at z=-0.42, well past the default near plane of 0.1, so clipping is not the issue. I am now calculating whether the -1.05 rad X rotation places the sheet behind the camera or leaves it edge-on.\n\nThe rotation math confirms the plane is not behind the camera. At 0.4 meters away it spans roughly 64 degrees of field of view, so it should dominate the lower half of the screen. Its absence from the frame is therefore unexpected.\n\nThe map description explicitly places the panel on the left third, confirming it is the clinic building rather than a held sheet. The map is not obvious in that image, so its absence from the frame is now the main puzzle. I am now considering whether backface culling, a render camera transform mismatch after look system copying, or frustum culling due to scale is hiding it.\n\nThe map sheet is likely invisible because it is parented to the camera, but the camera itself is not in the scene graph. Three.js only renders camera children when the camera is added to the scene. This explains why the held map never appears in the frame.\n\nThe fix is to parent the map sheet to a scene-level holder object instead of the camera. The rig will copy the camera's world matrix each frame so the sheet stays in front of the view.\n\nThe rig will copy the camera's world matrix directly and disable auto-update. Because the rig is a scene child, its local transform becomes the world transform.\n\nI will set the rig's position and quaternion from the camera world matrix instead of copying the full matrix. This avoids manual world updates and keeps auto-update working. The sheet local offset stays at (0.02, -0.12, -0.42) relative to the rig. I also need to confirm the camera is not a scene child.\n\nThe GPS marker is a child of the sheet, so it will move with the rig. The test must be updated to assert the sheet is in the scene and follows the camera rather than being parented directly to the camera. Adding the camera to the scene is another possible fix.\n\nAdding the camera to the scene would risk double-transforms or engine breakage, so copying its world matrix onto a scene-level rig is the safer fix. The updated test will verify the sheet lives under a scene child and confirm that rig follows the camera by comparing their world positions.\n\nThe test will compare the rig's world position directly to the camera's world position, avoiding a THREE import. The assertion threshold is 0.05 meters. Walking and driving both update the camera pose, so the rig should stay aligned after each step.\n\nThe `poseMap` implementation is being written. It creates a scene-level `held-map-rig` group, adds it to the scene if missing, parents the map sheet to that rig, and copies the camera's world position into the rig's local position so the sheet follows the view.\n\nThe rig will also copy the camera's world quaternion and call `updateMatrixWorld(true)` so its local transform becomes the world transform. `mountMap` must stop parenting the sheet to the camera and instead leave it for `poseMap` to attach to the rig. In headless mode the camera matrix may be zero before the first pose, but the test steps many frames so the camera should already be at the player.\n\nThe camera pose is copied before the engine updates it, so the rig lags one frame behind. That lag is acceptable for a held map, and the test only checks parent identity rather than position alignment.\n\nThe camera matrix will be identity on the first frame, so the rig starts at the origin before the engine updates it. The toon material and DataTexture should render correctly because the texture is updated every poseMap call and flipY is set to true.\n\nThe sheet and GPS marker will be set to double-sided so the back face is visible. Because the toon material may be cached, directly changing its side property could affect other users of that material. Cloning the material first is the safer approach.\n\nThe material side will be set directly after creation rather than cloned, since the texture is unique to this level. The map plane sits at 0.4 meters, which could fall behind a near plane of 1.0 and get clipped.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-169d36dd-7a5d-4153-80f5-69610e89d650-122","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"near:","glob":"**/API.md","head_limit":15}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-169d36dd-7a5d-4153-80f5-69610e89d650-123","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/core/look.js","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5623,"output_tokens":3854,"cache_read_input_tokens":155264,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"903c8bd6-0da2-4fe5-9a90-aed3f6eee54d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-169d36dd-7a5d-4153-80f5-69610e89d650-122","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,57,49,55,95,95,120,48,55,51,57,51,99,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-169d36dd-7a5d-4153-80f5-69610e89d650-123","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/** First-person look. Re-install after every setCamera while the preset is first. */\\nlet wantLock = false;\\n\\nexport function setWantLock(v) {\\n  wantLock = !!v;\\n}\\n\\nexport function installLook(ctx, cam) {\\n  ctx.camera.step = function (u) {\\n10→    if (cam.override) {\\n      this.camera.position.set(cam.override.position[0], cam.override.position[1], cam.override.position[2]);\\n      this.camera.lookAt(cam.override.target[0], cam.override.target[1], cam.override.target[2]);\\n      this.focus.copy(this.camera.position);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;\\n    const mx = u.input.mouseDelta.x;\\n    const my = u.input.mouseDelta.y;\\n    this.yaw -= mx * 0.0025 * cam.sensX * k;\\n20→    const inv = cam.invertY ? -1 : 1;\\n    this.pitch = Math.max(-1.45, Math.min(1.45, this.pitch - my * 0.0025 * cam.sensY * inv * k));\\n    this.focus.copy(u.target);\\n    this.camera.position.copy(u.eye);\\n    this.camera.rotation.set(this.pitch, this.yaw, 0);\\n  };\\n  ctx.input.gamepadButtons = {\\n    0: 'PadCross', 1: 'PadCircle', 2: 'PadSquare', 3: 'PadTriangle',\\n    4: 'PadL1', 5: 'PadR1', 6: 'PadL2', 7: 'PadR2',\\n    8: 'PadCreate', 9: 'PadOptions', 10: 'PadL3', 11: 'PadR3',\\n30→    12: 'PadUp', 13: 'PadDown', 14: 'PadLeft', 15: 'PadRight',\\n  };\\n  Object.defineProperty(ctx.input, 'wantsPointerLock', {\\n    get: () => wantLock,\\n    set() {},\\n    configurable: true,\\n  });\\n}\\n\\nexport const LOOK = {\\n40→  scene: [\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/** First-person look. Re-install after every setCamera while the preset is first. */\\nlet wantLock = false;\\n\\nexport function setWantLock(v) {\\n  wantLock = !!v;\\n}\\n\\nexport function installLook(ctx, cam) {\\n  ctx.camera.step = function (u) {\\n10→    if (cam.override) {\\n      this.camera.position.set(cam.override.position[0], cam.override.position[1], cam.override.position[2]);\\n      this.camera.lookAt(cam.override.target[0], cam.override.target[1], cam.override.target[2]);\\n      this.focus.copy(this.camera.position);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;\\n    const mx = u.input.mouseDelta.x;\\n    const my = u.input.mouseDelta.y;\\n    this.yaw -= mx * 0.0025 * cam.sensX * k;\\n20→    const inv = cam.invertY ? -1 : 1;\\n    this.pitch = Math.max(-1.45, Math.min(1.45, this.pitch - my * 0.0025 * cam.sensY * inv * k));\\n    this.focus.copy(u.target);\\n    this.camera.position.copy(u.eye);\\n    this.camera.rotation.set(this.pitch, this.yaw, 0);\\n  };\\n  ctx.input.gamepadButtons = {\\n    0: 'PadCross', 1: 'PadCircle', 2: 'PadSquare', 3: 'PadTriangle',\\n    4: 'PadL1', 5: 'PadR1', 6: 'PadL2', 7: 'PadR2',\\n    8: 'PadCreate', 9: 'PadOptions', 10: 'PadL3', 11: 'PadR3',\\n30→    12: 'PadUp', 13: 'PadDown', 14: 'PadLeft', 15: 'PadRight',\\n  };\\n  Object.defineProperty(ctx.input, 'wantsPointerLock', {\\n    get: () => wantLock,\\n    set() {},\\n    configurable: true,\\n  });\\n}\\n\\nexport const LOOK = {\\n40→  scene: [\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/src/core/look.js\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"/** First-person look. Re-install after every setCamera while the preset is first. */\\nlet wantLock = false;\\n\\nexport function setWantLock(v) {\\n  wantLock = !!v;\\n}\\n\\nexport function installLook(ctx, cam) {\\n  ctx.camera.step = function (u) {\\n    if (cam.override) {\\n      this.camera.position.set(cam.override.position[0], cam.override.position[1], cam.override.position[2]);\\n      this.camera.lookAt(cam.override.target[0], cam.override.target[1], cam.override.target[2]);\\n      this.focus.copy(this.camera.position);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;\\n    const mx = u.input.mouseDelta.x;\\n    const my = u.input.mouseDelta.y;\\n    this.yaw -= mx * 0.0025 * cam.sensX * k;\\n    const inv = cam.invertY ? -1 : 1;\\n    this.pitch = Math.max(-1.45, Math.min(1.45, this.pitch - my * 0.0025 * cam.sensY * inv * k));\\n    this.focus.copy(u.target);\\n    this.camera.position.copy(u.eye);\\n    this.camera.rotation.set(this.pitch, this.yaw, 0);\\n  };\\n  ctx.input.gamepadButtons = {\\n    0: 'PadCross', 1: 'PadCircle', 2: 'PadSquare', 3: 'PadTriangle',\\n    4: 'PadL1', 5: 'PadR1', 6: 'PadL2', 7: 'PadR2',\\n    8: 'PadCreate', 9: 'PadOptions', 10: 'PadL3', 11: 'PadR3',\\n    12: 'PadUp', 13: 'PadDown', 14: 'PadLeft', 15: 'PadRight',\\n  };\\n  Object.defineProperty(ctx.input, 'wantsPointerLock', {\\n    get: () => wantLock,\\n    set() {},\\n    configurable: true,\\n  });\\n}\\n\\nexport const LOOK = {\\n  scene: [\\n\",\"total_lines\":47}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"04d3eb05-a6d3-40a4-9f52-131ec03b3e5f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_541","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The camera step runs as ctx.camera.\n\nThe camera is missing from the scene. I will add a rig that copies the camera transform into poseMap. I also need to call cam.updateMatrixWorld() before reading matrixWorld, or compute the pose directly from position and quaternion.\n\nCopying the camera quaternion directly may be stale if matrixWorld hasn't updated. I'll instead extract position and quaternion from cam.matrixWorld after calling updateMatrix and updateMatrixWorld(true). This correctly handles any parent transform.\n\nThere is a one-frame delay. game.update copies the camera pose before camera.step moves it, so the map rig lags one frame behind the camera. After the first frame, the two stay synchronized.\n\nI will set frustumCulled to false and use DoubleSide on the map sheet so it is not culled. I will also update the tests. The mountMap function will no longer add the sheet directly to the camera; instead poseMap will attach it to the new rig.\n\nI am writing an `assertHeldMap` test helper. It verifies the map sheet is visible and attached to the scene under the hand rig. It also checks that the rig position matches the camera position after updating both matrix worlds.\n\nThe test helper asserts the map rig is within 0.05 units of the camera. During tests, syncViews runs before camera.step, so the rig may lag one frame behind the camera.\n\nThe 0.05 meter threshold is too strict. After the player moves, the map rig lags about 0.09 meters behind the camera, so the assertion would fail.\n\nI will hook poseMap to run after camera.step instead of before it. installLook already replaces ctx.camera.step, so I can wrap that call to pose the map immediately after the camera updates.\n\nI will add a `hookCamera` method that wraps `ctx.camera.step`. It calls the original step and then `poseMap`, ensuring the map rig copies the camera transform after the camera moves but before rendering. A `_mapHook` flag prevents duplicate wrapping.\n\nThe engine likely calls camera.step inside updateView before rendering, so the map will match the camera this frame. Calling poseMap twice is harmless. I need to verify that headless tests actually invoke camera.step through updateView.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7f8efe7f-ce38-40a5-9c62-c2eab2e89da1-124","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"python3 - << 'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\ntext = Path(\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/A Cry Too Far-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917/pixel-engine.js\").read_text()\ni = text.find(\"updateView(e){\")\nprint(text[i:i+900])\nPY","description":"See when the engine updates the camera"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4544,"output_tokens":1439,"cache_read_input_tokens":160768,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aadc-c537-7073-8756-0b078a9e97df","uuid":"f60c623a-4d20-4a4e-b243-53c9ef5df7f5"}
