,105,115,105,110,103,32,116,104,101,32,109,97,112,32,97,116,32,116,104,101,32,119,104,101,101,108,46,10,55,57,52,58,51,46,32,70,108,97,114,101,32,105,110,116,111,32,100,114,121,32,103,114,97,115,115,44,32,116,104,101,32,119,105,110,100,32,116,97,107,101,115,32,105,116,44,32,115,111,108,100,105,101,114,115,32,114,117,110,32,98,117,114,110,105,110,103,46,10,55,57,57,58,56,46,32,68,114,97,103,103,101,100,32,111,117,116,32,98,121,32,97,32,98,117,100,100,121,59,32,115,121,114,101,116,116,101,32,111,114,32,102,105,110,105,115,104,32,105,116,46,10,56,48,51,58,49,50,46,32,84,104,101,32,109,97,112,32,111,110,32,97,32,116,97,98,108,101,32,119,105,116,104,32,116,104,101,32,115,99,97,114,115,32,103,108,111,119,105,110,103,46,10,56,49,55,58,45,32,84,104,101,32,118,97,108,108,101,121,32,97,114,111,117,110,100,32,116,104,101,32,67,117,116,58,32,116,104,101,32,99,97,109,112,97,105,103,110,39,115,32,116,101,114,114,97,105,110,44,32,119,105,116,104,32,111,110,108,121,32,116,104,101,32,67,117,116,44,32,80,53,32,97,110,100,32,116,104,101,32,82,97,110,103,101,114,32,80,111,115,116,32,108,105,118,101,44,32,49,54,32,116,111,32,50,52,32,115,111,108,100,105,101,114,115,44,32,119,105,110,100,32,97,110,100,32,102,105,114,101,32,111,110,46,10,56,49,57,58,45,32,69,118,101,114,121,111,110,101,32,115,116,97,114,116,115,32,97,116,32,97,32,100,105,102,102,101,114,101,110,116,32,114,111,97,100,32,101,110,100,32,119,105,116,104,32,96,116,114,97,99,116,111,114,96,32,40,99,111,110,100,32,55,48,41,44,32,96,102,108,97,114,101,96,44,32,96,99,97,110,101,96,44,32,50,32,115,121,114,101,116,116,101,115,44,32,50,32,98,111,116,116,108,101,115,46,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":80,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/PLAN.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":18,\"content\":\"**Perspective:** first person on foot, at the wheel and in story scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles only. The source's best ideas (a gun jamming, tablets in your palm, pliers in your own forearm, a paper map in your hands while someone shoots at you) only work through your own eyes and hands.\"},{\"line_number\":21,\"content\":\"**Pitch.** You come to a dying country to kill the man who drew its maps. Your gun rusts, your truck dies, your blood has a fever with a timetable, and the grass burns whoever lit it. Two armies pay you to make things worse. Your only friends are for hire, and you may have to finish one yourself.\"},{\"line_number\":25,\"content\":\"2. **Fire is your best weapon and your worst neighbour.** Grass burns by wind on a grid. Scars stay in the world and on your map.\"},{\"line_number\":32,\"content\":\"**Original, not a recreation.** Build in the genre and use similar mechanics, but invent everything else: country, people, factions, story, map, weapons' names, models, music. Never use the source's proper nouns, characters, plot, map, level layouts, music, logos or look. The only allowed references to it are the riff in your title and the \\\"Source\\\" credit.\"},{\"line_number\":37,\"content\":\"| Hunting an arms dealer who sells to both sides; recorded interviews | Hunting the Surveyor who sells maps to both sides; his notes in your map's margins, found at trig points |\"},{\"line_number\":41,\"content\":\"| Map and GPS held in hand | Paper map and GPS held in hand, and the map turns out to be the plot (sections 14, 16) |\"},{\"line_number\":47,\"content\":\"Content limits: an invented country that maps to no real nation, people or flag. A mixed international cast. No child soldiers, no sexual violence. Civilians are not targets: they flee, and the Ledger counts them. The clinic's emblem is a green cross, because the Red Cross emblem is legally protected.\"},{\"line_number\":59,\"content\":\"**The target.** **Emil Harrow, the Surveyor:** the old company's cartographer, who drew every road and border in the Sorrel. He now sells both armies maps of each other, including minefields, and some of them are wrong.\"},{\"line_number\":61,\"content\":\"**You.** **Aster**, a silent contractor with forged papers as a surveyor's assistant. You were paid to kill Harrow. Your contract includes a map with a note in the margin, in Harrow's hand: *MIND THE FEVER. YOU ARE LATE ALREADY. E.H.*\"},{\"line_number\":73,\"content\":\"**The twist.** Harrow paid for your contract himself. He is dying of the same fever. Each burn he steered you toward (the East Siding, the Cut, the Long Field) lies on one arc across the Kettle: a firebreak. Burned grass cannot burn twice, so a refugee column can walk that scar to the mined pass at the border while both armies try to burn them out. *You have been drawing for me.* He has one more thing for you: the only honest map of the minefield.\"},{\"line_number\":81,\"content\":\"**The central line,** which is never explained: *A map is a promise made by someone who has already left.*\"},{\"line_number\":98,\"content\":\"| Default pad mapping presses keyboard codes (R2 = KeyC, Square = KeyJ...) | Replace `input.gamepadButtons` with `Pad*` codes (below). `isDown`, `wasPressed` and `consumePress` then work on them and keep the devices apart |\"},{\"line_number\":114,\"content\":\"    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;               // 0 while a menu is open or the map cursor has the stick\"},{\"line_number\":131,\"content\":\"**The map in your hands works.** A `THREE.DataTexture` (RGBA `Uint8Array`, nearest filter, `colorSpace = SRGBColorSpace`) on a `PlaneGeometry` with `toonMaterial(0xffffff, { map })`, parented to the camera, reads crisp. It needs no DOM, so it also runs in Node. Draw into the array yourself.\"},{\"line_number\":154,\"content\":\"  layers:[0,1,2,3].map(i => ({ at:[i*10,0], pixels:FRAMES[i], legend:{a:'a',b:'b',c:'c',d:'d'} })) });\"},{\"line_number\":162,\"content\":\"- Use this soldier as your starting point (as written it passes `checkModel` with no problems or warnings; shoulders about 1.12 m, head top about 1.74 m, faces +Z):\"},{\"line_number\":166,\"content\":\"export const soldier = model({ id:'soldier', about:'Soldier on the hero rig', tags:['actor'], floats:true, collider:'none',\"},{\"line_number\":186,\"content\":\"**Movement.** `CharacterController` stays grounded on the heightfield and is right for a grounded soldier. `PlatformerCharacter` (triple jumps, ground pounds) is not. `speed` is set in the constructor and read as a plain field; `setInput(move, jump, jumpHeld)` takes a move vector of length up to 1.\"},{\"line_number\":202,\"content\":\"- A pad works: `globalThis.navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`, with `pad = { connected:true, mapping:'standard', axes:[0,0,0,0], buttons:[...{pressed,value}] }`.\"},{\"line_number\":235,\"content\":\"src/world/                terrain.js roads.js sites.js grass.js sky.js fire.js wind.js map-texture.js\"},{\"line_number\":236,\"content\":\"src/actors/               player.js soldier.js squad.js buddy.js civilian.js fauna.js rig.js\"},{\"line_number\":253,\"content\":\"- **Data over code.** All content lives in `src/content/` and `src/art/` as plain data. Adding a weapon, site, mission, line of dialogue or model must not need a code change. This is what lets the rest of the game be built later \\\"with normal designer tools\\\": tables, the sheets tool and dev mode's placement readout. Keep a one-page `DESIGNERS.md` in the dev folder saying how to add each.\"},{\"line_number\":254,\"content\":\"- **Actions, not keys.** `src/core/actions.js` maps an action to its pad code and its keyboard/mouse codes (section 16). All gameplay asks for actions. In `fixedUpdate` use `input.consumeAny(codes)`; in `update` use `wasPressed`.\"},{\"line_number\":257,\"content\":\"- **`game.state()`.** One plain JSON snapshot for every test: `{ mode, screen, focus, device, glyphSet, notice, attract, mission, step, hp, stones, tablets, syrettes, weapon:{id,cond,jammed,mag}, fever, pos, yaw, vehicle, hour, wind, fire:{burning,burnt}, prompts:[...], subtitle, buddies, ledger }`. Expose it in `ready(ctx)` as `window.__GAME__ = game` when `window` exists. Give `status()` a useful one-liner and `fingerprint()` a hash of hp, stones, weapon state, fire counts and actor positions.\"},{\"line_number\":258,\"content\":\"- **Sim and view apart.** A soldier is data plus an optional view; a fire cell is data plus sprites. In a round, a client's soldiers are puppets with no brain and no collider, driven by snapshots. Write them this way from the first day, because retrofitting it is the expensive part of section 19.\"},{\"line_number\":305,\"content\":\"**Checkpoints** (posts of 4 to 6 soldiers, sandbags, a boom gate, sometimes a technical): P1 (90, -80) north road · P2 (-200, 30) west road · P3 (170, 90) east road · P4 (-320, -90) fort road · P5 (280, -210) mine road · P6 (300, 390) salt road. A cleared post re-mans after `TUNING.postRemanHours` (24 game hours); the source's instant respawn is one of its known annoyances, so we don't copy it.\"},{\"line_number\":328,\"content\":\"**Grass:** `GrassField` chunks of 48 × 48 m with about 7,000 blades, `mask` from the fuel map, `ground` from `heightAt`. Build a chunk the first time it comes within 170 m of the camera, hide it beyond 200 m, dispose beyond 320 m. On build, consult the fire grid (burnt cells get no blades).\"},{\"line_number\":330,\"content\":\"**Buildings** are `model()` defs with `collider:'parts'` so doorways stay open. Single storey with open doorways; no doors, no ladders. Watchtowers carry a soldier the player can shoot but not climb to.\"},{\"line_number\":351,\"content\":\"- `ignite(x, z, radius)` is the only way in. Used by flare, molotov, flamethrower, explosions, burning soldiers, wrecks and tracer fire on full-auto from the mounted MG (small chance).\"},{\"line_number\":357,\"content\":\"- Actors in a burning cell catch fire: soldiers panic (section 10), players burn (section 9).\"},{\"line_number\":365,\"content\":\"- Burn scars show on your held map.\"},{\"line_number\":369,\"content\":\"**Wind.** One vector, a slow random walk (direction changes by up to 15° a minute, speed 0 to 8 m/s). It drives `grass.wind`, `WindUniforms` (trees via `swayObject`) and rain. Read it from the grass, from a strip of red cloth on every post (`clothGrid` + `SoftMesh`), and from a small wind arrow on the GPS. Missions can pin it (`wind` field).\"},{\"line_number\":384,\"content\":\"**Weather.** Dry by default. A dust wind (`particles`, stronger wind, haze raised) and a storm (`Precipitation` rain, lightning `screen.flash` + thunder, fire damped). Storms are rare, scripted for M9, and available from dev mode.\"},{\"line_number\":414,\"content\":\"**In a menu or with the map cursor active,** the look stick does nothing. Otherwise the world never pauses except in the pause menu.\"},{\"line_number\":428,\"content\":\"| `flare` | Flare pistol | sidearm | 1 / 8 | 0.6/s | 12 + ignite | arcs to 70 m | 0 | given in M3 | Gravity projectile; lights fuel where it lands; sets soldiers alight |\"},{\"line_number\":429,\"content\":\"| `tractor` | \\\"Tractor\\\" rifle | main | 30 / 150 | 9/s | 24 | 110 m | 0.35 | 6 | Every soldier has one, mostly rusted |\"},{\"line_number\":438,\"content\":\"Hip fire has a small spread; L2/RMB aims (FOV 62 to 45; `empire` to 12) with tighter spread and recoil. Hitscan for bullets using `physics.castRay`, with the collider-to-actor map from section 10; rockets, flares and bottles are projectiles integrated per fixed step with a ray per step. Head ×2.5, legs ×0.7, by hit height on the capsule.\"},{\"line_number\":448,\"content\":\"**Feedback:** `engine.shake.add`, `hitstop(0.02)` on a hit, muzzle `lights.request({ lifetime:0.06 })`, tracer (`Trail` or sprite) on every third round, `particles.burst('impact')` plus red pixel blood, `Decals` for scorches, hit marker (small X) and rumble. Body-shot soldiers play `HitReact`; a killing shot switches to `Ragdoll` with the bullet's push.\"},{\"line_number\":463,\"content\":\"- **Down at 0:** if a buddy is available, a rescue scene plays (section 12). Otherwise: *YOU DIED IN THE SORREL. THE PAPERWORK WILL TAKE LONGER.* Then Continue (last save) or Title.\"},{\"line_number\":476,\"content\":\"**Bodies:** the soldier model in faction palettes (several `skin` values and hat variants), a rifle parented to `HandR`, a `CharacterController` capsule (speed from role), `physics.tag(collider, 'npc')` and a `Map` from collider handle to actor. Clips are in section 21.\"},{\"line_number\":488,\"content\":\"**Hostility.** Everyone outside Mile Forty shoots you on sight, with one exception: **while you hold a contract from a faction, you carry its pass**, and its soldiers leave you alone unless you fire near them or walk into their HQ's inner yard. Choosing whose ground to cross becomes a real decision.\"},{\"line_number\":490,\"content\":\"**Brain:** a `BehaviorTree` for each soldier, ticked at 10 Hz, in priority order:\"},{\"line_number\":512,\"content\":\"**Driving in first person:** eye at the driver's seat, your hands on a wheel that turns with steering, a dash, a small arrow on the dash pointing to your waypoint. R3/C toggles a chase camera (computed in your `step`, via `cam.override`). You can raise the map while driving, as you should: it's the most famous moment in this kind of game, and it's dangerous.\"},{\"line_number\":530,\"content\":\"| Perk | Rescues you by truck, from farther away | Gives 2 free charges per mission | +1 syrette at any safehouse; rescues you with 60 HP instead of 40 |\"},{\"line_number\":534,\"content\":\"**Alternate plans.** On certain contracts the buddy calls (radio squelch, subtitles) with a better way, adding objectives and more risk, in exchange for bonus stones and +1 trust. You can accept or ignore them on the spot. If you take it, they fight beside you on that mission.\"},{\"line_number\":536,\"content\":\"**Rescue.** At HP 0 with a buddy available: the screen drops, and you are dragged backward in first person (low camera, sky, their boots, their gunfire) while they swear at you; then you are up at 40 HP (Mags 60) with them next to you, and the buddy is spent for 1 game day. 15% chance they take a wound and are out for 2 days. They remind you of it later.\"},{\"line_number\":538,\"content\":\"**Down.** A buddy fighting with you has 200 HP. At 0 they fall, wounded, for 40 s, marked by a floating pixel cross. At their side, a two-button prompt:\"},{\"line_number\":542,\"content\":\"If you have no syrettes, only the second option is lit. If the 40 s run out, they die anyway. If they are burning, they ask you to finish it. M6 forces this moment at least once.\"},{\"line_number\":544,\"content\":\"A dead buddy stays dead: name scratched on the safehouse wall, their gun leaning under it, one Ledger line, and no more of their lines anywhere. The game must play on without any of them. Write their missions so the alternate plan just doesn't arrive.\"},{\"line_number\":546,\"content\":\"**At the end,** every surviving buddy comes to the Pass for the stones (section 14). Those with trust 3 or more first offer to split it, then draw anyway if you refuse.\"},{\"line_number\":568,\"content\":\"- **Crate:** ammo and syrettes.\"},{\"line_number\":573,\"content\":\"**Trig points** (6): stone pillars on hilltops. Use one: a new note in Harrow's hand appears in your map's margin, and you can read it on the map. All six: he leaves you a clean `empire` at a locker. A note that says so.\"},{\"line_number\":596,\"content\":\"  alt:{ buddy:'ines', call:'ines:m3.alt', bonus:5, steps:[ {do:'steal',tag:'tanker',text:'TAKE THE TANKER AT P3'} ] },\"},{\"line_number\":603,\"content\":\"**Step types:** `goto leave talk report use deliver destroy kill collect steal hold escort ride timer choice script`. A step shows its `text` for 5 s at the top when it starts, and always in the map margin. Bots drive these same steps.\"},{\"line_number\":609,\"content\":\"| M0 | The Goods Line | 2 min, dusk, skippable | You ride Old Bakr's flatcar into the Kettle as he talks. Prompt: raise the map (must do). The margin note. Fever on the platform; you fall | Look, map | - | Fade to the clinic |\"},{\"line_number\":610,\"content\":\"| M1 | Mercy Run (Odile) | 7 min, morning | Wake at the clinic. Take a tablet. Given an old `tractor` (cond 45). Drive medicine to Three Wells. On the way back P2 is manned. The 5th shot **jams by script**; then the truck is hit and stalls: repair. Syrette. Report | Walk, use, tablet, drive, waypoint, fire, jam, reload, syrette, repair | - | +3 tablets, 5 stones, Pump Shed |\"},{\"line_number\":611,\"content\":\"| M2 | A Cage at the Post (Tibor) | 7 min, best at night | Ines owes Tibor. She's in a cage at Ranger Post (8 Concord, a tower). Scout with the monocular, cross in grass, cane knife, or go loud. Open the cage (hold). Ines fights her way out with you. She explains rescues by doing one if you fall | Monocular, stealth, melee, sleep to choose the hour, safehouse capture, buddy | - | Ranger Post safehouse, 8 stones, Ines |\"},{\"line_number\":612,\"content\":\"| M3 | Fuel for the Peace (Vance) | 7 min | Burn the Harvest's 3 tanks at East Siding. Vance hands you the flare pistol. Wind pinned toward the depot: the grass will carry fire if you start it upwind | Flare, wind, fire, chain explosions | Ines: steal the tanker at P3 and drive it in; soldiers hold fire until you're inside | Scar A, 15 stones (+5). A column of smoke visible across the map for a game day |\"},{\"line_number\":615,\"content\":\"| M6 | The Long Field (Ansa) | 9 min, forced night | Burn the Concord's crop and its ammo bunker. Your highest-trust buddy comes along (with no buddy alive, a Harvest guide named Pell does). At about 70% the wind turns (scripted) and they go down on fire: **Syrette or Finish It** | Night fire, buddy choice | The mission itself | Scar B, 25 stones, the Ledger's first hard line |\"},{\"line_number\":616,\"content\":\"| M7 | The Cut (Harrow's note) | 10 min | A note at your safehouse: *You have drawn well. Come and see.* Truce collapses: both armies fight for the mine. Reach the sluice house through crossfire and wildfire, take his ledger. In it: *One contractor, paid in advance, to walk the Kettle.* Signed by him | Three-way fight, technical MG, big fire | A surviving buddy calls: they hold the east road if you go in from the pit side (no bonus stones, +1 trust) | Scar C. Truce over: Mile Forty loses its no-fire zone, Tibor moves his stock into safehouse lockers. Act 2 fever pace |\"},{\"line_number\":617,\"content\":\"| M8 | Last Train (Odile) | 7 min | Mile Forty under shells. Hold the platform 120 s while people board. Then ride Old Bakr's train to East Siding on a flatcar MG, 60 s, on rails, under fire. *He says the grass won't burn twice.* The column starts walking the scar | Defence, on-rails MG | - | Column on the map, moving slowly and visibly along the scar |\"},{\"line_number\":618,\"content\":\"| M9 | The Surveyor | 10 min, night, storm | Climb to the Observatory (deserters from both armies; no fires, it's raining). Harrow sits at a table with a lamp, as sick as you. The scene: he lays your contract map down; the three scars glow as one line; he draws the last stroke. He offers tablets and the minefield sheet, and asks you to finish your contract, or not | Choice | - | `choice` steps: shoot him or don't (a Ledger line, no other effect). Then who gets the sheet |\"},{\"line_number\":624,\"content\":\"- **Walk out:** *You reached the border with a case of stones and a map nobody else will ever use. The guard asks your trade. You tell him: surveyor.*\"},{\"line_number\":627,\"content\":\"**Attract, tests and dev mode** can jump to any mission start: each mission has a `setup` that puts the world in the right state (`world.reset`, story flags, scars, buddies, loadout), so `dev.startMission('m6')` is one call.\"},{\"line_number\":649,\"content\":\"- **A burning buddy:** *Don't let it be the fire. Please. Not the fire.*\"},{\"line_number\":652,\"content\":\"  1. *A map is a promise made by someone who has already left.*\"},{\"line_number\":676,\"content\":\"- 5 health segments, syrette and tablet counts bottom-left.\"},{\"line_number\":716,\"content\":\"**The mouse is native.** In menus the pointer is free (no lock), hover moves focus, click activates (read `input.buttonEvents`; convert with `x = (p.x+1)/2*hud.width`, `y = (1-p.y)/2*hud.height`). The wheel scrolls pages. Over the world it aims and looks under pointer lock. On the held map it's a cursor (hover focuses a marker, click sets the waypoint). On Tibor's rack and in dialogue choices, click works too. Keyboard in menus: arrows or WASD, Enter or Space, Esc back. Pad in menus: D-pad or left stick with key repeat, Cross confirms, Circle goes back, L1/R1 change tabs and pages. If the pad disconnects during play, open the pause menu.\"},{\"line_number\":718,\"content\":\"**The held map** (`src/world/map-texture.js`). The heart of pillar 3.\"},{\"line_number\":721,\"content\":\"- Redrawn at up to 2 Hz on a copy: burn scars (ink), your position and heading, the waypoint, sites you've seen, mission step and Harrow's notes in the margin, wind arrow and clock on a small GPS clipped to the corner.\"},{\"line_number\":722,\"content\":\"- Raise and lower takes 0.4 s with paper sound. **The world never stops.** You can walk and drive with it up. While it's up, the look input moves the map cursor.\"},{\"line_number\":752,\"content\":\"| Spawn | Squad of each faction, pickup, technical, truck, a stone case, a buddy |\"},{\"line_number\":754,\"content\":\"| Mission | Start any mission, complete current step, set story flags, give stones, tablets, syrettes, all guns; break gun; force jam; force fever; down a buddy |\"},{\"line_number\":778,\"content\":\"| `buddyWoundChance` | 0.15 | 0-1 |\"},{\"line_number\":792,\"content\":\"1. Dusk, driving a technical down the road, first person, raising the map at the wheel.\"},{\"line_number\":794,\"content\":\"3. Flare into dry grass, the wind takes it, soldiers run burning.\"},{\"line_number\":799,\"content\":\"8. Dragged out by a buddy; syrette or finish it.\"},{\"line_number\":803,\"content\":\"12. The map on a table with the scars glowing.\"},{\"line_number\":817,\"content\":\"- The valley around the Cut: the campaign's terrain, with only the Cut, P5 and the Ranger Post live, 16 to 24 soldiers, wind and fire on.\"},{\"line_number\":819,\"content\":\"- Everyone starts at a different road end with `tractor` (cond 70), `flare`, `cane`, 2 syrettes, 2 bottles.\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-6247b2f8-ece9-4d0c-baee-e55c7082c748-16","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Far Cry 2\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:39:17\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Far Cry 2\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:39:17\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013917__x07393c/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":200,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Far Cry 2\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013917].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.